// Rep Max Learn lessons: one per topic. Fields: s, t, kind (method, idea or writing), why, hook (idea topics only), chunks, example (task, steps, answer), selfx, faded, trap, recap. Written by Sonnet, checked by Sonnet, flagged parts settled by Opus.
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "This underpins every Paper 1 and 2 answer: you will use ceteris paribus and assumptions in diagrams, and evaluate models in short and extended questions.", "hook": {"q": "A shop cuts its prices and sales fall in the same month. What should you check first before deciding that cutting prices lowers sales?", "o": ["Whether anything else changed that could affect sales", "Nothing; the fall proves the effect", "Whether the shop is large", "Whether the prices were exactly 10% lower"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: other changes, such as a rival or a holiday, may explain the fall.", "Wrong: sales falling alongside a price cut does not show the price cut caused it.", "Wrong: size does not tell you whether another factor changed.", "Wrong: the exact size does not address other factors affecting sales."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Economics as social science", "b": "Economics studies how people, firms and governments make choices. It uses scientific methods: build a **model**, make predictions, then test them against evidence. It is a **social science** because it studies human behaviour, which is less predictable than natural matter."}, {"h": "Models and assumptions", "b": "A **model** is a simplified version of reality. It uses **assumptions**, such as rational consumers or perfect information, to make analysis manageable. Simplifying brings clarity. The cost is that unrealistic assumptions can make predictions less accurate."}, {"h": "Ceteris paribus", "b": "**Ceteris paribus** means 'all other things being equal'. You change one variable and hold others constant, so you can see its effect alone. A demand curve shifts only when a factor other than price changes. It is an assumption, not a claim about reality."}, {"h": "No controlled experiments", "b": "Natural scientists can run **controlled experiments**. Economists usually cannot, because an economy cannot be put in a laboratory and many variables change together. So economists rely on models, data and **ceteris paribus** to isolate effects."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why economists use ceteris paribus when analysing the effect of a fall in the price of cinema tickets on the quantity demanded.", "steps": [{"do": "State the problem: the quantity of cinema tickets demanded is affected by price, income, tastes and the price of substitutes.", "why": "Shows that many variables influence the outcome, so one change is hard to isolate."}, {"do": "Apply ceteris paribus: assume income, tastes and the price of streaming stay constant while ticket prices fall.", "why": "Holding other factors constant isolates the effect of price alone."}, {"do": "Link to the model: a fall in price causes an extension along the demand curve, so quantity demanded rises.", "why": "Uses correct terminology: movement along, not a shift of, the curve."}, {"do": "Explain why this is needed: economists cannot run a controlled experiment on the whole economy to hold factors constant.", "why": "Connects ceteris paribus to the limits of a social science."}, {"do": "Note the limit: in reality other factors change too, so actual quantity demanded may differ from the prediction.", "why": "Brief evaluation of the assumption."}], "answer": "Many factors affect demand for cinema tickets. Ceteris paribus holds all but price constant, so a price fall causes an extension along demand and quantity rises. It is needed as economists cannot run controlled experiments, though real outcomes may differ."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example say the effect is an extension along the demand curve rather than a shift?", "o": ["Only price changed, and other factors were held constant", "Income rose at the same time", "Demand curves never shift", "Ceteris paribus means nothing changes"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: a price change alone moves you along the curve; other factors changing would shift it.", "Wrong: if income rose, the curve would shift; the example held income constant.", "Wrong: demand curves shift when non-price factors change.", "Wrong: ceteris paribus holds other variables constant, while the studied variable still changes."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why an economist's prediction that higher interest rates reduce consumer spending might not match real data.", "given": ["Step 1: The model predicts that higher interest rates reduce spending, ceteris paribus.", "Step 2: This assumes income, confidence and prices are unchanged."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["Note that in reality other factors may change at the same time, so the prediction may not hold", "Conclude that the model is proved wrong", "Say economists can run controlled experiments to check", "State that ceteris paribus guarantees accuracy"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: real data mix many changes, so a gap does not mean the model is wrong.", "Wrong: a mismatch may be due to other factors changing, so it does not prove the model wrong.", "Wrong: controlled experiments on whole economies are generally impossible.", "Wrong: ceteris paribus is an assumption, not a guarantee of accuracy."]}, "trap": "Don't treat ceteris paribus as a description of reality. It is an assumption to isolate one effect; real data often reflect several changes at once.", "recap": "Economists use simplified models and ceteris paribus to isolate effects, because they cannot run controlled experiments on whole economies."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions on this topic appear as short multiple-choice or data-response items, and the skill underpins evaluation in every essay where policy judgements are made.", "hook": {"q": "Which claim could you settle by looking at data?", "o": ["Council tax is unfair", "Council tax should be abolished", "Council tax raised more last year than the year before", "Council tax is the worst tax"], "a": 2, "f": ["'Unfair' is an opinion about fairness, so data cannot settle it.", "'Should' is a value judgement, so data alone cannot settle it.", "Yes. Revenue figures can be checked, so the claim is testable.", "'Worst' is an opinion, so data cannot settle it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Positive statements", "b": "A **positive statement** is about what is, was or will be. It can be tested against evidence, so it can be shown **true or false**. A wrong claim is still positive if evidence could test it."}, {"h": "Normative statements", "b": "A **normative statement** contains a **value judgement** about what ought to be. Words like should, fair, too much and better are clues. Data cannot prove or disprove it, though data can inform the debate."}, {"h": "Value judgements in policy", "b": "Economists can use positive analysis to show a policy's effects. Choosing between outcomes, such as **equity** or **efficiency**, needs value judgements. Different people weigh outcomes differently, so they may disagree even with the same evidence."}, {"h": "Mixed statements", "b": "Many sentences combine both types. Split them into parts. 'Inflation rose, which is bad' has a testable claim and a **value judgement**. Do not label a sentence positive just because it has a number in it."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why 'Raising the tobacco tax will cut smoking, so the government should raise it' contains both positive and normative elements.", "steps": [{"do": "Identify the first part: 'raising the tobacco tax will cut smoking'.", "why": "It predicts a cause and effect that sales data could test, so it is positive."}, {"do": "Note that it could be false if demand is very inelastic.", "why": "Positive statements can be wrong; being testable is what matters."}, {"do": "Identify the second part: 'the government should raise it'.", "why": "'Should' expresses a value judgement about what ought to happen, so it is normative."}, {"do": "Explain that evidence alone cannot justify 'should'.", "why": "The conclusion also depends on how one values health against, say, personal choice or the burden on poorer smokers."}], "answer": "The first part is positive because sales data could test whether the tax cuts smoking. The second is normative because 'should' reflects a value judgement. Evidence informs the policy, but the choice depends on values."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the first part of the example still positive even if it might be false?", "o": ["Because it contains the word 'will'", "Because it can be tested against evidence", "Because the government believes it", "Because it is about a tax"], "a": 1, "f": ["'Will' is not the test; a prediction is positive because evidence can check it.", "Correct. Positive means testable, whether the result turns out true or false.", "Government belief is opinion; it does not decide whether a claim is testable.", "Subject matter does not decide it; testability does."]}, "faded": {"task": "Classify: 'Cutting corporation tax will raise investment, which is the most important aim for the UK.'", "given": ["Split the sentence into two parts.", "Part one: 'cutting corporation tax will raise investment'."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Classify part one as normative because it is about policy", "Classify part one as positive, since investment data could test it", "Classify the whole sentence as positive because it is about investment", "Skip part one as it cannot be classified"], "a": 1, "f": ["Policy topics are not automatically normative; the claim is about cause and effect.", "Yes. Investment data could test the claim, so it is positive. Then classify 'most important aim' as normative.", "The second part is a value judgement, so the whole sentence is not positive.", "It can be classified: it is testable, so positive."]}, "trap": "Do not call a statement positive just because it has a number or sounds factual, or normative because it might be false. Ask: can evidence test it, or does it rely on opinion?", "recap": "Positive statements can be tested against evidence, true or false; normative statements rest on value judgements about what ought to be."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Scarcity and opportunity cost underpin every Edexcel economics paper; you use them to define terms and to explain choices made by consumers, firms and governments.", "hook": {"q": "You have £10 and can buy a cinema ticket, a meal or a book. You pick the meal. What have you really given up?", "o": ["The cinema ticket and the book together", "Only the next best of the other two", "The £10 itself", "Nothing, since you got the meal"], "a": 1, "f": ["You can only do one alternative instead, so you give up the best other option, not both.", "Right: the cost of a choice is the best alternative you gave up.", "The £10 is the money spent; the economic cost is the benefit of the best alternative use.", "Choosing always means giving something up, because resources are limited."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The basic problem", "b": "**Scarcity** means finite resources but unlimited wants. It is the **economic problem**. Because of it, every economy must decide what to produce, how to produce it and for whom. It affects rich and poor countries alike."}, {"h": "Types of resource", "b": "**Renewable resources** can regenerate, such as fish stocks or forests, but only if not overused. **Non-renewable resources**, such as oil and coal, are finite and cannot be replaced on a human timescale. Both are scarce."}, {"h": "Opportunity cost", "b": "**Opportunity cost** is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made. It is one option, not the total of all alternatives. It applies to consumers, firms and governments, and need not be a money amount."}, {"h": "Choice links them", "b": "Scarcity forces **choice**, and choice creates opportunity cost. If resources were unlimited, nothing would need to be given up. This chain of reasoning is how you build an explanation in the exam."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a government that builds a new school has an opportunity cost.", "steps": [{"do": "State that resources are scarce: the government has limited funds, land and labour.", "why": "Scarcity is the starting point of the economic problem."}, {"do": "State that wants for public spending are unlimited, such as health, defence and transport.", "why": "Scarce resources against unlimited wants makes choice necessary."}, {"do": "Say that using resources on the school means they cannot be used elsewhere.", "why": "Resources have alternative uses, so choosing one excludes another."}, {"do": "Identify the next best use, for example a new hospital ward.", "why": "Opportunity cost is the next best alternative, so name it."}, {"do": "Conclude that the benefit of that ward is the opportunity cost of the school.", "why": "This links the choice to the definition."}], "answer": "Funds are scarce but wants for spending are unlimited, so the government must choose. Resources used on a school cannot be used elsewhere. The benefit of the next best alternative, such as a hospital ward, is the opportunity cost."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer name only one alternative, the hospital ward, as the opportunity cost?", "o": ["Opportunity cost is the next best alternative only", "Governments can only ever choose between two options", "Other alternatives have no value to the economy", "Opportunity cost must be measured in money terms"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: opportunity cost is the value of the single next best alternative forgone.", "Governments face many options; the definition, not the number of options, limits the cost to one.", "Other options may be valuable; they are simply not the next best.", "Opportunity cost need not be in money; it can be benefits forgone."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a firm using its factory to make chairs has an opportunity cost.", "given": ["Resources such as machines, labour and floor space are scarce.", "Using them for chairs means they cannot be used to make tables."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Say the opportunity cost is the cost of the chairs' materials", "Say the opportunity cost is the profit from the next best product, such as tables", "Say that the firm has no opportunity cost because it makes a profit", "Say the opportunity cost is all other products the firm could make"], "a": 1, "f": ["Material cost is a money cost of production; opportunity cost is the value of the alternative forgone.", "Right: identify the next best alternative and link its value to the definition.", "Profit does not remove opportunity cost; the best alternative use was still given up.", "Only the next best alternative counts, not all products combined."]}, "trap": "Do not list every forgone option or quote the money price. Name the single next best alternative given up, and say it is its value that is the cost.", "recap": "Scarcity forces choice, and the opportunity cost of any choice is the value of the next best alternative given up."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "PPF diagrams appear in short-answer and data questions, and support analysis of growth, efficiency and opportunity cost in essays throughout Theme 1 and Theme 3.", "hook": {"q": "A town has a fixed number of workers and can build either houses or roads. If it builds more houses, what happens to the number of roads it can build?", "o": ["It must build fewer roads", "It can build more roads too", "It builds the same number of roads", "It can build unlimited roads"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: resources are scarce, so using more on houses leaves fewer for roads. That is opportunity cost.", "Only possible if there were unused resources or growth, not with fixed resources fully used.", "Resources are limited, so extra houses must take resources away from roads.", "Scarcity means output cannot be unlimited."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What the PPF shows", "b": "A **production possibility frontier** shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce with its resources and technology. Points on it are **productively efficient**. Points inside show unemployed or misallocated resources. Points outside are **unattainable**."}, {"h": "Opportunity cost", "b": "**Opportunity cost** is the next best alternative given up. On a PPF it is the output of one good sacrificed to gain another. A **bowed-out** curve shows **increasing opportunity cost**; a straight line shows constant opportunity cost."}, {"h": "Growth and shifts", "b": "**Economic growth** is an outward shift of the PPF, caused by more or better resources, or improved technology. Moving from inside the curve towards it is use of **spare capacity**, not a shift. A fall in capacity shifts the curve inwards."}, {"h": "Capital versus consumer goods", "b": "Choosing more **capital goods** now means fewer **consumer goods** now. Capital raises future productive capacity, so the PPF shifts out further later. The trade-off is current consumption against **future growth**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why an economy that produces more capital goods today may be able to consume more in future.", "steps": [{"do": "Start on the PPF with capital goods on one axis and consumer goods on the other. The economy chooses a point with more capital goods.", "why": "Shows the choice is a movement along the PPF."}, {"do": "This means fewer consumer goods are produced now, because resources are scarce.", "why": "This is the opportunity cost of the choice."}, {"do": "The extra capital goods, such as machinery, raise the economy's productive capacity.", "why": "Links investment to the PPF's determinants."}, {"do": "Over time the PPF shifts outwards, from PPF1 to PPF2.", "why": "Defines economic growth in PPF terms."}, {"do": "So the economy can reach a point with more consumer goods than before, at the cost of lower consumption initially.", "why": "Completes the chain to the conclusion."}], "answer": "Producing more capital goods means fewer consumer goods now, an opportunity cost. Capital raises productive capacity, shifting the PPF outwards. Later the economy can produce more of both goods, so consumption can exceed its earlier level."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the PPF shifts outwards, rather than the economy just moving along it?", "o": ["Because capital raises productive capacity, changing what is attainable", "Because fewer consumer goods are produced", "Because the economy moves inside the curve", "Because opportunity cost is falling"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: more capital increases the maximum output possible, which is a shift of the whole curve.", "Fewer consumer goods now is a movement along the existing curve.", "Moving inside the curve would mean inefficiency, not growth.", "Opportunity cost is not the reason for a shift; changed capacity is."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a rise in unemployment moves an economy away from its PPF.", "given": ["Start with the economy producing on its PPF, using all resources efficiently."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Unemployed workers mean resources are idle, so output falls to a point inside the PPF", "The PPF shifts outwards because fewer workers are needed", "The economy moves to a point outside the PPF", "The opportunity cost of every good falls to zero"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: idle labour means output is below potential, so the economy moves inside the curve.", "Unemployment does not raise capacity; the PPF only shifts if capacity changes.", "Points outside are unattainable, and unemployment reduces output.", "Opportunity cost does not vanish; idle resources just mean output is inefficiently low."]}, "trap": "Do not call a movement towards the PPF, or along it, economic growth. Growth is an outward shift of the whole curve caused by greater capacity.", "recap": "On the PPF means efficient, inside means wasted resources, outside is unattainable, and only an outward shift is economic growth."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.5", "kind": "idea", "why": "Short explain and analyse questions often ask why specialisation raises output, what its costs are, and why money exists. It also underpins trade topics later.", "hook": {"q": "Four friends run a café. Which arrangement will probably serve most customers per hour?", "o": ["Everyone does every job themselves", "Each person focuses on one task: till, drinks, food or cleaning", "Everyone does the same job in turn each hour", "One person does everything while others rest"], "a": 1, "f": ["Switching between jobs wastes time and prevents anyone gaining skill, so output tends to be lower.", "Focusing on one task builds skill and saves switching time, so output per worker usually rises.", "Rotating each hour wastes time switching and loses the gains from repeated practice.", "This wastes the other workers' labour and so cuts total output."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Specialisation and division", "b": "**Specialisation** means focusing on producing a limited range of goods or tasks. The **division of labour** is a form of it: production is split into separate tasks, each done by a different worker."}, {"h": "Why output rises", "b": "Workers repeat one task, so they become faster and more skilled. Less time is lost switching tasks. Capital can be used more fully. Higher **labour productivity** (output per worker) lowers **unit costs** and raises total output."}, {"h": "Drawbacks", "b": "Repetitive work can cause **boredom** and lower motivation. Workers have narrow skills, so they find it hard to move jobs (**occupational immobility**). An economy or region may become **over-dependent** on one industry, and a breakdown at one stage halts production."}, {"h": "Trade and money", "b": "Specialists have a surplus and need other goods, so they **trade**. Barter needs a **double coincidence of wants**. Money solves this as a **medium of exchange**, and also acts as a **store of value**, **unit of account** and **standard of deferred payment**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how specialisation in a bakery can raise output and why this leads to the use of money.", "steps": [{"do": "Define: the bakery divides work so one worker mixes, one bakes and one serves.", "why": "Shows knowledge of division of labour, with context."}, {"do": "Each worker repeats one task, so becomes quicker and more skilled.", "why": "Links the cause to higher labour productivity."}, {"do": "Less time is lost switching between tasks, so more loaves are made per hour.", "why": "Adds a second reason and connects it to output."}, {"do": "The bakery now has a surplus of bread but needs flour, which it does not make, so it must trade.", "why": "Links specialisation to trade through surplus and interdependence."}, {"do": "Barter needs a double coincidence of wants, so money as a medium of exchange makes trade easier.", "why": "Completes the chain to the function of money."}], "answer": "Dividing tasks raises productivity through skill and saved time, so output rises. The surplus must be traded for goods the bakery does not make. Barter needs a double coincidence of wants, so money as a medium of exchange is used."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer link repeated tasks to higher output via labour productivity?", "o": ["Output per worker rises when workers become faster and more skilled", "Wages always rise when tasks are repeated", "Unit costs rise, which increases output", "Workers become less dependent on one another"], "a": 0, "f": ["Repetition builds speed and skill, so each worker produces more, which raises output.", "Wages are not part of this chain and need not rise.", "Unit costs usually fall with higher productivity, not rise.", "Division of labour increases interdependence, not reduces it."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain one drawback of the division of labour for a firm making smartphones.", "given": ["Drawback: production is split into narrow tasks, each worker doing only one stage.", "Workers repeat the same task all day."], "q": "What is the best next link in the chain?", "o": ["Repetition causes boredom, lowering motivation and possibly productivity and quality", "Repetition increases skill, so productivity always rises", "Therefore money loses its role as a store of value", "Therefore the firm can barter its phones"], "a": 0, "f": ["Boredom and falling motivation can reduce output and quality, completing the drawback.", "This is a benefit, not a drawback, so it does not answer the question.", "Money's functions are unrelated to repetitive work.", "Bartering is unrelated to worker boredom."]}, "trap": "Do not list only benefits. Name a drawback, link it to the firm or economy in the question, and keep money's four functions separate from specialisation.", "recap": "Specialisation raises productivity and creates surpluses to trade, but brings boredom and dependence; money overcomes the double coincidence of wants in barter."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.6", "kind": "idea", "why": "Economic systems and the views of Smith, Hayek and Marx appear in short-answer definitions and in evaluating the role of the state in essays.", "hook": {"q": "Nobody in the UK plans how many loaves of bread are baked each day. What probably decides it?", "o": ["A government committee", "Consumer demand and firms seeking profit", "A national rationing scheme", "Bakers taking turns"], "a": 1, "f": ["No committee sets bread output; it is left to firms.", "Right: firms respond to prices and demand. This is the price mechanism at work.", "Rationing is not used for bread in normal times.", "Bakers respond to demand, not a rota."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three systems", "b": "A **free market economy** allocates resources through the **price mechanism** with private ownership. A **command economy** uses state planning and ownership. A **mixed economy** combines market allocation with state provision and regulation. Real economies are mixed."}, {"h": "Smith and Hayek", "b": "Smith's **invisible hand**: self-interested individuals in markets promote the wider good without being directed. Hayek: **dispersed knowledge** is scattered among millions, and prices bring it together, which planners cannot do."}, {"h": "Marx's view", "b": "Marx argued capitalism lets owners of capital exploit workers by keeping **surplus value**. He favoured **collective ownership** of the means of production, with the state planning, in place of private ownership."}, {"h": "Role of the state", "b": "In mixed economies the state may provide **public goods**, correct **market failure**, redistribute income and regulate. Evaluation: more intervention can correct failures but risks **government failure** and weaker incentives."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a free market economy may allocate resources more efficiently than a command economy.", "steps": [{"do": "State that in a free market prices signal scarcity and consumer wants.", "why": "Defines the price mechanism, the core of the argument."}, {"do": "Link to firms: rising prices show high demand, so profit-seeking firms produce more of that good.", "why": "Shows how signals change resource allocation."}, {"do": "Add Hayek: the knowledge is dispersed among millions, and prices communicate it.", "why": "Uses an economist's argument to support the point."}, {"do": "Contrast: planners lack this information, so risk shortages or surpluses.", "why": "Makes the comparison the question asks for."}, {"do": "Conclude that profit incentives also push firms to cut costs.", "why": "Adds a second reason for efficiency."}], "answer": "Prices signal scarcity and wants, so profit-seeking firms move resources to where demand is high. Hayek argued the needed knowledge is dispersed and prices communicate it. Planners lack this information and risk shortages or surpluses."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer bring in Hayek's idea of dispersed knowledge?", "o": ["It shows planners earn less profit", "It explains why prices can coordinate information that planners cannot gather", "It proves free markets have no inequality", "It shows prices never change"], "a": 1, "f": ["Hayek's point is about information, not planners' profit.", "Right: it gives the reason prices work better than planning for allocation.", "Hayek did not claim there is no inequality.", "Prices change as information changes; that is the point."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain one disadvantage of a free market economy.", "given": ["Free markets rely on the price mechanism and private ownership.", "Some goods, such as street lighting, are public goods."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["State that firms cannot profitably provide them because non-payers cannot be excluded, so they may be under-provided", "State that prices are fixed by government for these goods", "State that public goods are always produced in excess", "State that free markets cannot provide any private goods"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: non-excludability creates free riders, so the market under-provides, a market failure.", "Fixed prices describe state control, not the free market.", "Public goods tend to be under-provided, not over-provided.", "Free markets do provide private goods; the weakness is elsewhere."]}, "trap": "Do not call any real economy a pure free market or command economy. Write 'mixed' and discuss the degree of state involvement.", "recap": "Free markets use prices, command economies use planning, mixed economies use both; Smith, Hayek and Marx give the arguments."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Rational decision making underpins demand and supply analysis and often appears as a short explain question or as an evaluation point about behavioural economics in longer answers.", "hook": {"q": "You are choosing between two cinema snacks. Which choice would economists call 'rational'?", "o": ["The one giving you the most satisfaction for its cost", "The cheapest one, whatever you enjoy", "The most expensive one, as it must be best", "The one everyone else is buying"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: rational means weighing benefits against costs to get the greatest net satisfaction.", "Wrong: cheapest ignores benefit; rationality compares both sides.", "Wrong: price does not guarantee satisfaction; cost is weighed against benefit.", "Wrong: following others is a behavioural influence, not utility maximisation."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Consumers: maximise utility", "b": "**Utility** is the satisfaction from consuming a good or service. Rational consumers choose the combination of goods that gives the greatest total utility from their limited income. They compare the **marginal utility** of each extra unit with its price."}, {"h": "Firms: maximise profit", "b": "Rational firms aim to maximise **profit**, which is total revenue minus total cost. Profit is greatest where **marginal revenue equals marginal cost** (MR = MC). Producing a unit with MC above MR lowers profit; producing one with MR above MC raises it."}, {"h": "Governments and workers", "b": "Governments are assumed to maximise **social welfare**, weighing the costs and benefits to society. Workers are assumed to maximise **welfare** by balancing wages against leisure and working conditions. In each case the decision-maker weighs costs against benefits."}, {"h": "Limits of the assumption", "b": "Real behaviour may differ. Consumers may follow **habit**, be influenced by others or lack **information**. Firms may pursue sales or growth instead of profit. This is why **behavioural economics** challenges the rationality assumption."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a firm assumed to be a rational profit maximiser stops raising output at the point where MR = MC.", "steps": [{"do": "Define the firm's aim: a rational firm seeks to maximise profit, the gap between total revenue and total cost.", "why": "Starting with the objective frames the whole argument."}, {"do": "State that each extra unit adds marginal revenue (MR) to revenue and marginal cost (MC) to cost.", "why": "Profit changes at the margin, so MR and MC are the relevant terms."}, {"do": "If MR exceeds MC, the extra unit adds more to revenue than to cost, so profit rises and the firm expands output.", "why": "This shows why it should not stop before MR = MC."}, {"do": "If MC exceeds MR, the extra unit lowers profit, so the firm would cut output.", "why": "This shows why it should not go beyond MR = MC."}, {"do": "So profit is maximised where MR = MC, as no change in output can then add to profit.", "why": "The conclusion links the two earlier points directly to the question."}], "answer": "Profit is maximised at MR = MC because below that output extra units add more to revenue than cost, and above it they add more cost than revenue."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example consider what happens when MC exceeds MR?", "o": ["It shows that producing beyond MR = MC would reduce profit", "It shows that firms always want lower costs", "It proves that revenue falls as output rises", "It shows that consumers maximise utility"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: if MC is above MR, the extra unit reduces profit, so output beyond that point is not rational.", "Wrong: the aim is maximum profit, not lowest cost; the comparison is of MC with MR.", "Wrong: MR may still be positive; the point is that cost rises by more.", "Wrong: this step concerns firms, not consumers."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a rational consumer might stop buying more of a good even though it still gives some satisfaction.", "given": ["Rational consumers aim to maximise utility from limited income.", "Each extra unit gives marginal utility, which tends to fall as more is consumed."], "q": "What is the next point?", "o": ["Buy until the marginal utility of an extra unit no longer justifies its price", "Buy until marginal utility is as high as possible", "Buy until income is fully spent on this good", "Buy until the price falls to zero"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the consumer compares marginal utility with price and stops when extra satisfaction is worth less than the cost.", "Wrong: marginal utility falls as consumption rises, so it is not maximised by buying more.", "Wrong: spending all income on one good ignores the utility from other goods.", "Wrong: price is given to the consumer; they respond to it rather than choosing it."]}, "trap": "Do not say rational means selfish or always right. It means weighing costs and benefits to meet one's aims given the information held, which may be imperfect.", "recap": "Rational agents weigh costs against benefits: consumers maximise utility, firms maximise profit at MR = MC, though real behaviour may differ."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Distinguishing a movement along from a shift of the demand curve is tested in multiple-choice, data response and diagram-based explanations throughout Papers 1 and 3.", "hook": {"q": "A bakery cuts the price of its sourdough loaf. More loaves are sold. Has the demand curve for sourdough moved?", "o": ["Yes, it has shifted right", "Yes, it has shifted left", "No, we have moved along the same curve", "No, because quantity demanded did not change"], "a": 2, "f": ["Cheaper sourdough raises quantity demanded, but price is on the axis, so the curve stays put.", "Wrong direction, and a price change does not shift the curve anyway.", "Price is on the vertical axis, so its change is shown as a movement along the curve.", "Quantity demanded did rise; that is the movement along the curve."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Movement or shift?", "b": "A change in the good's own **price** causes a **movement along** the demand curve: an **extension** if price falls, a **contraction** if price rises. Any other influence changes demand at every price and causes a **shift** of the curve."}, {"h": "Conditions of demand", "b": "The **conditions of demand** are: real income, tastes and preferences (including advertising), prices of **substitutes** and **complements**, population size and structure, and expectations. A rise in demand shifts the curve right; a fall shifts it left."}, {"h": "Related goods and income", "b": "If a substitute's price rises, demand for this good rises. If a complement's price rises, demand for this good falls. Demand for a **normal good** rises with income; for an **inferior good** it falls as income rises."}, {"h": "Diminishing marginal utility", "b": "**Utility** is satisfaction. **Marginal utility** is the extra satisfaction from one more unit. It falls as consumption rises: **diminishing marginal utility**. So consumers will only buy more if price falls, which explains the downward-sloping demand curve."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a fall in the price of games consoles may shift the demand curve for video games to the right.", "steps": [{"do": "Identify the relationship: consoles and video games are complements, used together.", "why": "Naming the type of related good is the knowledge mark."}, {"do": "State that cheaper consoles extend the quantity of consoles demanded, so more consoles are owned.", "why": "Shows the movement along the console demand curve first."}, {"do": "Link: more console owners want more games at any given game price.", "why": "This is the chain of reasoning that earns analysis marks."}, {"do": "Conclude that demand for games rises at every price, so the curve shifts right.", "why": "A shift, not a movement, because the game's own price has not changed."}], "answer": "Consoles and games are complements. Cheaper consoles mean more are bought, so more people want games at every price. A change in a condition of demand, not the game's own price, so the curve shifts right."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the shift in the games curve not described as an extension of demand?", "o": ["The game's own price has not changed, so it is not a movement along", "Extensions only happen to normal goods", "Games are inferior goods, so no extension occurs", "Extensions apply only to complements"], "a": 0, "f": ["Extensions come from a change in the good's own price; here the cause is a related good's price.", "Extensions apply to any good with a falling own price, not just normal goods.", "Nothing suggests games are inferior goods, and the type of good is irrelevant here.", "Extensions are not limited to complements; they depend on own price."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a rise in the price of butter may shift the demand curve for margarine to the right.", "given": ["Butter and margarine are substitutes."], "q": "What is the next step in the chain?", "o": ["Dearer butter makes margarine relatively cheaper, so consumers switch towards margarine", "Dearer butter lowers margarine's price, extending demand along the curve", "Dearer butter reduces incomes, so demand for margarine as a normal good falls", "Dearer butter makes consumers buy more of both goods at every price"], "a": 0, "f": ["Switching to the relatively cheaper substitute leads to more margarine demanded at every price.", "Margarine's own price has not changed, so no extension occurs.", "The link is substitution, not income, and a fall would shift the curve left.", "Substitutes move in opposite directions, so buying more of both is wrong."]}, "trap": "Saying a price change shifts the curve. Own price moves you along it; only conditions of demand shift it. Check what has changed before choosing.", "recap": "Own price changes move you along the demand curve; changes in conditions of demand shift the whole curve."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.3", "kind": "method", "why": "Elasticity calculations and their interpretation appear in Paper 1 and 3 data-response questions, and the results support evaluation of pricing and tax decisions.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "The three formulas", "b": "**PED** = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔP. **YED** = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔY (income). **XED** = %ΔQd of good A ÷ %ΔP of good B. Always convert changes into percentages first: change ÷ original × 100. Keep the sign."}, {"h": "Reading the value", "b": "PED: beyond −1 (magnitude above 1) is **elastic**; between 0 and −1 is **inelastic**. YED: positive means a **normal good**, negative means an **inferior good**; above +1 is income elastic. XED: positive means **substitutes**, negative means **complements**; larger magnitude means a closer link."}, {"h": "PED and revenue", "b": "Revenue = price × quantity. If demand is **elastic**, a price rise lowers revenue and a price cut raises it. If demand is **inelastic**, a price rise raises revenue and a price cut lowers it. At unit elasticity revenue does not change."}, {"h": "Determinants and taxes", "b": "PED depends on the number and closeness of **substitutes**, whether the good is a necessity, its share of income, and the time period (demand is more elastic over longer periods). Governments tax goods with **inelastic demand** to raise revenue, since quantity falls little."}], "example": {"task": "Price falls from £5.00 to £4.50 and quantity demanded rises from 200 to 230. Calculate PED, say what it shows, and state the effect on revenue.", "steps": [{"do": "%ΔP = (4.50 − 5.00) ÷ 5.00 × 100 = −10%.", "why": "Use the percentage change from the original price; a fall gives a negative figure."}, {"do": "%ΔQd = (230 − 200) ÷ 200 × 100 = +15%.", "why": "Same method for quantity, using the original quantity as the base."}, {"do": "PED = +15 ÷ −10 = −1.5.", "why": "Divide %ΔQd by %ΔP and keep the sign; it is negative as expected."}, {"do": "Demand is price elastic, as the magnitude 1.5 is greater than 1.", "why": "Quantity changes proportionately more than price."}, {"do": "Revenue before = £5 × 200 = £1,000. After = £4.50 × 230 = £1,035. Revenue rises by £35.", "why": "This confirms the rule that a price cut raises revenue when demand is elastic."}], "answer": "PED = −1.5, so demand is elastic. The price cut raises revenue from £1,000 to £1,035."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does revenue rise after the price cut in the worked example?", "o": ["Price fell, and a lower price always raises revenue", "Quantity rose by 15%, more than the 10% fall in price", "Quantity demanded rose by 30 units, which is more than the price fell", "The PED is negative, so revenue must rise"], "a": 1, "f": ["A lower price does not always raise revenue; it depends on the PED.", "Right: the percentage rise in quantity exceeds the percentage fall in price, which is what elastic demand means, so P × Q rises.", "Comparing units with pounds is meaningless; elasticity compares percentage changes.", "PED is usually negative, so the sign cannot decide the effect. The size relative to 1 does."]}, "faded": {"task": "Income rises from £1,500 to £1,800. Monthly quantity of a good falls from 50 to 45 units. Calculate YED and say what type of good it is.", "given": ["%ΔY = 300 ÷ 1,500 × 100 = +20%", "%ΔQd = −5 ÷ 50 × 100 = −10%"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["YED = −10 ÷ 20 = +0.5, a normal good", "YED = 20 ÷ −10 = −2.0, an inferior good", "YED = −10 ÷ 20 = −0.5, an inferior good", "YED = −10 ÷ 20 = −0.5, a normal good"], "a": 2, "f": ["Dropped the sign: quantity fell as income rose, so YED is negative.", "You inverted the formula; %ΔQd goes on top, giving −0.5, not −2.0.", "Right: YED = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔY = −0.5. A negative YED means demand falls as income rises, so it is an inferior good.", "The value is right, but a negative YED means an inferior good, not a normal one."]}, "trap": "Using absolute changes instead of percentages, or ignoring the sign. Always work out % change from the original value, and read the sign to name the type of good.", "recap": "Elasticity is %ΔQd divided by the % change in price, income or another good's price; the sign and size tell you the interpretation."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Supply questions often test whether you can tell a movement from a shift. Diagram and explanation marks depend on it.", "hook": {"q": "A baker's costs of flour rise. Which is more likely to happen to the number of loaves she is willing to supply at £1 each?", "o": ["It rises", "It falls", "It stays the same", "It cannot be known"], "a": 1, "f": ["Higher costs cut profit per loaf, so she would not supply more at the same price.", "Right: higher costs reduce profit at each price, so she supplies fewer loaves at £1.", "Costs affect profit margins, so supply at each price would change.", "The direction is clear: higher costs reduce willingness to supply."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Movement along the curve", "b": "A change in the good's **own price** causes a movement along the **supply curve**. A higher price gives an **extension** of supply. A lower price gives a **contraction**. The curve itself does not move."}, {"h": "Shifts of the curve", "b": "A change in a **condition of supply** shifts the curve. A rightward shift is an **increase in supply**: more is supplied at every price. A leftward shift is a **decrease in supply**: less is supplied at every price."}, {"h": "The conditions of supply", "b": "These include **costs of production**, **technology**, **indirect taxes**, **subsidies**, the number of firms, producers' objectives, and weather or other shocks. Costs up, taxes up, or fewer firms shift supply left. Subsidies and better technology shift it right."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a rise in the cost of raw materials affects the supply of chocolate bars.", "steps": [{"do": "Identify the factor: raw material costs are a condition of supply.", "why": "It is not the bar's own price, so it shifts the curve."}, {"do": "State that higher costs of production reduce profit per bar at each price.", "why": "This links the cause to firms' behaviour."}, {"do": "Firms are willing to supply fewer bars at every price.", "why": "This is the meaning of a decrease in supply."}, {"do": "The supply curve shifts left, from S1 to S2.", "why": "Shows the diagram effect correctly as a shift."}, {"do": "Conclude: the equilibrium price rises and quantity falls, assuming demand is unchanged.", "why": "Links the shift to the market outcome."}], "answer": "Higher raw material costs reduce profit per bar at each price, so supply decreases and the curve shifts left. Equilibrium price rises and quantity falls."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example show a shift left rather than a contraction along the curve?", "o": ["Because the cause is a change in cost, not the bar's own price", "Because firms always supply less when prices rise", "Because contractions only happen with taxes", "Because a leftward shift needs a fall in demand"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: cost is a condition of supply, so it moves the whole curve.", "Firms supply more when own price rises; that would be an extension.", "Contractions come from a fall in own price, not taxes.", "Demand does not cause supply to shift."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a new subsidy for wind farms affects the supply of electricity from wind.", "given": ["Identify the factor: a subsidy is a condition of supply.", "It lowers the costs of production per unit."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Wind farms supply more at every price, so the supply curve shifts right", "Wind farms supply more only at higher prices, so supply extends along the curve", "Demand for wind power falls, shifting the demand curve left", "Wind farms supply less because profits fall"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: lower costs mean more is supplied at every price, shifting supply right.", "An extension needs a rise in own price; the subsidy is a different cause.", "A subsidy affects firms' costs, not demand.", "A subsidy raises profit per unit, so supply rises, not falls."]}, "trap": "Treating a cost change as a movement along the curve. Ask: is the cause the good's own price? If not, the curve shifts.", "recap": "Own price moves you along the supply curve; every other condition of supply shifts the whole curve."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.5", "kind": "method", "why": "PES appears in calculation questions and in 'explain' and 'evaluate' answers on price changes, often alongside PED, with marks for the time-period point.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "The PES formula", "b": "**Price elasticity of supply** measures the responsiveness of **quantity supplied** to a change in price. $PES=\\frac{\\%\\Delta Q_s}{\\%\\Delta P}$. It is positive for normal supply curves. Use percentage changes, not absolute ones."}, {"h": "Interpreting values", "b": "If PES > 1, supply is **elastic**. If PES < 1, it is **inelastic**. PES = 1 is **unit elastic**, 0 is perfectly inelastic (vertical), and infinite is perfectly elastic (horizontal). A straight line from the origin always has PES = 1."}, {"h": "Determinants of PES", "b": "PES is higher with **spare capacity**, **stocks**, mobile factors of production, easy entry for new firms and a short production time. It is lower when capacity is full, inputs are scarce or specialised, or stocks are perishable."}, {"h": "Short run and long run", "b": "In the **short run** at least one factor is fixed, so supply is less elastic. In the **long run** all factors can change and new firms can enter, so supply is more elastic. Exam answers should link time to this."}], "example": {"task": "A farm's price per tonne of wheat rises from £150 to £180. Quantity supplied rises from 400 to 460 tonnes. Calculate PES and interpret it.", "steps": [{"do": "Find %ΔP: (180 − 150) ÷ 150 × 100 = 20%.", "why": "Change divided by the original price, as a percentage."}, {"do": "Find %ΔQs: (460 − 400) ÷ 400 × 100 = 15%.", "why": "Same method for quantity, using the original quantity."}, {"do": "PES = 15 ÷ 20 = 0.75.", "why": "Apply %ΔQs ÷ %ΔP with quantity on top."}, {"do": "Interpret: 0.75 is below 1, so supply is inelastic.", "why": "Quantity supplied rises proportionately less than price."}], "answer": "PES = 0.75. Supply is price inelastic: a 20% price rise leads to only a 15% rise in quantity supplied, perhaps because farmland and growing time are fixed."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked example divide by the original values (150 and 400)?", "o": ["Percentage change is measured relative to the starting value", "The new values are always less reliable", "PES must be less than 1", "It removes the need to use percentages"], "a": 0, "f": ["Percentage change is the change as a share of the starting point.", "Reliability is not the reason; the method is a convention for percentage change.", "PES can take any positive value; the result depends on the data.", "The method still uses percentages; it just fixes the base."]}, "faded": {"task": "A toy shop's supplier raises its price from £8 to £10 per toy. Quantity supplied rises from 200 to 250 toys.", "given": ["%ΔP = (10 − 8) ÷ 8 × 100 = 25%"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["%ΔQs = 50 ÷ 250 × 100 = 20%", "%ΔQs = 50 ÷ 200 × 100 = 25%, so PES = 25 ÷ 25 = 1", "%ΔQs = 50 ÷ 200 × 100 = 25%, so PES = 25 × 25", "%ΔQs = 250 ÷ 200 = 125%, so PES = 5"], "a": 1, "f": ["This divides by the new quantity; use the original, 200.", "Correct: 50 ÷ 200 = 25%, then 25 ÷ 25 = 1, so supply is unit elastic.", "Multiplying the percentages is wrong; PES is a ratio, so divide.", "This treats the new quantity as a percentage of the old, not the change."]}, "trap": "Do not use absolute changes or divide the wrong way round. Put quantity supplied on top, use the original values as the base, and remember PES is positive.", "recap": "PES = %ΔQs ÷ %ΔP; supply is more elastic with spare capacity, stocks and more time, so long-run supply is more elastic."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.6", "kind": "idea", "why": "Price determination underpins almost every diagram question in Papers 1 and 3, and your analysis chains rely on shifting curves correctly.", "hook": {"q": "A shop has too many unsold items at its current price. What would you expect it to do?", "o": ["Raise the price", "Lower the price", "Keep the price the same", "Stop selling the item entirely"], "a": 1, "f": ["Raising price would reduce sales further and worsen the surplus.", "Right: surplus stock pushes sellers to cut price until sales match stock. This is how markets reach equilibrium.", "Leaving price unchanged leaves the surplus in place.", "Stopping sales is extreme; price cuts usually clear the surplus first."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Equilibrium price", "b": "**Equilibrium** is where **quantity demanded equals quantity supplied**. Demand and supply curves cross here. The price is the **market clearing price**: there is no tendency to change, as no buyer or seller is frustrated."}, {"h": "Disequilibrium", "b": "Above equilibrium, **excess supply** (a surplus) exists, so price falls. Below it, **excess demand** (a shortage) exists, so price rises. These price changes are **movements along** the curves, which restore equilibrium. This is the **price mechanism** at work."}, {"h": "Shifts of curves", "b": "A change in a **non-price factor** (income, tastes, costs, taxes) **shifts** a curve. This creates disequilibrium at the old price. Price then adjusts, causing an **extension or contraction** along the other curve, to a new equilibrium."}, {"h": "Direction rules", "b": "Demand right: price up, quantity up. Demand left: both down. Supply right: price down, quantity up. Supply left: price up, quantity down. When both shift, one of price or quantity is certain; the other depends on **relative size**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a rise in the cost of flour affects the equilibrium price and quantity of bread in a UK market.", "steps": [{"do": "Flour is a cost of production, so higher flour costs reduce profitability at every price.", "why": "Identifies a non-price supply factor, not a price change."}, {"do": "Supply shifts left, from S1 to S2.", "why": "Firms supply less at each price, so the curve moves leftwards."}, {"do": "At the old price there is excess demand, as Qd exceeds the new Qs.", "why": "Describes disequilibrium created by the shift."}, {"do": "The shortage bids price up.", "why": "The price mechanism rations the scarce bread."}, {"do": "As price rises, quantity demanded contracts along D and quantity supplied extends along S2, reaching a new equilibrium with higher price and lower quantity.", "why": "Shows the movements along curves and the final result."}], "answer": "Higher flour costs shift supply left, creating excess demand at the old price. Price rises, causing a contraction in demand and extension in supply along S2. The new equilibrium has a higher price and lower quantity."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer describe a contraction of demand rather than a shift of demand?", "o": ["Demand curves never shift", "The fall in quantity demanded is caused by the price rise, a change in price", "Flour is not related to bread demand", "Contractions happen only when supply shifts right"], "a": 1, "f": ["Demand curves do shift when non-price factors change, such as income.", "Right: a change in the good's own price causes a movement along the curve, not a shift.", "Flour affects supply costs; the point is about what moves demand, not relatedness.", "Contraction follows a price rise, which here comes from a left shift of supply."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a fall in consumers' incomes affects the market for a normal good such as new cars.", "given": ["Income is a non-price factor affecting demand.", "Demand shifts left, from D1 to D2."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Excess supply exists at the old price, so price falls", "Excess demand exists at the old price, so price rises", "Supply shifts right to meet the lower demand", "Price stays the same but quantity falls"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: with lower demand, Qs exceeds Qd at the old price, so price falls.", "Excess demand would follow a rightward demand shift, not a leftward one.", "Supply does not shift; income is a demand factor. Supply contracts along its curve.", "Price adjusts through the price mechanism; it does not stay fixed."]}, "trap": "Do not shift a curve because price changed. Only non-price factors shift curves; the price change moves you along the other curve to the new equilibrium.", "recap": "Non-price changes shift a curve, creating excess demand or supply; price then adjusts along the other curve to a new equilibrium."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.7", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 1 and 3 questions ask you to explain how prices allocate resources, usually by applying rationing, incentive and signalling to a market change.", "hook": {"q": "Concert tickets for a popular band sell out fast and resale prices soar. What is the price doing?", "o": ["Showing demand is high and deterring some buyers", "Making demand rise further", "Reducing the band's incentive to play more shows", "Having no effect on who gets tickets"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the high price signals strong demand and deters buyers who are not willing or able to pay.", "A higher price does not raise demand; it reduces quantity demanded along the curve.", "High prices raise the incentive to supply more shows, not reduce it.", "Price does decide who gets tickets, as only those willing and able to pay will buy."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Rationing", "b": "Resources are **scarce**, so not everyone can have what they want. A rising price reduces **quantity demanded**. Only consumers **willing and able to pay** get the good. Rationing works when demand exceeds supply at the current price."}, {"h": "Incentives", "b": "Higher prices raise **profit**, giving firms an **incentive** to supply more. Lower prices cut profit and give an incentive to produce less. Consumers also respond: a price rise gives them an incentive to buy substitutes."}, {"h": "Signalling", "b": "Price changes act as **signals** about where resources are wanted. A rise after a demand shift signals producers to **reallocate resources** into that market. A fall signals resources should leave. This happens without central planning."}, {"h": "Linking to shifts", "b": "Start with a shift in **demand** or **supply**. This creates **excess demand or supply** at the old price. Price then adjusts to a new equilibrium, and the three functions explain how the market responds."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how the price mechanism allocates resources when demand for plant-based burgers increases.", "steps": [{"do": "Demand shifts right, creating excess demand at the original price.", "why": "Starts with the cause, a shift in demand, as the analysis must begin with a change."}, {"do": "Price rises, so some consumers are priced out. Quantity demanded contracts along the new demand curve.", "why": "This is rationing: price allocates scarce burgers to those willing and able to pay."}, {"do": "Higher price and profit give producers an incentive to supply more.", "why": "Incentive function: profit motive drives increased supply."}, {"do": "The higher price signals to firms that resources should move into plant-based burgers from less profitable uses.", "why": "Signalling function: resources are reallocated without central direction."}, {"do": "Supply extends until a new equilibrium is reached where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.", "why": "Shows the market clearing, completing the chain."}], "answer": "Demand rises, causing excess demand. Price rises, rationing burgers to those willing and able to pay. Higher profit gives firms an incentive to supply more and signals resources should move here, until a new equilibrium."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say quantity demanded contracts along the demand curve, rather than demand falling?", "o": ["Because the cause of the change is price, which moves us along the curve", "Because the demand curve shifts left when price rises", "Because consumers' incomes have fallen", "Because supply has also shifted left"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: a change in the good's own price causes a movement along the demand curve, not a shift.", "A price rise does not shift demand; shifts come from non-price factors.", "Nothing says income changed; the cause is the price rise.", "Supply does not need to shift; the price rise comes from the demand shift."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the price mechanism responds when a poor harvest reduces the supply of wheat.", "given": ["Supply shifts left, causing excess demand at the original price.", "Price rises."], "q": "What is the next step in the chain of explanation?", "o": ["Rationing: quantity demanded contracts as higher price deters some buyers", "Demand shifts right because wheat is now scarcer", "Producers face weaker incentives so all leave the market", "The price falls to restore equilibrium"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the higher price rations the limited wheat by reducing quantity demanded along the demand curve.", "Scarcity does not shift demand; price changes cause movements along the curve.", "Higher price raises profit incentives for remaining and new suppliers, not weakens them.", "Price needs to rise, not fall, to remove excess demand after a supply fall."]}, "trap": "Do not just say price rises or falls. Name the function, rationing, incentive or signalling, and link it to a shift and a movement along a curve.", "recap": "Prices ration scarce goods, create incentives through profit, and signal where resources should move after a shift in demand or supply."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.8", "kind": "method", "why": "Short calculation and diagram questions ask you to find or compare surplus areas, and longer answers use surplus to judge who gains or loses when a market changes.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What the surpluses are", "b": "**Consumer surplus** is the difference between what consumers are willing to pay and the price they actually pay. **Producer surplus** is the difference between the price producers receive and the minimum price they would accept. Both are measured in £."}, {"h": "Finding the areas", "b": "**Consumer surplus** is the area under the demand curve and above the price, up to the quantity traded. **Producer surplus** is the area above the supply curve and below the price. With straight lines each is a triangle: ½ × quantity × price gap."}, {"h": "Shifts change the areas", "b": "A shift in demand or supply moves **equilibrium** price and quantity, so both areas change. Cheaper price with more sold usually enlarges **consumer surplus**. A higher price and more sold enlarges **producer surplus**. Recalculate both triangles to compare."}], "example": {"task": "Demand is P = 24 − 2Q and supply is P = 4 + 2Q (P in £, Q in units). Calculate consumer surplus and producer surplus at equilibrium.", "steps": [{"do": "Set demand equal to supply: 24 − 2Q = 4 + 2Q, so 20 = 4Q and Q = 5. Then P = 24 − 2(5) = £14.", "why": "Surpluses are measured at the equilibrium price and quantity."}, {"do": "Read the intercepts: demand meets the price axis at £24 and supply at £4.", "why": "These are the tops and bottoms of the triangles."}, {"do": "Consumer surplus = ½ × 5 × (24 − 14) = ½ × 5 × 10 = £25.", "why": "Height is the gap from the demand intercept to the price, not the price."}, {"do": "Producer surplus = ½ × 5 × (14 − 4) = ½ × 5 × 10 = £25.", "why": "Height is the gap from the price down to the supply intercept."}], "answer": "Equilibrium is Q = 5 and P = £14. Consumer surplus is £25 and producer surplus is £25, so total surplus is £50."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the height of the consumer surplus triangle 24 − 14 and not 24?", "o": ["Consumers only gain the amount above the price they actually pay", "The price axis starts at £14 on this diagram", "Demand and supply must both be subtracted to find any triangle", "Consumer surplus is measured from the supply intercept upwards"], "a": 0, "x": "", "f": ["Right: the surplus is willingness to pay minus price paid, so the triangle sits between the demand curve and the price line.", "The axis starts at zero; the £14 is the equilibrium price, which forms the triangle's base line.", "Only the price is subtracted for consumer surplus; supply's intercept is used for producer surplus.", "The supply intercept (£4) is the lower edge of producer surplus, not of consumer surplus."]}, "faded": {"task": "Demand is P = 40 − 4Q and supply is P = 10 + 2Q (£, units). Calculate consumer surplus at equilibrium.", "given": ["Set equal: 40 − 4Q = 10 + 2Q, so 30 = 6Q and Q = 5.", "Price: P = 40 − 4(5) = £20.", "Demand intercept is £40; supply intercept is £10."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["CS = ½ × 5 × 40 = £100", "CS = 5 × (40 − 20) = £100", "CS = ½ × 5 × (20 − 10) = £25", "CS = ½ × 5 × (40 − 20) = £50"], "a": 3, "f": ["The height must be the gap 40 − 20, not the full £40 intercept.", "This leaves out the ½; a triangle's area is half base times height.", "This is producer surplus, using the supply intercept; consumer surplus uses the demand intercept.", "Right: base 5 units, height is 40 − 20 = £20, so ½ × 5 × 20 = £50."]}, "trap": "Using the full price or intercept as the height, or forgetting the ½. Always subtract: demand intercept minus price for CS; price minus supply intercept for PS.", "recap": "Surplus is a triangle, ½ × quantity × price gap, found at equilibrium; when a curve shifts, recalculate both areas."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.9", "kind": "method", "why": "Indirect tax and subsidy diagrams appear in data-response and calculation questions, often asking you to find who bears a tax, or what it costs the government.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Shifting the supply curve", "b": "A per-unit **indirect tax** raises costs, so **supply** shifts vertically up by the amount of the tax. A **subsidy** lowers costs, so supply shifts vertically down by the subsidy. Demand does not move. Read the new price and quantity at the new equilibrium."}, {"h": "Splitting the tax", "b": "The tax is the vertical gap between the two supply curves. **Consumer burden** = (new price − old price) × new quantity. **Producer burden** = (tax − price rise) × new quantity. Producers receive the new price minus the tax."}, {"h": "Revenue and subsidy cost", "b": "**Tax revenue** = tax per unit × new quantity. It equals consumer burden plus producer burden. For a subsidy, **government spending** = subsidy per unit × new quantity. The price fall is the consumers' gain per unit; the rest of the subsidy is the producers' gain."}, {"h": "Why elasticity matters", "b": "The more **inelastic** demand is, the more of a tax consumers bear. The more inelastic supply is, the more producers bear. Inelastic demand also means quantity falls less, so tax revenue is higher."}], "example": {"task": "A £4 per-unit tax moves equilibrium from price £15.50, quantity 450, to price £16.50, quantity 350. Find the consumer burden, producer burden and tax revenue.", "steps": [{"do": "Find the price rise: £16.50 − £15.50 = £1.00.", "why": "This is how much more each consumer pays per unit."}, {"do": "Consumer burden = £1.00 × 350 = £350.", "why": "Multiply by the new quantity, since tax is paid only on units still sold."}, {"do": "Producer share per unit = £4.00 − £1.00 = £3.00. Producer burden = £3.00 × 350 = £1,050.", "why": "Firms absorb the part of the tax not passed on in the price."}, {"do": "Tax revenue = £4 × 350 = £1,400.", "why": "Revenue is tax per unit times new quantity."}, {"do": "Check: £350 + £1,050 = £1,400.", "why": "The two burdens must add up to the revenue."}], "answer": "Consumers bear £350, producers bear £1,050 and the government raises £1,400. Producers bear more because only a quarter of the tax (£1 of £4) is passed on in a higher price, which suggests demand is more elastic than supply."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is producer burden calculated using the new quantity of 350, not the old quantity of 450?", "o": ["Because the tax is paid only on units still bought and sold after the tax", "Because the old quantity is only used for subsidies", "Because producers always sell exactly the new quantity at the old price", "Because demand has shifted left to 350 after the tax"], "a": 0, "x": "", "f": ["Right. The 100 lost units are not sold, so no tax is paid on them. All incidence areas use the new quantity.", "Wrong. The old quantity is not used for subsidies either; subsidy cost also uses the new quantity.", "Wrong. Producers receive less than the old price per unit after the tax, and the quantity is lower because of the higher price.", "Wrong. Demand has not shifted. Supply shifted up, and quantity fell by a movement along the demand curve."]}, "faded": {"task": "A £3 per-unit subsidy lowers the price from £8.00 to £6.50 and raises quantity from 200 to 260. Find the gain to producers.", "given": ["Government cost = £3 × 260 = £780.", "Consumers' gain per unit = £8.00 − £6.50 = £1.50."], "q": "What is the next step to find the total gain to producers?", "o": ["(£3.00 + £1.50) × 260 = £1,170", "£3.00 × 260 = £780", "(£3.00 − £1.50) × 260 = £390", "(£8.00 − £6.50) × 200 = £300"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. The consumers' £1.50 is part of the £3 subsidy, not extra on top of it. Subtract it from £3.00 to get the producers' share.", "Wrong. £780 is the whole subsidy, which consumers and producers share. Subtract the consumers' £1.50 first.", "Right. Producers gain the part of the subsidy not passed on in the price: £1.50 per unit, times the new quantity of 260.", "Wrong. This is the consumers' gain per unit, and it uses the old quantity. Producers' share is the rest of the subsidy."]}, "trap": "Using the old quantity, or treating the whole tax as paid by consumers. Always use the new quantity, then check that the burdens add to tax revenue.", "recap": "Tax or subsidy per unit × new quantity gives the total; the price change gives the consumers' share and the remainder is the producers'."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.10", "kind": "idea", "why": "Behavioural economics appears in short explain questions and in evaluation of rational-choice theory, so you need to name each influence and link it to demand.", "hook": {"q": "You choose a restaurant because it has a long queue outside, without reading any reviews. Why might you do this?", "o": ["You assume others know something you do not", "You have calculated the exact utility of each meal", "You are following your own past choices", "You have perfect information about every restaurant"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: you treat other people's choices as a signal of quality. This is the idea behind herd behaviour.", "Wrong: you have not calculated anything, since you skipped reviews.", "Wrong: this is not a repeat of your own past choice; it is a new restaurant.", "Wrong: if you had perfect information, you would not need the queue as a signal."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Rational choice benchmark", "b": "Traditional theory assumes consumers act **rationally** to maximise **utility**, using full information and weighing costs and benefits. **Behavioural economics** studies how real people depart from this, which affects how demand behaves."}, {"h": "Other people's choices", "b": "**Herd behaviour** means copying what others do, often because their choices seem to signal quality or safety. **Social norms** also shape what people buy. Demand can rise or fall sharply without any change in price or real quality."}, {"h": "Habit", "b": "**Habitual behaviour** means repeating past choices automatically, without comparing alternatives. It saves effort, but consumers may pay more than necessary and respond less to price changes, so demand is less **price elastic**."}, {"h": "Difficulty with calculations", "b": "Comparing complex options is hard, so people use **rules of thumb**, such as choosing the lowest headline price. This is **bounded rationality**: limited ability to process information. Consumers may then miss the best-value choice."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why many consumers buy a popular smartphone even when a cheaper phone offers the same features.", "steps": [{"do": "Say that traditional theory predicts rational consumers choosing the cheaper phone for the same utility.", "why": "This sets the benchmark the behaviour departs from."}, {"do": "Identify herd behaviour: consumers copy others, assuming popularity signals quality.", "why": "This names the specific influence of other people's choices."}, {"do": "Add habit: owners of earlier models of the brand simply buy the next one without comparing.", "why": "A second factor shows depth, and habit is a distinct concept."}, {"do": "Add difficulty with calculations: comparing specifications and contract costs is hard, so people use the brand as a shortcut.", "why": "This links to bounded rationality and rules of thumb."}, {"do": "Conclude that demand for the popular phone is higher and less price sensitive than rational theory predicts.", "why": "The final link to demand turns description into explanation."}], "answer": "Rational consumers would buy the cheaper phone. Herd behaviour, habit and difficulty comparing options mean many buy the popular brand, so its demand is higher and less price elastic than theory predicts."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example end by linking the behaviour to demand?", "o": ["Because the question asks for the exact value of elasticity", "Because explanations must show the effect on the market, not just name the behaviour", "Because herd behaviour only affects supply", "Because habit always makes demand perfectly inelastic"], "a": 1, "x": "", "f": ["Wrong: no calculation is needed; the link is a qualitative effect.", "Right: naming a concept earns little. Linking it to what happens to demand completes the chain of reasoning.", "Wrong: herd behaviour changes consumer demand, not supply.", "Wrong: habit makes demand less elastic, not perfectly inelastic."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why many households stay with the same bank account for years despite better offers elsewhere.", "given": ["Rational consumers would compare offers and switch to the one giving the most utility.", "Habit means households keep using the same account without reviewing alternatives."], "q": "Which is the best next step?", "o": ["Add that herd behaviour means households choose a bank because of its low price.", "Add that comparing interest rates, fees and conditions is complex, so people rely on rules of thumb or avoid comparing.", "State that the households have perfect information but dislike switching.", "State that the banks must have the same prices, so there is no benefit to switching."], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong: herd behaviour is about copying others, and price is not the cause here.", "Right: this adds difficulty with calculations, a second distinct factor that explains the inertia.", "Wrong: perfect information contradicts the idea of behavioural limits.", "Wrong: this assumes away the 'better offers' stated in the question."]}, "trap": "Do not just list behaviours. Name the behaviour, explain it in the context, then say how it makes demand differ from the rational-choice prediction.", "recap": "People often follow others, repeat habits and use shortcuts for hard calculations, so demand departs from rational-choice predictions."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.3.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Market failure underpins all of theme 1.3, and short-answer questions often ask you to define it or identify which type of failure a scenario shows.", "hook": {"q": "A shop on your street pays to put up bright lights. Everyone walking past benefits. What is the likeliest problem for the shop?", "o": ["It cannot easily charge passers-by, though they benefit", "It will be forced to cut its prices", "Passers-by will use the lights up, leaving none for others", "The lights will raise the shop's costs of production"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: people benefit without paying. This is the free-rider idea behind missing markets and externalities.", "Price cuts have nothing to do with it; the problem is non-payment by beneficiaries.", "Light is not used up by others, so it is non-rival; the problem is not scarcity of the light.", "The shop does bear costs, but the key issue is that benefits go to others who do not pay."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What market failure means", "b": "**Market failure** occurs when the free market leads to a misallocation of resources, so **social welfare** is not maximised. Prices fail to reflect the full costs or benefits to society, or a market is missing altogether."}, {"h": "Externalities", "b": "An **externality** is a cost or benefit to a third party, outside the market transaction. **MSC = MPC + marginal external cost**; **MSB = MPB + marginal external benefit**. Negative externalities cause over-production or over-consumption; positive ones cause under-provision."}, {"h": "Public goods", "b": "A **public good** is **non-rival** and **non-excludable**. Non-excludability lets people **free-ride**, so firms cannot charge. The market does not provide the good, a missing market. Examples: street lighting, national defence."}, {"h": "Information gaps", "b": "**Asymmetric information** means one party knows more than the other. Consumers may misjudge the benefits or costs of a good, such as underestimating the harm of smoking. They then consume the wrong amount, so resources are misallocated."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a private market may fail to provide the efficient quantity of vaccinations, which give benefits to people other than the person vaccinated.", "steps": [{"do": "Identify the failure: vaccination creates a positive externality of consumption.", "why": "Naming the correct type of market failure is the first step."}, {"do": "State that the individual considers only their own marginal private benefit (MPB).", "why": "Private decisions ignore benefits to third parties."}, {"do": "Explain that others gain lower infection risk, so marginal social benefit (MSB) is greater than MPB.", "why": "MSB = MPB + marginal external benefit."}, {"do": "Conclude that the free market quantity is where MPB meets marginal cost, below the social optimum where MSB meets it.", "why": "This shows under-consumption."}, {"do": "State the result: resources are under-allocated to vaccination and social welfare is not maximised.", "why": "This links back to the definition of market failure."}], "answer": "Vaccination has a positive externality. Buyers weigh only MPB, ignoring benefits to others, so MSB exceeds MPB. The market quantity, where MPB equals marginal cost, is below the socially optimal quantity. Resources are under-allocated, so welfare is not maximised."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer compare the free market quantity with the point where MSB meets marginal cost?", "o": ["The social optimum is where MSB equals marginal cost, so the gap shows the misallocation", "MSB is always the same as market demand", "Marginal cost is zero for vaccinations", "Firms always produce where MSB is highest"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: social efficiency is where marginal social benefit equals marginal cost; the shortfall from this shows market failure.", "MSB differs from private demand when externalities exist; that gap is the problem.", "Vaccinations have costs, so marginal cost is not zero.", "Firms follow private benefits and costs, not the highest MSB."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a chemical firm may over-produce when its factory pollutes a nearby river.", "given": ["The pollution is a negative externality of production.", "The firm considers only its marginal private cost (MPC)."], "q": "What is the next step in the answer?", "o": ["Marginal social cost is greater than MPC because of the external cost to others", "Marginal social cost is below MPC because pollution lowers costs", "Marginal social benefit exceeds MPB because of the pollution", "The river is a public good, so the firm charges for it"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: MSC = MPC + marginal external cost, so MSC lies above MPC.", "Pollution adds cost to society; MSC cannot be below MPC with a negative externality.", "This is a benefit-side statement; here the problem lies on the cost side.", "Charging is the issue only for public goods; the question concerns an externality."]}, "trap": "Do not just say a good is harmful. Show a gap between private and social costs or benefits, or a missing market, and say resources are misallocated.", "recap": "Market failure is misallocation of resources, caused by externalities, missing public goods or information gaps, so social welfare is not maximised."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.3.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Externality diagrams and explanations appear in Paper 1 and Paper 3 questions, and they underpin the case for government intervention.", "hook": {"q": "A factory's smoke harms nearby residents who never buy its products. Is that harm included in the factory's costs and so in the price?", "o": ["Yes, the market price always includes every cost", "No, the factory only counts its own costs", "Yes, but only if the residents are customers", "No, because smoke has no cost to anyone"], "a": 1, "f": ["Markets only include costs that the buyers and sellers themselves bear.", "Right. Private decision-makers count only their own costs, so harm to third parties is ignored.", "Whether the residents are customers is irrelevant; they are third parties either way.", "The smoke does have a cost, such as health damage; it is just not paid by the factory."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Private, external, social", "b": "**Private costs** (MPC) are paid by the producer; **private benefits** (MPB) go to the consumer. **External costs and benefits** fall on third parties. **Social cost** = private + external cost. **Social benefit** = private + external benefit."}, {"h": "Negative production externality", "b": "The firm ignores the **external cost**, so **MSC** lies above **MPC**. The gap is the external cost per unit. The market sets **MPB = MPC** at Qm, but the social optimum is where **MSB = MSC**, at a lower output, Q*."}, {"h": "Positive consumption externality", "b": "The consumer ignores the **external benefit**, so **MSB** lies above **MPB**. The market sets MPB = MPC at Qm. The social optimum, where MSB = MSC, is at a higher output, Q*. The good is **under-consumed**."}, {"h": "Welfare loss and gain", "b": "The **welfare loss** is the triangle between MSC and MSB, between Q* and Qm, in the over-production case. Each unit there costs society more than it benefits. In the under-consumption case, the same triangle is the **welfare gain** from moving to Q*."}], "example": {"task": "Explain, with the help of a diagram, why a chemical works that pollutes a river leads to market failure.", "steps": [{"do": "Define the externality: the works pays its private costs, but the pollution imposes an external cost on third parties such as anglers, so MSC = MPC + external cost.", "why": "Defining the external cost shows why the MSC curve lies above MPC."}, {"do": "On the diagram, MSC is above MPC by the external cost per unit. The market equilibrium is where MPB = MPC, at output Qm.", "why": "Firms and consumers ignore the third-party cost."}, {"do": "The social optimum is where MSB = MSC, at Q*, which is to the left of Qm.", "why": "Social efficiency needs all costs and benefits to be counted."}, {"do": "Between Q* and Qm, MSC exceeds MSB, so the good is over-produced.", "why": "This shows the misallocation of resources."}, {"do": "The welfare loss is the triangle between MSC and MSB from Q* to Qm.", "why": "It measures the net loss of social welfare."}], "answer": "The works ignores the external cost of pollution, so MSC lies above MPC. The market produces where MPB = MPC (Qm), beyond the social optimum where MSB = MSC (Q*). Over-production creates a welfare loss triangle, so there is market failure."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the welfare loss triangle only cover output between Q* and Qm?", "o": ["Because below Q* the external cost is zero", "Because the market does not produce below Q*", "Because below Q*, MSB exceeds MSC, so each unit adds net social benefit", "Because above Qm, firms stop producing"], "a": 2, "f": ["The external cost is present on every unit, not only beyond Q*.", "The market does produce those units; they are efficient, not a loss.", "Right. Up to Q*, MSB > MSC, so each unit adds net benefit. Only units beyond Q* cost society more than they give.", "Qm is where the market output stops because MPB = MPC, not because firms abruptly stop."]}, "faded": {"task": "Adult evening classes give an external benefit to employers and communities. Explain why the market under-provides them.", "given": ["Step 1: The learner ignores the external benefit, so MSB lies above MPB.", "Step 2: The market equilibrium is where MPB = MPC, at output Qm."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Show that the social optimum is where MSB = MSC, at a higher output Q*, so classes are under-consumed", "Show that the social optimum is where MSB = MSC, at a lower output Q*, so classes are over-consumed", "Show that MSC lies above MPC, so the market over-produces", "Show that the welfare gain is the rectangle between Qm and Q*"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. MSB lies above MPB, so MSB = MSC is to the right of Qm, meaning under-consumption.", "With a positive externality the optimum is at a higher, not lower, output, so there is under-consumption.", "MSC above MPC is a negative production externality; this question is about a consumption benefit.", "The gain is a triangle between MSB and MSC, not a rectangle."]}, "trap": "Drawing welfare loss as a rectangle or the whole external cost. It is only the triangle between MSC and MSB from the social optimum to market output.", "recap": "Markets equate MPB with MPC, ignoring externalities; the social optimum is where MSB = MSC, and the gap between them is a welfare-loss triangle."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.3.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Public goods are a standard market failure topic: expect definitions, examples and short explain questions on why markets under-provide them, often as 4 to 6 mark answers.", "hook": {"q": "A street has one lamp post. If you and your neighbour both use its light, does it get dimmer for either of you?", "o": ["No, and you can't stop the neighbour using it", "No, but the owner can easily stop the neighbour", "Yes, because it is shared between users", "Yes, but only at night"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Use doesn't reduce anyone else's, and nobody can be excluded. That's the core of a public good.", "Not for a lamp: you can't switch light off for one person, so exclusion is impractical.", "Light isn't used up by extra users, so it doesn't dim.", "Time of day doesn't change the point: more users don't reduce the light."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Two defining features", "b": "A **pure public good** is **non-rival**: one person's consumption does not reduce the amount available to others. It is also **non-excludable**: people cannot be stopped from using it, even if they haven't paid. Examples: national defence, street lighting."}, {"h": "The free-rider problem", "b": "Because nobody can be excluded, people can enjoy the good without paying. This is the **free-rider problem**. Each person hopes others will pay. Firms cannot charge a price, so they earn too little revenue to cover costs."}, {"h": "Under-provision by markets", "b": "With no price signal, **effective demand** is far below true willingness to pay. Private firms will not supply, so there is a **missing market** or a very low output. This is market failure and means resources are misallocated."}, {"h": "Not the same as public provision", "b": "Public goods are defined by their **characteristics**, not by who provides them. The NHS and state schools are publicly provided but are rival or excludable. Governments often provide true public goods through **taxation**, which overcomes free riding."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a free market is unlikely to provide flood defences for a coastal town. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Flood defences are non-rival: protecting one household does not reduce the protection for others.", "why": "Defines the first characteristic and applies it to the context."}, {"do": "They are non-excludable: a sea wall protects every house behind it, whether or not the owner paid.", "why": "Defines the second characteristic with application."}, {"do": "So households can free ride, hoping neighbours pay, and a firm cannot charge those who benefit.", "why": "Links the characteristics to the free-rider problem."}, {"do": "Revenue falls short of cost, so no private firm supplies. The market is missing, causing resource misallocation.", "why": "Concludes with the consequence: under-provision as market failure."}], "answer": "Flood defences are non-rival and non-excludable. Households can free ride, so firms cannot charge everyone who benefits and revenue falls short of costs. No private firm will supply them. The market is missing, so resources are misallocated."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention that a firm cannot charge those who benefit?", "o": ["Non-excludability stops it collecting revenue, which is why the market fails", "Non-rivalry means the firm's costs are too high", "Flood defences are provided by the government by definition", "Consumers do not value flood defences"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Without the power to exclude, no price can be enforced, so revenue is inadequate.", "Non-rivalry means extra users cost nothing; the problem is revenue, not cost.", "Provider doesn't define the good; the characteristics do.", "People value protection highly; free riding hides it in the market."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a free market may under-provide a national radio emergency-warning broadcast. (4 marks)", "given": ["The broadcast is non-rival: one listener tuning in does not reduce the signal for others.", "It is non-excludable: anyone with a radio can receive it without paying."], "q": "What is the next step in the answer?", "o": ["People can free ride, so no price can be charged and revenue is too low to cover costs", "The broadcast is a merit good, so people underestimate its benefits", "Government provision makes it a public good", "Marginal cost is high, so firms will not supply it"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. This links the two features to free riding and weak revenue, leading to under-provision.", "Merit goods involve information failure; this answer should follow the free-rider chain.", "Provision by government is a solution, not what makes it public, and it skips the explanation.", "Non-rivalry means the marginal cost of an extra listener is zero, not high."]}, "trap": "Don't call a good public just because the government provides it. Check both features: non-rival and non-excludable. Schools and the NHS fail that test.", "recap": "Public goods are non-rival and non-excludable, so free riding prevents firms charging and markets under-provide them."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.3.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Information failure appears in multiple-choice, short answers and evaluation of government intervention across Papers 1 and 3.", "hook": {"q": "You buy a used phone online. The seller knows its battery is failing. You do not. What is likely to happen to the price you are willing to pay?", "o": ["You pay the price of a perfect phone", "You offer less, expecting some could be faulty", "You always refuse to buy any used phone", "The price is irrelevant to you"], "a": 1, "f": ["Without knowing quality you would not assume the best case.", "Right: uncertainty about quality lowers what buyers will pay, which is the start of market failure.", "Most buyers still buy, just at a lower price for the risk.", "Price is how buyers respond to the risk."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Symmetric and asymmetric", "b": "**Symmetric information** means buyers and sellers have the same information. **Asymmetric information** means one party knows more than the other. Sellers often know more (used cars), but buyers can know more too (insurance applicants)."}, {"h": "Why resources are misallocated", "b": "Decisions rely on price signals and perceived costs and benefits. If these are wrong, consumers buy too much or too little. This causes **misallocation of resources** and a loss of **allocative efficiency**: a form of **market failure**."}, {"h": "Examples of gaps", "b": "**Adverse selection**: hidden information before a deal, such as risky people buying insurance. **Moral hazard**: riskier behaviour after the deal, such as being less careful once insured. Consumers may also misjudge harm from **demerit goods** or benefits of **merit goods**."}, {"h": "Possible remedies", "b": "Government can require **labelling**, run **information campaigns**, or regulate professionals. Firms can offer **guarantees** and ratings. Limits: **cost**, consumers may ignore information, and **government failure** from poor data."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how asymmetric information in the market for second-hand cars can lead to market failure.", "steps": [{"do": "Sellers know the true quality of a car; buyers cannot tell good cars from poor ones.", "why": "Defines the asymmetric information precisely and names who is better informed."}, {"do": "Buyers are unsure, so they offer a price reflecting average quality.", "why": "Links the information gap to the price signal."}, {"do": "Owners of good cars find the price too low and withdraw them, leaving mainly poor cars.", "why": "Shows adverse selection: the better products leave the market."}, {"do": "The market shrinks and some mutually beneficial trades do not happen.", "why": "Shows resources are not allocated where they are valued most."}, {"do": "So the market fails: output differs from the allocatively efficient level.", "why": "Concludes with the link to market failure."}], "answer": "Sellers know car quality; buyers do not. Buyers offer an average price, so good-car owners withdraw, leaving poor cars. Fewer beneficial trades occur, so resources are misallocated and the market fails."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say good cars are withdrawn from the market?", "o": ["Their owners receive a price below the value of their car", "Government bans them from sale", "Buyers prefer faulty cars", "Good cars are cheaper to produce"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the average price undervalues good cars, so their sellers exit.", "Nothing here involves a ban; the cause is the price signal.", "Buyers do not prefer faulty cars; they simply cannot tell the difference.", "Production cost is irrelevant to this second-hand sale."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how moral hazard in car insurance can cause market failure.", "given": ["Once a driver is insured, the insurer bears much of the cost of any accident.", "The insurer cannot observe how carefully the driver behaves."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["The driver may take more risks, so more claims occur than the insurer expected", "Drivers with high risk buy insurance before the contract", "The government sets a price ceiling on premiums", "Premiums fall because accidents are fewer"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: behaviour changes after the contract, increasing risk and costs.", "That describes adverse selection, which happens before the contract.", "A price ceiling is a policy, not the next step of the explanation.", "Riskier behaviour raises claims, so premiums would not fall."]}, "trap": "Do not say asymmetric information only hurts buyers, or confuse adverse selection (before the deal) with moral hazard (after it). Always finish by linking to misallocation.", "recap": "Asymmetric information distorts decisions and price signals, so resources are misallocated and the market fails."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.4.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Government intervention appears in Paper 1 and Paper 3 as diagram-based explain and evaluate questions; accurate tax, subsidy and price-control effects underpin your analysis marks.", "hook": {"q": "A government taxes cigarettes heavily. Smokers find it hard to cut down. Who is likely to bear most of the tax?", "o": ["Smokers, because their demand is inelastic", "Tobacco firms, because they pay the tax to the government", "Both equally, because tax is always split 50:50", "Nobody, because the price stays the same"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Inelastic demand means buyers keep buying, so firms pass most of the tax on in the price.", "Firms hand over the tax, but who bears it depends on elasticity, not on who pays the government.", "The split depends on PED and PES, not a fixed 50:50.", "The tax raises costs, so price rises; it does not stay the same."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Taxes and subsidies", "b": "A **specific tax** is a fixed £ per unit and shifts supply left in parallel. An **ad valorem tax** is a percentage of price, so the supply curve pivots. A **subsidy** lowers costs and shifts supply right. Both change equilibrium price and quantity."}, {"h": "Who bears the tax", "b": "**Tax incidence** is the share of the tax borne by consumers and producers. The less elastic side bears more. If demand is less elastic than supply, consumers pay most; if supply is less elastic than demand, producers pay most. Consumer burden is the price rise, not the full tax."}, {"h": "Price controls", "b": "A **maximum price** set below equilibrium causes **excess demand** (a shortage). A **minimum price** set above equilibrium causes **excess supply** (a surplus). A maximum price set above equilibrium, or a minimum price set below it, has no effect, because it is not binding."}, {"h": "Other methods", "b": "**Tradable permits** cap pollution and let firms trade the right to emit. **State provision** means government supplies the good directly. **Regulation** sets legal rules, and **information provision** corrects imperfect information. Each carries costs and possible government failure."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a subsidy on bus travel affects the market and who benefits.", "steps": [{"do": "A per-unit subsidy lowers firms' costs of supplying, so the supply curve shifts right (down) by the amount of the subsidy.", "why": "Subsidies change supply, not demand."}, {"do": "The new equilibrium has a lower price and a higher quantity, because the right shift moves along the demand curve.", "why": "Shows the market outcome."}, {"do": "Consumers gain through a lower price, but the price falls by less than the subsidy unless supply is perfectly elastic or demand is perfectly inelastic.", "why": "Incidence is shared."}, {"do": "Producers also gain, since the price they receive is the consumer price plus the subsidy.", "why": "Both sides benefit."}, {"do": "The government pays the subsidy cost, an opportunity cost that could fund other spending.", "why": "Links to a cost of intervention."}], "answer": "Supply shifts right by the subsidy, lowering price and raising quantity. Consumers and producers share the gain by elasticity. The government bears the cost, with an opportunity cost."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example say the consumers' price fall is less than the subsidy?", "o": ["Because the subsidy is shared between consumers and producers", "Because demand shifts left when subsidies are paid", "Because subsidies only help producers", "Because the government keeps part of the subsidy"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Producers receive part of the subsidy as a higher price received, so consumers see a smaller fall.", "Subsidies do not shift demand; they shift supply.", "Both sides gain; consumers get a lower price.", "The government pays out the full subsidy; it does not keep part."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain the effect of a £2 specific tax on a good whose supply is price inelastic and less elastic than demand.", "given": ["The tax shifts supply left (up) by £2 at every quantity."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Producers bear most of the tax, as they cannot cut supply easily, so price rises by less than £2", "Consumers bear the full £2 because the tax shifts supply", "Price falls because supply has fallen", "Quantity traded rises as firms get higher revenue"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Supply is the less elastic side, so firms bear most of the tax and the consumer price rises by less than £2.", "Consumers bear the full £2 only if demand is perfectly inelastic or supply perfectly elastic; here supply is the less elastic side.", "Lower supply raises the equilibrium price; it does not lower it.", "A leftward shift reduces equilibrium quantity, not raises it."]}, "trap": "Do not assume consumers pay the whole tax. The consumer share is the price rise, and the rest falls on producers; check which side is less elastic.", "recap": "Taxes and subsidies shift supply, and the burden or benefit is shared according to relative price elasticities."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.4.2", "kind": "writing", "why": "Evaluation questions on intervention often ask you to judge a policy, and government failure is a main counter-argument in extended answers.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What counts as failure", "b": "**Government failure** occurs when intervention causes a **net welfare loss**, so resources are allocated less efficiently than without it. A policy that only misses its target is not enough. You must show the outcome is worse overall."}, {"h": "Main causes", "b": "Key causes: **unintended consequences** (such as black markets), **distortion of price signals**, **administrative costs**, and **information gaps** (the government cannot know the right tax or price)."}, {"h": "Showing it with diagrams", "b": "A tax set above the marginal external cost shifts supply too far left. Output falls below the social optimum. The **welfare loss** is the triangle between marginal social benefit and marginal social cost on the lost units."}, {"h": "Evaluating", "b": "Weigh government failure against **market failure**. Judge by size: is the welfare loss smaller than the original one? Consider time, data quality and the scale of the policy. Reach a supported conclusion."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate whether a government price cap on energy bills is likely to lead to government failure. (Model answer built in parts.)", "steps": [{"do": "A price cap sets a maximum price below the free market equilibrium, so quantity demanded rises and quantity supplied falls.", "why": "Knowledge: defines the intervention and its direct effect on the market."}, {"do": "In the energy market, if suppliers cannot cover costs they may cut investment or leave, so supply shrinks and shortages emerge, distorting price signals.", "why": "Application and analysis: links the policy to a causal chain and a specific cause of failure."}, {"do": "This creates a welfare loss, because units valued above their cost are no longer produced, and administration of the cap adds costs, so the outcome may be worse than no intervention.", "why": "Analysis: shows the net welfare loss, which defines government failure."}, {"do": "However, if the cap is set close to the equilibrium, the loss may be small compared with the gain to vulnerable households, so judgement depends on its level and duration.", "why": "Evaluation: weighs magnitude and time, balancing the argument."}, {"do": "Overall, failure is likely if the cap is long-term and set well below cost, but a modest, temporary cap may bring net benefits.", "why": "Conclusion: a supported judgement that follows from the reasoning."}], "answer": "A cap below equilibrium creates excess demand, can cut supply and investment, and distorts price signals, causing a welfare loss and admin costs. Failure is likely if long-term and far below cost, but a modest, temporary cap may bring net benefits."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer end with a judgement about the level and duration of the cap?", "o": ["Because evaluation needs a supported conclusion based on magnitude and time", "Because the cap level never affects the welfare loss", "Because the answer must always conclude failure occurs", "Because definitions earn the marks at the end of an essay"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: evaluation marks reward weighing factors such as size and time and reaching a justified conclusion.", "Wrong: the level of the cap strongly affects the size of the welfare loss.", "Wrong: conclusions must follow the reasoning, and failure is not always the answer.", "Wrong: definitions earn knowledge marks early on; the end is for judgement."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate whether a government subsidy for electric cars may cause government failure.", "given": ["Knowledge: A subsidy lowers costs, shifting supply right so price falls and output rises.", "Application: Take-up may be mostly by richer households who would have bought anyway."], "q": "What is the best next step in the answer?", "o": ["Explain that the cost to taxpayers and wasted spending may outweigh the benefit, causing a net welfare loss", "State that subsidies always remove externalities so there can be no failure", "Define market failure at length without linking to the subsidy", "Conclude immediately that the subsidy has succeeded because output rose"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: it develops analysis of opportunity cost and a net welfare loss, the core of government failure.", "Wrong: assuming a subsidy always works ignores possible over-subsidy and wasted spending.", "Wrong: a long definition is not linked to the case, so it earns little analysis credit.", "Wrong: more output alone does not show net welfare gain; costs must be weighed."]}, "trap": "Do not say a policy is government failure just because it missed its target. Show a net welfare loss compared with no intervention, then weigh it against the original market failure.", "recap": "Government failure is intervention causing a net welfare loss; evaluate by weighing its size against the market failure it aimed to correct."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.1.1", "kind": "method", "why": "Theme 2 is examined on Papers 2 and 3, where you may need to calculate real GDP and GDP per head before judging living standards.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Real versus nominal GDP", "b": "**Nominal GDP** values output at current prices, so inflation inflates the figure. **Real GDP** uses constant prices to show volume growth: $\\text{Real GDP}=\\frac{\\text{Nominal GDP}}{\\text{Price index}}\\times 100$. Always use real figures to measure economic growth."}, {"h": "GDP per head and GNI", "b": "Divide real GDP by population to get **real GDP per head**, showing average output per person. **Gross National Income** (GNI) adds net property income from abroad to GDP: income UK residents earn on assets overseas minus income foreign owners earn on UK assets."}, {"h": "PPPs and wellbeing limits", "b": "**Purchasing Power Parities** (PPPs) adjust exchange rates for local living costs when comparing countries. Even PPP real GDP per head ignores **income distribution**, unpaid work, pollution and leisure, so economists also track ONS **national wellbeing** measures."}], "example": {"task": "A made-up economy has Year 1 nominal GDP £200bn (price index 100, population 10m) and Year 2 nominal GDP £231bn (price index 105, population 11m). Find real GDP growth and real GDP per head growth.", "steps": [{"do": "Year 1 real GDP is $£200\\text{bn}$ because the base index is 100. Year 1 real GDP per head is $\\frac{£200\\text{bn}}{10\\text{m}}=£20,000$.", "why": "At a base index of 100, nominal and real values are equal."}, {"do": "Convert Year 2 nominal GDP to real GDP: $\\frac{£231\\text{bn}}{105}\\times 100=£220\\text{bn}$.", "why": "Dividing by the price index removes the 5% rise in prices."}, {"do": "Calculate total real GDP growth: $\\frac{220-200}{200}\\times 100=10\\%$.", "why": "Percentage change equals new minus old, divided by old, times 100."}, {"do": "Find Year 2 real GDP per head: $\\frac{£220\\text{bn}}{11\\text{m}}=£20,000$, so growth per head is $0\\%$.", "why": "Population also rose by 10%, leaving average real output per person unchanged."}], "answer": "Total real GDP grew by 10%, from £200bn to £220bn, but real GDP per head stayed at £20,000 (0% growth) because population grew at the same 10% rate."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why did Step 2 divide £231bn by 105 and multiply by 100 instead of comparing £231bn directly with £200bn?", "o": ["To convert the figure from GDP into Gross National Income", "To remove the 5% inflation included in Year 2 nominal GDP", "To adjust the total figure for the 1m rise in population", "To express the output using Purchasing Power Parity rates"], "a": 1, "f": ["Converting GDP to GNI adds net property income from abroad, not a price index.", "Deflating by the price index removes price rises so only volume growth remains.", "Population is accounted for in Step 4 by dividing real GDP by population.", "PPPs adjust for cross-country price differences, not inflation between two years in one country."]}, "faded": {"task": "In another made-up economy, Year 1 real GDP is £400bn (index 100). In Year 2, nominal GDP is £462bn and the price index is 110. Find percentage real GDP growth.", "given": ["Year 1 real GDP = $£400\\text{bn}$.", "Year 2 real GDP = $\\frac{£462\\text{bn}}{110}\\times 100 = £420\\text{bn}$."], "q": "What is the next step to find percentage real GDP growth?", "o": ["$\\frac{462-400}{400}\\times 100 = 15.5\\%$", "$\\frac{420-400}{420}\\times 100 = 4.8\\%$", "$\\frac{420-400}{400}\\times 100 = 5.0\\%$", "$\\frac{462-420}{420}\\times 100 = 10.0\\%$"], "a": 2, "f": ["This uses Year 2 nominal GDP (£462bn) instead of deflated real GDP (£420bn).", "This divides by the new value (£420bn) instead of the original Year 1 value (£400bn).", "Subtracting old real GDP from new real GDP and dividing by old real GDP gives 5.0%.", "This calculates the 10% inflation rate between nominal and real GDP, not real growth."]}, "trap": "Students often compute nominal growth or divide by the new figure instead of the base figure. Always deflate first, divide by Year 1, then check population.", "recap": "Always deflate nominal GDP with the price index before calculating growth, and divide by population to judge living standards."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.1.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Inflation questions appear in Papers 1 and 2 as data and short answers, and underpin macro evaluation. You must define terms precisely and explain causes and effects.", "hook": {"q": "The inflation rate falls from 5% to 3%. What is happening to prices in the shops?", "o": ["They are falling on average", "They are still rising, but more slowly", "They are unchanged", "They have fallen by 2%"], "a": 1, "f": ["Falling prices would need a negative inflation rate, not 3%.", "Right. A positive rate means the general price level is still rising, just at a slower pace.", "Unchanged prices would mean 0% inflation.", "The 2 points is the fall in the rate, not a fall in prices."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three key terms", "b": "**Inflation** is a sustained rise in the general price level. **Deflation** is a sustained fall in the general price level, so the rate is negative. **Disinflation** is a fall in the rate of inflation: prices still rise, more slowly."}, {"h": "Measuring inflation", "b": "The **CPI** tracks the cost of a **weighted basket** of goods and services bought by households, with weights based on spending shares. The **RPI** also includes housing costs such as mortgage interest and council tax. Rate = change in index ÷ old index × 100."}, {"h": "Causes of inflation", "b": "**Demand-pull** inflation comes from AD rising faster than the economy's capacity, shifting AD right. **Cost-push** inflation comes from higher production costs, shifting SRAS left. Rapid growth in the **money supply** can also fuel demand-pull inflation."}, {"h": "Effects of inflation", "b": "Inflation erodes **purchasing power** and the **real value** of savings if interest rates are below inflation. Firms face **menu costs** and uncertainty, which can reduce investment. Higher UK prices can cut **international competitiveness**, so exports fall."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a rise in aggregate demand can cause inflation.", "steps": [{"do": "AD rises, for example after a cut in interest rates raises consumption and investment. The AD curve shifts right.", "why": "Names a specific trigger and the diagram movement."}, {"do": "If the economy is near full capacity, the SRAS curve is steep, so extra demand raises prices more than output.", "why": "Links the size of the price effect to spare capacity."}, {"do": "Firms compete for scarce labour and materials, so their costs rise and they pass these on in higher prices.", "why": "Shows the mechanism behind the price rise."}, {"do": "The general price level rises. If this continues over time, it is demand-pull inflation.", "why": "Concludes by tying to the definition of sustained rise."}], "answer": "A cut in interest rates raises consumption and investment, shifting AD right. Near full capacity, SRAS is steep, so output rises little. Firms bid up wages and input costs and raise prices. The general price level rises; if sustained, this is demand-pull inflation."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the economy is near full capacity?", "o": ["Because AD can only shift right at full capacity", "Because with little spare capacity, extra demand mainly raises prices, not output", "Because full capacity means SRAS shifts left", "Because inflation cannot occur below full capacity"], "a": 1, "f": ["AD can shift right at any level of output; capacity affects the result, not the shift.", "Right. With little spare capacity SRAS is steep, so more demand pushes up prices rather than output.", "A leftward SRAS shift is cost-push; here SRAS does not move.", "Prices can rise below full capacity, though by less; it is a matter of degree."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain one effect of inflation on savers.", "given": ["Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level.", "Suppose a savings account pays 2% interest and inflation is 4%."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["The real interest rate is about −2%, so the purchasing power of savings falls", "The real interest rate is +6%, so savers gain purchasing power", "The real interest rate is 2%, because inflation does not affect interest", "The real interest rate is about −2%, so savers gain purchasing power"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Real rate ≈ nominal rate − inflation = 2% − 4% = −2%, so savings buy less than before.", "6% adds inflation instead of subtracting it.", "Inflation affects the real return, even though the nominal rate stays at 2%.", "The calculation is right, but a negative real rate means savers lose purchasing power."]}, "trap": "Calling any fall in the inflation rate 'deflation'. Check the sign: a positive rate that falls is disinflation; only a negative rate is deflation.", "recap": "Inflation is a sustained rise in prices; disinflation is slower inflation; deflation is falling prices; CPI measures it with a weighted basket."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.1.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "The 9EC0 specification asks you to distinguish the ILO (Labour Force Survey) and Claimant Count measures and to explain the types and consequences of unemployment.", "hook": {"q": "An unemployed parent stops applying for jobs to care full-time for a relative. What happens to the UK ILO unemployment figure?", "o": ["It rises by one because they still have no job", "It stays the same until they reach pension age", "It falls by one as they become economically inactive", "It falls only if they were claiming benefits"], "a": 2, "f": ["To count as ILO unemployed you must be actively seeking work and available to start.", "Age does not matter: someone neither seeking work nor available to start is economically inactive.", "Stopping job search moves a person from unemployed to economically inactive, reducing the ILO count.", "Benefit status affects the Claimant Count, not the ILO survey definition."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Two UK unemployment measures", "b": "The **Labour Force Survey** (ILO measure) counts jobless people who actively sought work in the past four weeks and can start within two weeks. The **Claimant Count** records people claiming unemployment-related benefits. People neither working nor seeking work are **economically inactive**."}, {"h": "Main types of unemployment", "b": "**Cyclical unemployment** comes from a lack of aggregate demand in a downturn. **Structural unemployment** arises when industries decline and workers face occupational or geographical immobility. **Frictional unemployment** is short-term search between jobs; seasonal and real-wage unemployment are the other main types."}, {"h": "Effects on the economy", "b": "High unemployment cuts household incomes and firm sales, and worsens the **fiscal balance** through lower tax receipts and higher welfare spending. Long-term joblessness causes **hysteresis**, where lost skills reduce future employability. Rising **underemployment** also leaves labour resources underused."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the UK Labour Force Survey (ILO) measure of unemployment can differ from the Claimant Count.", "steps": [{"do": "Define both measures: the LFS counts people without a job who have actively sought work and are available to start, whereas the Claimant Count counts people claiming unemployment-related benefits.", "why": "Clear definitions show that the two measures count different groups."}, {"do": "Point out that some jobseekers are not eligible for these benefits, for example because of a partner's income or their own savings.", "why": "Benefit rules can leave genuine jobseekers out of the Claimant Count while the LFS still counts them."}, {"do": "Note that some eligible jobseekers choose not to claim because of stigma or the effort of applying, yet they still appear in the LFS.", "why": "Non-take-up is a second reason the LFS can exceed the Claimant Count."}, {"do": "Note that under Universal Credit some people in low-paid work must search for more work, so they enter the Claimant Count although the LFS counts them as employed.", "why": "This pushes the Claimant Count up, so the gap can run either way."}, {"do": "Conclude that eligibility rules and non-take-up push the LFS above the Claimant Count, while Universal Credit rules push the Claimant Count up, so neither measure is always the larger.", "why": "Linking back to the question completes a coherent explanation."}], "answer": "The two measures differ because the LFS counts all jobless people actively seeking and available for work, whereas the Claimant Count leaves out jobseekers who are ineligible or do not claim, but includes some low-paid workers on Universal Credit who must look for more work. So either figure can be the higher one."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 2 mention jobseekers with a higher-earning partner or savings?", "o": ["Because they are classed as economically inactive in the LFS", "Because they are counted twice in the Labour Force Survey", "Because they always count as structurally unemployed workers", "Because benefit rules can exclude them from the Claimant Count despite job search"], "a": 3, "f": ["If they actively seek work and are available, the LFS counts them as unemployed.", "The LFS is a household sample survey and does not double-count respondents.", "Ineligibility for benefits does not determine which type of unemployment a worker faces.", "Means-tested rules can keep them out of the Claimant Count, while the LFS still counts them if they seek work."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a UK recession affects a small high-street toy shop and its staff.", "given": ["A recession lowers aggregate demand, causing cyclical unemployment across the economy.", "With lower household incomes, fewer customers buy non-essential gifts at the toy shop."], "q": "Which next linked point best develops the economic effects on the firm and workers?", "o": ["Lower sales force cuts in hours or staff, raising underemployment or unemployment", "Lower sales raise the shop's profits because its tax rate automatically drops to zero", "Staff who lose hours immediately leave the economy and count as seasonal workers", "Reduced demand shifts the labour supply curve rightwards and raises hourly wages"], "a": 0, "f": ["This links lower firm revenue directly to labour demand, underemployment and job losses.", "Lower sales usually cut profits, and tax rates do not automatically fall to zero in a recession.", "Cutting hours creates underemployment, and losing a job in a slump is cyclical, not seasonal.", "Lower demand for toys shifts the derived demand for retail labour leftwards, not supply rightwards."]}, "trap": "Students often call everyone without a job unemployed. Check whether they are actively seeking work and available; if not, they are economically inactive.", "recap": "Unemployment excludes the economically inactive, differs between LFS and Claimant Count, and depends on demand, structure and skills."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.1.4", "kind": "method", "why": "Theme 2 is examined on Papers 2 and 3, where you may be given balance of payments figures to calculate and interpret the current account balance.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Trade in goods and services", "b": "The balance of payments records financial flows between the UK and the rest of the world. **Trade in goods** is physical exports minus imports, while **trade in services** covers invisibles such as banking, insurance and tourism. Together they form the **trade balance**."}, {"h": "Primary and secondary income", "b": "**Primary income** records net flows of wages, interest, profits and dividends earned on cross-border investments. **Secondary income** records net current transfers where nothing is supplied in return, such as overseas aid and remittances."}, {"h": "Deficits and interconnectedness", "b": "A **current account deficit** means total outflows across all four parts exceed inflows. It is financed by net inflows on the **financial account**. Interconnectedness means deficits in some countries are matched by surpluses in others, and a slowdown abroad cuts demand for UK exports."}], "example": {"task": "A made-up economy has goods exports £180bn, goods imports £240bn, services exports £110bn, services imports £75bn, net primary income −£15bn and net secondary income −£10bn. Find its current account balance.", "steps": [{"do": "Calculate trade in goods: $£180\\text{bn}-£240\\text{bn}=-£60\\text{bn}$.", "why": "Exports are credits (+) and imports are debits (−)."}, {"do": "Calculate trade in services: $£110\\text{bn}-£75\\text{bn}=+£35\\text{bn}$.", "why": "Service exports exceed service imports, creating a services surplus."}, {"do": "Find the overall trade balance: $-£60\\text{bn}+£35\\text{bn}=-£25\\text{bn}$.", "why": "The services surplus partly offsets the goods deficit."}, {"do": "Add primary and secondary income: $-£25\\text{bn}+(-£15\\text{bn})+(-£10\\text{bn})=-£50\\text{bn}$.", "why": "The current account includes all four components, giving a £50bn deficit."}], "answer": "The economy has a trade deficit of £25bn and an overall current account deficit of £50bn (−£50bn)."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 4 add −£15bn and −£10bn after already finding the −£25bn trade balance?", "o": ["Because primary and secondary income are part of the current account", "Because every deficit must be doubled to match the financial account", "Because goods exports are omitted until the final step", "Because overseas aid is subtracted twice under Edexcel rules"], "a": 0, "f": ["The current account equals trade in goods, trade in services, primary income and secondary income combined.", "The financial account finances the deficit separately; you never double a trade deficit.", "Goods exports were already included in Step 1 when finding the goods balance.", "Overseas aid is included once inside secondary income, never subtracted twice."]}, "faded": {"task": "Another made-up economy has trade in goods −£40bn, trade in services +£52bn, net primary income −£8bn and net secondary income −£6bn. Find its current account balance.", "given": ["Trade balance = $-£40\\text{bn} + £52\\text{bn} = +£12\\text{bn}$."], "q": "What is the next step to find the overall current account balance?", "o": ["$+£12\\text{bn} + £8\\text{bn} + £6\\text{bn} = +£26\\text{bn}$ surplus", "$+£12\\text{bn} - £8\\text{bn} - £6\\text{bn} = -£2\\text{bn}$ deficit", "$-£40\\text{bn} - £8\\text{bn} - £6\\text{bn} = -£54\\text{bn}$ deficit", "$+£52\\text{bn} - £8\\text{bn} - £6\\text{bn} = +£38\\text{bn}$ surplus"], "a": 1, "f": ["This flips the negative signs on primary and secondary income, turning net outflows into inflows.", "Adding −£8bn and −£6bn to the +£12bn trade surplus leaves a −£2bn current account deficit.", "This forgets the +£52bn surplus on trade in services.", "This omits the −£40bn deficit on trade in goods."]}, "trap": "Students often stop at exports minus imports and forget primary and secondary income, or misread minus signs. Always list all four components with clear + and − signs.", "recap": "Sum trade in goods, trade in services, primary income and secondary income with careful signs to find the current account balance."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "AD/AS analysis runs through Edexcel Theme 2, so you must separate movements along the AD curve from shifts of it accurately.", "hook": {"q": "If the UK's general price level falls while government policy and confidence stay the same, what happens to the AD curve?", "o": ["The whole AD curve shifts to the right", "There is a movement down along the existing AD curve", "The whole AD curve shifts to the left", "The AD curve rotates to become horizontal"], "a": 1, "f": ["A rightward shift needs a non-price rise in C, I, G or (X−M) at every price level.", "Price level is on the vertical axis, so a lower price level causes an extension along AD.", "Lower prices raise real quantity of output demanded rather than shifting AD left.", "The AD curve remains downward-sloping; a price change moves along it rather than flattening it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Components of aggregate demand", "b": "**Aggregate demand** (AD) is total planned spending on UK-produced goods and services at a given price level: $AD=C+I+G+(X-M)$. **Net trade** $(X-M)$ subtracts imports because spending on foreign goods leaves the UK circular flow."}, {"h": "Relative size in the UK", "b": "In the UK, **consumption** ($C$) is the largest component of AD, so even a small percentage change in household spending is a large change in AD in pounds. **Government expenditure** ($G$) is next largest, **investment** ($I$) is smaller but volatile, and net trade is usually small and negative."}, {"h": "Why AD slopes downwards", "b": "A lower price level increases real output demanded through three channels: the **wealth effect** (savings buy more), the **trade effect** (UK exports look cheaper abroad), and the **interest rate effect** (less money is needed for transactions, so interest rates tend to fall and borrowing rises)."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the aggregate demand curve slopes downwards from left to right.", "steps": [{"do": "State what the axes show: the general price level on the vertical axis and real GDP (output) on the horizontal axis, with an inverse relationship.", "why": "Naming the axes grounds the explanation in the Edexcel AD diagram."}, {"do": "Explain the trade effect: if the UK price level falls relative to other countries, UK exports become more price-competitive and imports look dearer, raising $(X-M)$.", "why": "Higher net trade directly increases the real output demanded at lower prices."}, {"do": "Explain the wealth effect: a lower price level raises the real purchasing power of households' cash and savings, encouraging higher consumption ($C$).", "why": "Higher real wealth boosts household spending along the AD curve."}, {"do": "Explain the interest rate effect: lower prices reduce the demand for money and inflation pressure, allowing lower interest rates that encourage borrowing for $C$ and $I$.", "why": "Lower interest rates make borrowing cheaper, so spending rises as the price level falls."}], "answer": "AD slopes downwards because a lower price level makes UK exports more competitive (raising $X-M$), increases the real value of household wealth (raising $C$), and allows lower interest rates (boosting $C$ and $I$), so higher real output is demanded."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 2 say UK exports become more price-competitive when the UK price level falls?", "o": ["Because the UK government automatically pays export subsidies", "Because a lower price level eliminates all UK imports", "Because UK goods become cheaper relative to foreign rivals", "Because real GDP is always fixed when prices change"], "a": 2, "f": ["The trade effect works through relative prices, not government subsidies.", "Lower UK prices reduce demand for some imports, but never eliminate imports completely.", "Lower UK prices make UK products better value abroad, increasing export demand and cutting imports.", "Moving down along AD increases real output demanded; real GDP is not fixed."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a cut in UK income tax shifts the aggregate demand curve.", "given": ["A cut in income tax leaves UK households with higher disposable income at every price level.", "Households spend a large share of this extra income, so consumption ($C$) rises."], "q": "Which next linked point completes the explanation using the AD diagram?", "o": ["Because taxes fell, there is a downward movement along the existing AD curve", "The AD curve shifts leftwards because government tax revenue has fallen", "Only the trade balance changes, leaving the AD curve in the same position", "Higher $C$ at every price level shifts the whole AD curve to the right"], "a": 3, "f": ["Tax cuts are a non-price factor, so they shift the whole AD curve rather than moving along it.", "Lower tax receipts affect the budget balance, while higher household spending shifts AD rightwards.", "Income tax cuts act primarily through household consumption (C), directly shifting AD.", "A rise in C not caused by the price level is a non-price change, so AD shifts rightwards."]}, "trap": "Students often shift AD rightwards when the price level falls. Remember: price level is on the axis, so price changes move along AD; only changes in C, I, G or (X−M) shift it.", "recap": "Price-level changes cause movements along AD, whereas independent changes in $C$, $I$, $G$ or $(X-M)$ shift the AD curve."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.2", "kind": "writing", "why": "Edexcel Paper 2 questions can ask you to analyse and evaluate how interest rates or wealth affect UK consumption, and the higher-mark parts reward a supported judgement.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Disposable income and MPC", "b": "The main driver of consumption is **disposable income** (gross income minus direct taxes plus cash benefits). The **marginal propensity to consume** ($MPC=\\frac{\\Delta C}{\\Delta Y}$) shows the share of extra income spent. The **saving ratio** is the percentage of disposable income saved."}, {"h": "Interest rates and credit", "b": "Higher **interest rates** raise the **cost of borrowing** on credit cards, loans and mortgages, while making saving more rewarding. Rising mortgage repayments cut households' **discretionary income**, reducing spending on non-essential goods and shifting AD leftwards."}, {"h": "Wealth and consumer confidence", "b": "Rising house or share prices create a positive **wealth effect**, encouraging households to spend more or borrow against home equity. Low **consumer confidence**, by contrast, triggers **precautionary saving** as households prepare for possible job losses."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate the likely effect of a rise in UK Bank Rate from 3% to 5% on UK household consumption.", "steps": [{"do": "Consumption is household spending on goods and services, and a rise in Bank Rate normally pushes up mortgage, loan and savings rates across UK banks.", "why": "Earns Knowledge marks by defining key terms and linking Bank Rate to retail rates."}, {"do": "A 2 percentage point rise, from 3% to 5%, raises variable-rate mortgage repayments and cuts households' discretionary income, while dearer credit and higher savings returns raise the opportunity cost of spending, reducing $C$ and shifting AD leftwards.", "why": "Earns Application marks by using the figures in the question, and Analysis marks through a clear chain of reasoning on borrowing and saving."}, {"do": "Higher mortgage costs also cool UK house price growth, creating a negative wealth effect that makes homeowners feel less well off and delay big-ticket purchases.", "why": "Adds a second analytical channel (wealth effect) needed for top-level Edexcel analysis."}, {"do": "However, the impact may be slow or limited because many UK homeowners hold fixed-rate mortgages, net savers gain extra interest income, and strong consumer confidence could keep spending high.", "why": "Earns Evaluation marks by weighing timing lags and counter-effects on different household groups."}, {"do": "On balance, consumption is likely to fall, but gradually rather than at once, because the hit grows as fixed-rate deals expire; how far it falls depends mainly on how indebted households are and how confident they remain.", "why": "Earns top-level Evaluation by reaching a supported judgement on which effects dominate and what the outcome depends on."}], "answer": "A rise in Bank Rate from 3% to 5% is likely to cut UK consumption through dearer credit, lower discretionary income for borrowers and a weaker housing wealth effect, shifting AD left. Yet fixed-rate mortgages delay the hit, net savers gain income, and high confidence may soften the fall. On balance consumption falls gradually, by an amount that depends on household debt levels and confidence."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 4 bring in fixed-rate mortgages and net savers?", "o": ["To prove that higher interest rates always increase total UK consumption", "To define the formula for the marginal propensity to consume", "To show that interest rates only affect firms and never households", "To evaluate why the fall in consumption might be delayed or partly offset"], "a": 3, "f": ["Net savers' gains usually only dampen the fall; they do not prove total consumption rises.", "Step 4 is evaluating the size of the effect, not giving the MPC definition.", "Step 2 already showed how higher rates directly affect household borrowing and saving.", "Edexcel evaluation rewards showing why an effect depends on timing lags and uneven impacts across groups."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate how a sustained rise in UK house prices affects household consumption.", "given": ["Higher house prices increase homeowners' housing wealth, creating a positive wealth effect.", "Homeowners feel more financially secure and may borrow against rising equity, boosting consumption and shifting AD rightwards."], "q": "Which next sentence earns strong Edexcel evaluation marks?", "o": ["Therefore, house prices are always the same thing as monthly disposable income", "However, would-be buyers need bigger deposits, and many owners treat gains as paper wealth", "In addition, higher house prices mean every UK household stops saving altogether", "Consequently, higher house prices reduce consumption by cutting the MPC to zero"], "a": 1, "f": ["Housing wealth is a stock, not an income flow like disposable income, so this contains a conceptual error.", "This weighs losers (would-be first-time buyers) against winners and questions whether owners spend unrealised gains, qualifying the wealth effect.", "Rising house prices may lower the saving ratio, but it is false that every household stops saving, and the sentence does not weigh the argument.", "A positive wealth effect raises rather than cuts consumption and never pushes MPC to zero."]}, "trap": "Students often treat wealth and income as identical, or forget to evaluate who gains and loses. Distinguish flows from stocks, and always weigh lags and distributional effects.", "recap": "Consumption is driven by disposable income, interest rates, confidence and wealth, with its size shaped by MPC and household circumstances."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.3", "kind": "writing", "why": "Edexcel essays regularly ask you to evaluate how interest rates, business confidence or corporation tax affect UK business investment.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Gross and net investment", "b": "**Gross investment** is total spending by firms on new capital goods before accounting for wear and tear (**depreciation**). **Net investment** equals gross investment minus depreciation. Only positive net investment increases the capital stock and long-run capacity."}, {"h": "Growth and animal spirits", "b": "A higher **rate of economic growth** boosts expected sales and encourages firms to expand capacity. Keynes stressed **animal spirits**: business confidence and instincts under uncertainty. Higher **retained profit** gives firms internal funds for projects."}, {"h": "Interest rates, credit and tax", "b": "Higher interest rates raise borrowing costs and the **opportunity cost** of using retained profits instead of earning interest. Poor **access to credit** blocks loans regardless of rates, while lower corporation tax leaves firms more post-tax profit to invest."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate whether a cut in UK interest rates will significantly increase business investment.", "steps": [{"do": "Investment is firms' spending on capital goods. Lower interest rates cut the cost of bank loans and reduce the opportunity cost of using retained profits instead of saving them.", "why": "Earns Knowledge marks by defining investment and identifying both interest-rate channels."}, {"do": "For a small UK toy shop expanding its stockroom or a carmaker buying robotics, lower repayments make more projects profitable, lifting gross investment and shifting AD rightwards.", "why": "Earns Application and Analysis marks by linking cheaper finance to firm projects and AD."}, {"do": "If the extra spending exceeds depreciation, positive net investment enlarges the UK capital stock, shifting LRAS rightwards over time and supporting non-inflationary growth.", "why": "Develops a second analytical chain connecting net investment to long-run capacity."}, {"do": "However, investment may barely rise if animal spirits are weak in a downturn, firms already have spare capacity, or risk-averse banks restrict access to credit.", "why": "Earns Evaluation marks by weighing constraints that can outweigh cheaper borrowing."}, {"do": "On balance, a rate cut is unlikely to raise investment significantly on its own, because firms invest mainly when they expect demand to grow; the effect will be larger if confidence and demand are already recovering, banks are willing to lend, and projects are largely debt-financed.", "why": "Earns top-level Evaluation by reaching a supported judgement on the size of the effect and what it depends on."}], "answer": "Lower interest rates cut borrowing costs and the opportunity cost of retained profits, which can boost investment, AD and, through positive net investment, LRAS. Yet weak animal spirits, spare capacity or tight bank credit can stop firms investing even when loans are cheap. On balance the rise is unlikely to be significant on its own unless confidence and demand are recovering and banks are willing to lend."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 4 refer to animal spirits and spare capacity?", "o": ["To calculate the exact level of capital depreciation in the UK", "To argue that higher interest rates always raise business confidence", "To explain why firms might still delay investment despite cheaper borrowing", "To show that investment is excluded from the AD equation"], "a": 2, "f": ["Depreciation measures wear and tear on capital, not confidence or spare capacity.", "Higher interest rates raise costs and usually dampen rather than boost business confidence.", "If demand expectations are bleak or machines sit idle, cheaper loans alone rarely trigger new capital spending.", "Investment ($I$) is a core component of AD, already linked in Step 2."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate how a cut in UK corporation tax affects business investment.", "given": ["A cut in corporation tax lets UK firms keep a larger share of their profits.", "Higher post-tax retained profits provide cheaper internal finance and raise expected returns, encouraging higher investment."], "q": "Which next sentence earns strong Edexcel evaluation marks?", "o": ["However, firms may pay extra profits as dividends or save them if demand is weak", "Therefore, every pound of tax cut becomes net investment with zero depreciation", "In addition, lower corporation tax is the same thing as a fall in Bank Rate", "Consequently, investment falls because firms only invest when tax rates are high"], "a": 0, "f": ["This questions whether firms actually spend retained profits on capital when confidence or demand is low.", "Firms rarely invest all of a tax saving, and depreciation still reduces gross to net investment.", "Corporation tax is fiscal policy set by HM Treasury, whereas Bank Rate is monetary policy.", "Higher taxes reduce post-tax returns and retained funds, discouraging rather than encouraging investment."]}, "trap": "Students often call buying shares investment, or assume lower interest rates guarantee more capital spending. Stick to physical capital goods and always evaluate confidence and credit.", "recap": "Only positive net investment expands the capital stock, and business spending depends on confidence, growth and credit as much as interest rates."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 regularly asks you to explain how the UK trade cycle and fiscal policy alter government spending, AD and the budget balance.", "hook": {"q": "The UK enters a recession, and the Chancellor makes no new tax or spending announcements. What happens automatically to welfare outlays and tax receipts?", "o": ["Welfare outlays fall and tax receipts rise", "Both welfare outlays and tax receipts stay unchanged", "Both welfare outlays and tax receipts rise together", "Welfare outlays rise and tax receipts fall"], "a": 3, "f": ["That pattern occurs during an economic boom, not during a recession.", "Even without new policies, existing benefit rules and tax bands react automatically to GDP.", "Falling incomes and retail sales reduce income tax and VAT receipts during a downturn.", "More job losses raise benefit claims while lower incomes and spending cut tax receipts automatically."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What counts as G", "b": "**Government expenditure** ($G$) covers current spending on public services (such as NHS staff wages) and **capital spending** on infrastructure (such as schools and hospitals). **Transfer payments** (pensions, welfare benefits) are excluded from $G$ because no good or service is produced in exchange."}, {"h": "The trade cycle", "b": "As real GDP fluctuates across the **trade cycle**, **automatic stabilisers** cushion the economy without new laws. In a recession, welfare spending rises and tax receipts fall, widening the **fiscal deficit** and limiting the fall in AD."}, {"h": "Discretionary fiscal policy", "b": "Governments also use deliberate **fiscal policy** in the Budget. **Expansionary fiscal policy** raises $G$ or cuts taxes to shift AD rightwards and reduce cyclical unemployment. **Contractionary fiscal policy** cuts $G$ or raises taxes to shift AD leftwards and curb inflation or borrowing."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how the UK trade cycle automatically affects government spending and the fiscal balance during an economic boom.", "steps": [{"do": "Define the trade cycle as fluctuations in real GDP around trend growth, and automatic stabilisers as built-in tax and spending changes that operate without new legislation.", "why": "Clear definitions distinguish automatic trade-cycle effects from discretionary policy."}, {"do": "During a boom, firms hire more staff and unemployment falls, so fewer households claim unemployment-related benefits and total welfare spending drops automatically.", "why": "Links higher employment directly to lower government transfer outlays."}, {"do": "At the same time, higher wages, company profits and retail sales automatically boost receipts from income tax, corporation tax and VAT.", "why": "Shows the revenue side of automatic stabilisers during rapid growth."}, {"do": "Lower benefit outlays and higher tax revenues shrink the fiscal deficit (or create a surplus), dampening AD growth and helping to contain inflation.", "why": "Connects spending and tax changes back to the fiscal balance and stabilisation."}], "answer": "In a boom, automatic stabilisers reduce welfare spending as unemployment falls and raise income tax, corporation tax and VAT receipts as incomes and spending grow. Together these shrink the fiscal deficit (or build a surplus) and cool AD."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 1 specify 'without new legislation' when defining automatic stabilisers?", "o": ["Because Parliament is banned from changing taxes during a boom", "To separate built-in trade-cycle effects from discretionary fiscal policy", "Because automatic stabilisers only apply to private firms", "To show that transfer payments are included inside $G$"], "a": 1, "f": ["Governments can still change taxes in a boom; that would simply be discretionary fiscal policy.", "Automatic stabilisers work under existing rules, whereas discretionary policy requires deliberate new decisions.", "Automatic stabilisers are features of the government's own tax and welfare system.", "Transfer payments are still excluded from G in AD even when they act as automatic stabilisers."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how discretionary expansionary fiscal policy during a UK recession affects aggregate demand and the fiscal balance.", "given": ["In a recession, the government deliberately increases capital spending on new NHS hospitals and school buildings.", "Because capital spending is part of $G$, total government expenditure rises at every price level."], "q": "Which next linked point completes the explanation accurately?", "o": ["AD shifts leftwards because higher public spending instantly removes private jobs", "There is a movement down along AD and the fiscal balance moves into surplus", "AD shifts rightwards, boosting output and jobs, though the fiscal deficit widens initially", "The policy counts as an automatic stabiliser so the budget balance is unaffected"], "a": 2, "f": ["Higher G is an injection that shifts AD rightwards rather than leftwards.", "A deliberate rise in G shifts the AD curve rather than moving along it, and increases borrowing.", "Higher G shifts AD rightwards to support real output, while extra borrowing widens the deficit initially.", "Announcing new hospital construction is discretionary fiscal policy and directly raises public spending."]}, "trap": "Students often count welfare benefits inside $G$ or mix up fiscal deficits (annual flow) with national debt (total stock). Check definitions before analysing.", "recap": "Government spending reacts automatically across the trade cycle via stabilisers and deliberately through discretionary fiscal policy."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.5", "kind": "writing", "why": "Edexcel Paper 2 regularly asks you to evaluate how exchange rate shifts, global slowdowns or tariffs affect UK net trade and AD.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Incomes and the world economy", "b": "**Net trade** is export revenue minus import spending $(X-M)$. Faster UK real income growth lifts imports if the **marginal propensity to import** is high, worsening $(X-M)$. Strong growth in the **world economy** boosts overseas demand for UK exports."}, {"h": "Exchange rates and SPICED", "b": "The **exchange rate** is the price of one currency in terms of another. An **appreciation** (stronger pound) makes imports cheaper and exports dearer (SPICED), tending to worsen $(X-M)$. A depreciation makes UK exports cheaper abroad and imports pricier."}, {"h": "Protectionism and non-price factors", "b": "**Protectionism** uses **tariffs** or quotas to restrict imports and shield domestic firms, though retaliation can cut UK exports. **Non-price factors** such as quality, design, reliability and marketing also shape export demand."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate the likely effect of a 10% depreciation of the pound (£) on UK net trade $(X-M)$.", "steps": [{"do": "A depreciation is a fall in sterling's value against other currencies, making UK exports cheaper in foreign currency and imports dearer in pounds (the reverse of SPICED).", "why": "Earns Knowledge marks by defining depreciation and stating both price effects accurately."}, {"do": "A 10% fall makes a UK export about 10% cheaper in foreign currency and a foreign good about 11% dearer in pounds, so overseas orders rise ($X$ up) and UK buyers switch to domestic rivals (import volumes down).", "why": "Earns Application marks by using the 10% figure, and Analysis marks by tracing how relative prices alter export and import volumes."}, {"do": "If volumes respond enough, export revenue rises and import spending falls, so net trade $(X-M)$ improves, shifting the UK AD curve to the right.", "why": "Links the trade response directly to the AD diagram for full analysis."}, {"do": "However, in the short run, existing contracts and scarce UK substitutes make $X$ and $M$ price-inelastic, so higher import bills can worsen $(X-M)$ before volumes adjust later.", "why": "Earns Evaluation marks by applying price elasticity of demand and short-run time lags."}, {"do": "On balance, net trade is likely to worsen briefly and then improve (the J-curve), because demand becomes more price-elastic as contracts end; the gain needs export and import price elasticities together to exceed one (the Marshall-Lerner condition), and its size depends on UK non-price competitiveness and how much imported content UK exports contain.", "why": "Earns top-level Evaluation by reaching a supported judgement on timing and on what the outcome depends on."}], "answer": "A 10% depreciation makes UK exports about 10% cheaper abroad and imports about 11% dearer in pounds, tending to raise $X$, cut $M$ and shift AD rightwards. In the short run, inelastic demand can make $(X-M)$ worse first. On balance net trade is likely to improve after a lag, provided demand becomes elastic enough, by an amount that depends on non-price competitiveness and imported inputs."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 4 argue that net trade can worsen in the short run after a depreciation?", "o": ["Inelastic demand means higher import prices raise import bills before volumes adjust", "A weaker pound makes UK exports more expensive in foreign currency", "Tariffs automatically rise whenever a currency depreciates", "Foreign incomes always fall whenever sterling weakens"], "a": 0, "f": ["When buyers cannot switch quickly, paying more pounds per import lifts $M$ expenditure while $X$ barely rises.", "A weaker pound makes UK exports cheaper, not dearer, in foreign currency.", "Exchange rate shifts do not automatically trigger government tariffs.", "A fall in sterling does not automatically reduce real incomes in overseas economies."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate how a sharp recession in the Eurozone affects UK net trade $(X-M)$.", "given": ["The Eurozone is a major export market for UK goods and services.", "Falling European real incomes reduce overseas demand for UK exports, worsening $(X-M)$ and shifting UK AD leftwards."], "q": "Which next sentence earns strong Edexcel evaluation marks?", "o": ["Therefore, UK imports from the Eurozone immediately fall to zero at the same time", "In addition, a Eurozone recession is defined as an appreciation of the UK pound", "Consequently, UK net trade improves because lower exports increase $(X-M)$", "However, the hit shrinks if UK firms sell income-inelastic goods or diversify markets"], "a": 3, "f": ["UK imports depend on UK real incomes and prices, and never fall to zero.", "A Eurozone recession is a fall in European real GDP, not an exchange rate definition.", "Lower exports reduce rather than improve net trade $(X-M)$.", "This weighs income elasticity and market diversification to judge how severe the export fall will be."]}, "trap": "Students often assume a weaker pound guarantees a trade surplus or ignore retaliation against tariffs. Always check price elasticity of demand, time lags and non-price quality.", "recap": "Net trade responds to UK and world incomes, sterling's exchange rate, tariffs and non-price quality, but elasticity and lags shape the outcome."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.3.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "AS diagrams appear in 9EC0 multiple-choice, short-answer and essay questions; you must say whether a change is a movement or a shift, and whether it is short run or long run.", "hook": {"q": "Energy costs rise sharply for firms across the whole economy. At any given price level, what happens to the total output firms are willing to supply?", "o": ["It rises, because firms charge more", "It stays the same, because demand is unchanged", "It falls, because profit per unit is squeezed", "It rises, because workers are paid more"], "a": 2, "f": ["Firms may charge more, but at the same price level their costs are higher, so supplying is less profitable.", "Supply depends on costs and prices, not just demand; higher costs change what firms will supply.", "Right. Higher costs reduce profit at each price level, so firms supply less. That is a shift of SRAS to the left.", "Higher pay is a cost; it reduces the output firms will supply at each price level."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The SRAS curve", "b": "**Short-run aggregate supply (SRAS)** shows the total output firms will supply at each **price level**, when some costs, such as money wages, are fixed. It slopes upwards: higher prices with sticky costs raise profit, so firms supply more."}, {"h": "Movement or shift?", "b": "A change in the **price level** causes a **movement along** SRAS, usually after an AD shift. Anything else that changes firms' costs **shifts** SRAS: wages, raw material and energy prices, taxes, subsidies, exchange rate. Higher costs shift it left; lower costs, right."}, {"h": "Long-run AS", "b": "In the long run all factors can change. **LRAS** shows **potential output**. It shifts with the quantity or quality of factors, productivity, technology and institutions. Neoclassical LRAS is vertical. The Keynesian AS curve is L-shaped: output can rise with little price change when there is spare capacity."}, {"h": "Temporary or permanent?", "b": "A cost shock shifts SRAS but need not shift LRAS. In October 2026 the OBR judged the 2026 energy shock would not permanently lower UK potential output. In the year to October 2026, ONS provisional data showed producer input prices up 10.8%."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a rise in firms' energy costs shifts SRAS to the left but need not shift LRAS.", "steps": [{"do": "State that energy is a cost of production for firms.", "why": "Costs are a non-price-level determinant of SRAS, so they shift the curve."}, {"do": "Say that at every price level firms now earn less profit per unit, so they supply less output.", "why": "This explains the direction: SRAS shifts left, not a movement along it."}, {"do": "Link to the outcome: with AD unchanged, the price level rises and real GDP falls.", "why": "Shows the macroeconomic consequence: cost-push inflation and lower output."}, {"do": "Explain that LRAS depends on capacity and productivity, which the energy price rise leaves unchanged if it is temporary.", "why": "Separates a short-run cost shock from a change in potential output."}], "answer": "Energy is a cost of production, so higher prices cut profit at every price level and firms supply less: SRAS shifts left. With AD unchanged, prices rise and output falls. LRAS depends on capacity and productivity, so it stays put if the shock is temporary."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say SRAS shifts rather than saying there is a movement along it?", "o": ["Because energy prices are a cost factor, not a change in the general price level", "Because SRAS only ever moves when AD changes", "Because movements along SRAS only happen in the long run", "Because a left shift always lowers potential output"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Only a change in the price level causes a movement along SRAS; a change in a cost factor changes supply at every price level, so the curve shifts.", "AD shifts cause movements along SRAS, not shifts of it. Shifts come from supply-side factors such as costs.", "Movements along SRAS are short-run and caused by the price level; the long run is shown by LRAS.", "Potential output is shown by LRAS. A leftward SRAS shift can be temporary and leave LRAS unchanged."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the widespread adoption of new technology affects aggregate supply.", "given": ["Step 1: New technology raises output per worker, so labour productivity rises.", "Step 2: Firms can produce more with the same inputs, so unit costs fall at every price level."], "q": "What is the next step in the answer?", "o": ["SRAS shifts right, and LRAS also shifts right as potential output rises", "There is a movement up along SRAS because output has risen", "SRAS shifts right but LRAS is unchanged because technology is short run", "SRAS shifts left because firms need to buy new equipment"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Lower unit costs shift SRAS right, and higher productivity raises productive capacity, shifting LRAS right too.", "Output rising does not mean a movement along; the cause is lower costs, which shift the curve.", "Technology that lasts raises productive capacity, so it shifts LRAS as well as SRAS.", "Buying equipment is a one-off cost; the main effect of better technology is lower unit costs and higher capacity."]}, "trap": "Do not treat a rise in the price level as a shift of SRAS. Ask what caused the change: price level means a movement; costs or capacity mean a shift.", "recap": "Price level changes move you along SRAS; cost changes shift SRAS; changes in capacity or productivity shift LRAS."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.3.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Short-run AS shifts appear in AD/AS diagram questions and 'explain' or 'analyse' answers on cost-push inflation, exchange rates and tax policy.", "hook": {"q": "A UK bakery's energy bills double. At each price it can sell its bread for, how much will it now be willing to supply?", "o": ["More, because it needs extra revenue", "Less, because its costs have risen", "The same, because prices have not changed", "It depends only on consumer demand"], "a": 1, "f": ["Higher costs do not raise willingness to supply; profit per loaf falls at each price.", "Right. Higher costs cut profit at each price, so firms supply less at every price. That is a leftward SRAS shift.", "Prices unchanged does not mean supply unchanged; costs matter for supply at each price.", "Demand affects AD; supply depends on costs and profit."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What SRAS shows", "b": "**Short-run aggregate supply (SRAS)** shows the real output firms plan to produce at each **price level** in the short run. A change in costs of production changes output at every price level. So the **whole curve shifts**."}, {"h": "Raw materials and energy", "b": "Raw materials and energy are **costs of production**. A rise shifts SRAS **left**; a fall shifts it **right**. In the year to October 2026, ONS provisional figures showed manufacturers' crude oil input prices surged 88.4%. That is a **cost-push** pressure."}, {"h": "Exchange rates", "b": "Many firms buy **imported inputs** priced in foreign currency. A **depreciation** of sterling makes them dearer in pounds, so SRAS shifts left. An **appreciation** makes them cheaper, so SRAS shifts right. Exports and AD are a separate effect."}, {"h": "Taxes on firms", "b": "Taxes on firms, such as **employer National Insurance**, corporation tax and indirect taxes, raise **unit costs**. SRAS shifts left. Cuts in these, or **subsidies**, shift it right. From April 2025 employer NI rose from 13.8% to 15%."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a rise in energy prices affects the price level and real output in the short run.", "steps": [{"do": "Say energy is a cost of production for most firms.", "why": "Links the event to firms' costs, the route to supply."}, {"do": "State that unit costs rise at every price level, so firms supply less at each price.", "why": "Shows why the curve itself moves, not a point along it."}, {"do": "Say SRAS shifts left, for example from SRAS1 to SRAS2, with AD unchanged.", "why": "Names the correct shift and the curves involved."}, {"do": "Say the equilibrium price level rises and real GDP falls.", "why": "Follows the shift through to the new equilibrium: cost-push inflation."}], "answer": "Energy is a cost of production, so unit costs rise at every price level. SRAS shifts left, with AD unchanged. The equilibrium price level rises and real output falls, which is cost-push inflation."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say SRAS shifts left rather than showing a move along SRAS?", "o": ["Because costs changed at every price level, not the price level itself", "Because the price level rises, which always shifts SRAS", "Because AD is unchanged, so the SRAS curve must move", "Because energy is an import, and imports shift SRAS"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. A cost change alters output at every price level, so the whole curve shifts. Only a price level change moves along it.", "A price level change is a movement along SRAS, not a shift.", "AD being fixed is irrelevant to why SRAS shifts; the cause is costs.", "Energy need not be imported; the shift comes from higher costs, not from where it is sourced."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a fall in the world price of a raw material used by UK manufacturers affects the price level and real output.", "given": ["The raw material is a cost of production for UK manufacturers.", "Its lower price cuts unit costs at every price level."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["AD shifts right, raising both the price level and real output", "There is a movement down along SRAS to a lower price level", "SRAS shifts left because firms now earn higher profits", "SRAS shifts right; with AD unchanged the price level falls and real GDP rises"], "a": 3, "f": ["Input costs are a supply factor; they shift SRAS, not AD.", "Confuses a movement with a shift: the cost change moves the whole curve.", "Lower costs raise supply at each price, so the shift is right, not left.", "Right. Lower costs shift SRAS right, giving a lower equilibrium price level and higher real GDP."]}, "trap": "Drawing a cost change as a movement along SRAS. Ask whether costs or the price level changed first. Costs shift the whole curve; the price level is the result.", "recap": "Higher costs of raw materials, energy, imports (weaker £) or business taxes shift SRAS left; lower costs shift it right."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.3.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "LRAS appears in AD/AS diagrams, 'explain' questions and evaluations of supply-side policy, so you need to know both models and what shifts the curve.", "hook": {"q": "A toy shop already has every till, shelf and worker fully used. Customers double. What can the shop do in the long run to sell more?", "o": ["Nothing; demand alone raises capacity", "Expand: more staff, more space, better systems", "Raise prices and expect output to rise", "Hire fewer workers"], "a": 1, "f": ["Demand alone does not create capacity; resources must grow.", "Right: long-run output rises only if resources or productivity rise, like LRAS shifting.", "Higher prices do not create extra output at full capacity.", "Fewer workers would reduce capacity."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What LRAS shows", "b": "**Long-run aggregate supply** shows the economy's **potential output** when all resources are fully employed. It is determined by the quantity and quality of **factors of production**, not by the price level."}, {"h": "Classical LRAS", "b": "**Classical** economists say markets clear, so the economy returns to **full employment**. LRAS is **vertical** at that output. A rise in AD then only raises the price level in the long run."}, {"h": "Keynesian LRAS", "b": "**Keynesians** say the economy can be stuck below full employment. The curve is flat with **spare capacity**, rises in the middle as bottlenecks appear, and becomes vertical at full capacity. AD changes move the economy along it."}, {"h": "What shifts LRAS", "b": "LRAS shifts right with more or better factors: **technology**, higher productivity, improved **skills** and education, more capital, and a larger working-age population through births or migration. Falls in these shift it left."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how improved technology could affect an economy's long-run aggregate supply.", "steps": [{"do": "State that better technology raises productivity, so more output is produced per worker and per unit of capital.", "why": "Defines the mechanism, linking technology to productivity."}, {"do": "Explain that this raises the economy's productive capacity, or potential output, at full employment.", "why": "Links productivity to what LRAS measures."}, {"do": "State that LRAS therefore shifts to the right, on both classical (vertical) and Keynesian curves.", "why": "The diagram-based conclusion examiners reward."}, {"do": "Add that the price level can be lower and real GDP higher at the new full employment point, other things equal.", "why": "Shows the macro effect, not just the shift."}], "answer": "Better technology raises productivity, so more output comes from the same resources. This raises potential output, shifting LRAS right. At full employment, real GDP is higher and the price level can be lower, other things equal."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example say LRAS shifts, rather than the economy moving along it?", "o": ["Because technology raises AD more than supply", "Because prices have fallen", "Because capacity itself has changed, not the price level", "Because the curve is vertical"], "a": 2, "f": ["The mechanism is on the supply side, raising capacity, not AD.", "A price fall would cause a movement along a curve, not a shift of LRAS.", "Right: movements along are caused by price level changes; a change in capacity shifts the curve.", "Verticality alone does not decide shift versus movement; the cause does."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how an increase in the working-age population could affect LRAS.", "given": ["Step 1: A larger working-age population raises the quantity of labour available."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["LRAS shifts left as wages must fall", "AD shifts right because workers always consume more", "The economy moves along LRAS to a higher price level", "Potential output rises, so LRAS shifts right"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wages are not the issue; more labour raises capacity.", "Consumption matters for AD, but the question is about capacity.", "A movement along the curve would need a price level change.", "Right: more labour (a factor) raises potential output, so LRAS shifts right, assuming workers find jobs."]}, "trap": "Do not treat a rise in AD as shifting LRAS. AD changes only move the economy along a Keynesian LRAS; only supply-side changes in capacity shift it.", "recap": "LRAS shows potential output and shifts only when the quantity or quality of factors of production changes."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.4.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Circular flow and the stock–flow distinction underpin Theme 2. They appear in definition and explain questions and support later analysis of AD and growth.", "hook": {"q": "Your bank balance is £2,000 and you earn £300 a week. Which of these is income?", "o": ["The £2,000", "The £300 a week", "Both are income", "Neither is income"], "a": 1, "f": ["£2,000 is a stock at one moment: wealth, not income.", "Correct: income is earned over a period of time, a flow.", "Only the weekly earnings are income; the balance is accumulated wealth.", "The weekly £300 is income, so this is wrong."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Income and wealth", "b": "**Income** is a **flow**: earnings over a period, such as a year. **Wealth** is a **stock**: the value of assets less liabilities at one point in time. Saving turns income into wealth."}, {"h": "The basic circular flow", "b": "Households supply factors of production to firms and receive **income**. Firms produce goods and services, and households spend on them. Income, output and expenditure are equal in value, so the flow goes round continuously."}, {"h": "Injections and withdrawals", "b": "**Withdrawals** (W) are saving, taxation and imports. **Injections** (J) are investment, government spending and exports. If J > W, national income rises. If J < W, it falls. It is stable when J = W."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a rise in household saving may reduce national income in the circular flow model.", "steps": [{"do": "Saving is a withdrawal from the circular flow.", "why": "It is income not spent on domestic output."}, {"do": "Withdrawals now exceed injections, if investment, government spending and exports are unchanged.", "why": "J < W means less spending returns to firms."}, {"do": "Firms' revenue falls, so they cut output and incomes paid to households.", "why": "Income and output are linked in the flow."}, {"do": "National income falls until W = J again.", "why": "The flow only settles when withdrawals equal injections."}], "answer": "Saving is a withdrawal, so W exceeds J. Less spending reaches firms, so output and incomes fall. National income falls until withdrawals equal injections again."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say injections are 'unchanged'?", "o": ["Because saving cannot affect injections", "Because only a change in W should be isolated", "Because injections are always zero", "Because saving and investment are the same thing"], "a": 1, "f": ["Saving can affect investment, so it is not impossible; we simply hold it constant.", "Correct: ceteris paribus lets us show the effect of the rise in W alone.", "Injections are not zero; they are simply held constant here.", "They are different flows; assuming they are the same hides the effect."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a rise in UK exports may increase national income.", "given": ["Exports are an injection into the circular flow."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Injections now exceed withdrawals, so spending on UK output rises", "Withdrawals now exceed injections, so income falls", "Wealth rises, so income rises", "Imports rise by the same amount, so nothing changes"], "a": 0, "f": ["Correct: J > W means more spending reaches UK firms, raising output and income.", "Exports are not withdrawals; this reverses the effect.", "Exports raise a flow (spending), not a stock; this confuses wealth and income.", "Nothing in the question says imports rise; this assumes an unstated change."]}, "trap": "Do not confuse wealth with income, or assume saving is automatically re-spent. Saving is a withdrawal. Wealth is the stock built up from past saving.", "recap": "Income is a flow, wealth a stock; national income rises when injections exceed withdrawals and falls when withdrawals exceed injections."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.4.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Injections and withdrawals underpin the circular flow, national income equilibrium and the multiplier, so they appear in short-answer and essay questions on growth and demand.", "hook": {"q": "A household puts £100 of its income into a savings account instead of spending it on UK-made goods. What happens to the flow of spending on UK firms, other things equal?", "o": ["It is unchanged, as the £100 still exists", "It falls, as the £100 is not passed on to firms as spending", "It rises, as banks now hold more money", "It rises, as saving is a form of investment"], "a": 1, "f": ["The money exists, but it is not spent on firms' output, so the flow is smaller.", "Right: saving takes income out of the flow, so it is a withdrawal.", "Banks holding funds does not mean more spending on goods straight away.", "Saving and investment are different; investment is spending by firms on capital."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Injections", "b": "An **injection** adds spending to the circular flow of income from outside households' consumption of domestic output. There are three: **investment** (I) by firms, **government spending** (G) and **exports** (X), which are spending by overseas buyers on UK output."}, {"h": "Withdrawals", "b": "A **withdrawal** (leakage) removes income from the flow, so it is not spent on domestic output. There are three: **saving** (S), **taxation** (T) and **imports** (M), which is spending that goes to overseas firms. For example, the UK household saving ratio was 8.8% in Q2 2026 (ONS)."}, {"h": "Equilibrium condition", "b": "National income is in **equilibrium** when planned injections equal planned withdrawals: I + G + X = S + T + M. At that point the flow is neither expanding nor shrinking, so **national income** is stable."}, {"h": "Adjustment to equilibrium", "b": "If **injections exceed withdrawals**, spending rises, firms produce more and income grows. Higher income raises S, T and M until W = J again. If **withdrawals exceed injections**, income falls until the two are equal at a lower level."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a rise in household saving could change national income in the circular flow.", "steps": [{"do": "Identify saving as a withdrawal: income households do not spend on domestic output leaves the circular flow.", "why": "Classifying S correctly is the knowledge mark."}, {"do": "State that, with injections unchanged, withdrawals now exceed injections (W > J).", "why": "Equilibrium needs J = W, so you must compare the two."}, {"do": "Explain that firms' sales and output fall, so incomes fall.", "why": "This links the imbalance to the flow of income."}, {"do": "Conclude that lower income reduces S, T and M until W = J at a lower national income.", "why": "This shows how the new equilibrium is reached."}], "answer": "Saving is a withdrawal, so a rise in saving makes withdrawals exceed injections. Spending on firms' output falls, so firms produce less and incomes fall. As income falls, saving, taxation and imports fall too, until withdrawals equal injections again, at a lower level of national income."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say income falls until withdrawals equal injections, rather than falling for ever?", "o": ["Because injections rise automatically whenever income falls", "Because lower income reduces S, T and M, so W falls back to J", "Because the government always raises spending when saving rises", "Because saving stops being a withdrawal at low incomes"], "a": 1, "f": ["Planned injections are assumed unchanged here; they do not rise automatically.", "Right: withdrawals depend on income, so as income falls W falls until it equals J.", "No such government action is assumed; the adjustment comes from the withdrawals themselves.", "Saving is always a withdrawal; it is the amount that changes with income."]}, "faded": {"task": "Start from equilibrium. Exports rise by £8bn; other injections and all withdrawal schedules are unchanged. Explain the effect on national income.", "given": ["Step 1: Exports are an injection, so total injections rise by £8bn.", "Step 2: Injections now exceed withdrawals (J > W)."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["National income falls, as more exports raise imports immediately", "National income is unchanged, because exports are only one injection", "National income rises, which raises S, T and M until W = J again", "National income rises permanently, as withdrawals cannot respond"], "a": 2, "f": ["Exports are an injection; with J > W income rises, not falls.", "The change in any one injection matters; equilibrium is disturbed when J ≠ W.", "Right: spending and income rise, and higher income lifts withdrawals until they equal the higher injections.", "Withdrawals rise with income, which is what stops the expansion."]}, "trap": "Mixing up saving and investment, or listing imports as an injection. Learn S, T, M as withdrawals and I, G, X as injections, then always compare totals J and W.", "recap": "National income rises when injections (I, G, X) exceed withdrawals (S, T, M), falls when they are smaller, and is in equilibrium when J = W."}
{"why": "AD/AS diagrams appear in most macro essays and data-response answers, where you must explain what a shock does to output and the price level.", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.3", "kind": "idea", "hook": {"q": "Firms face a sharp rise in energy costs but demand has not changed. What do you expect to happen to prices and output?", "o": ["Prices rise and output falls", "Prices and output both rise", "Prices fall and output rises", "Prices rise and output is unaffected"], "a": 0, "f": ["Higher costs make firms supply less at each price level, so prices go up and output goes down.", "Both rising needs more demand; here only costs have changed, so output should not rise.", "Prices fall and output rises when costs fall, not when they rise.", "Output is affected: firms cut production when costs rise, so it is not unchanged in the short run."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Finding equilibrium", "b": "**Short-run equilibrium** is where **aggregate demand (AD)** meets **short-run aggregate supply (SRAS)**. This fixes real output and the price level. It can be below or above potential output, which LRAS shows."}, {"h": "Shifts in AD", "b": "A rise in AD (higher consumption, investment, government spending or net exports) shifts the curve right. Along an upward-sloping SRAS, **real output** and the **price level** both rise. A fall in AD does the opposite."}, {"h": "Shifts in SRAS", "b": "A rise in **costs of production**, such as energy or wages, shifts **SRAS** left: output falls and the price level rises. Lower costs shift it right: output rises and prices fall. Equilibrium then moves along AD; AD itself does not shift."}, {"h": "Keynesian and classical LRAS", "b": "The **classical LRAS** is vertical at full capacity: AD changes only alter the price level. The **Keynesian LRAS** is horizontal at low output, then rises, then turns vertical. With spare capacity, AD raises output with little price change."}], "example": {"task": "In November 2026 the Bank of England projected energy shocks would push UK CPI inflation to around 5% by mid-2027. Explain the effect on equilibrium output and prices.", "steps": [{"do": "State that higher energy prices raise firms' costs of production.", "why": "Energy is an input to many firms, so the shock is on the supply side."}, {"do": "Say SRAS shifts to the left, with AD unchanged.", "why": "Firms supply less at each price level when costs are higher."}, {"do": "Explain that the new equilibrium is where AD meets the new SRAS, further up the AD curve.", "why": "Equilibrium moves along the unchanged AD curve to a new intersection."}, {"do": "Conclude that the price level rises and real output falls.", "why": "This is cost-push inflation with lower output, as the Bank forecast slower growth."}], "answer": "Higher energy costs shift SRAS left. AD is unchanged, so equilibrium moves up along AD. The price level rises (cost-push inflation) and real output falls, slowing growth."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example show equilibrium moving along AD rather than AD shifting?", "o": ["Because AD is always fixed in the short run", "Because energy costs affect supply, so only SRAS shifts", "Because inflation only ever moves along a demand curve", "Because the Bank of England sets AD directly"], "a": 1, "f": ["AD can shift when its components change; here it simply has no reason to.", "Energy is a cost to firms, so it shifts SRAS. With AD unchanged, equilibrium slides along it.", "Inflation can come from AD shifts as well; this does not explain why AD stays put here.", "The Bank influences AD through policy, but it does not set it, and nothing here shifts it."]}, "faded": {"task": "A fall in firms' wage costs, with AD unchanged and a normal upward-sloping SRAS. Explain the effect on output and prices.", "given": ["Lower wage costs reduce firms' costs of production.", "SRAS shifts to the right; AD stays where it is."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["The new equilibrium is at a higher price level and a lower output", "The new equilibrium is at a lower price level and a higher output", "AD shifts right to match, so only output changes", "LRAS shifts left because costs have fallen"], "a": 1, "f": ["That is the effect of SRAS shifting left, the opposite of this case.", "Equilibrium moves down along AD to meet the new SRAS: the price level falls and real output rises.", "Nothing in the question moves AD; a supply shift does not by itself shift demand.", "Lower costs do not reduce potential output; LRAS would not shift left."]}, "trap": "Do not let a cost shock shift AD. Ask first: does this change demand (C, I, G, X-M) or costs and capacity? Then shift only that curve.", "recap": "Equilibrium output is where AD meets SRAS; AD shifts move output and prices together, SRAS shifts move them in opposite directions."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.4.4", "kind": "method", "why": "Multiplier calculations appear in the multiple-choice and short-answer sections, and the multiplier also supports AD and fiscal policy evaluation in longer answers.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What the multiplier does", "b": "An **injection** (investment, government spending or exports) raises incomes. Some of that income is re-spent on UK goods, which creates more income for others. The final rise in **national income** is larger than the first injection. This is the **multiplier effect**."}, {"h": "Calculating the multiplier", "b": "The **marginal propensity to withdraw** (MPW) is MPS + MPT + MPM. The **multiplier** is $k=\\frac{1}{MPW}$. If all of MPC is spent on UK output, this equals $\\frac{1}{1-MPC}$. Then: change in income = $k$ × injection."}, {"h": "Effect on AD", "b": "The initial injection shifts **aggregate demand** right. The multiplier makes the final shift larger. A higher MPS, MPT or MPM makes the multiplier **smaller**, because more of each extra £ leaves the circular flow."}], "example": {"task": "MPS = 0.2, MPT = 0.1, MPM = 0.1. Firms raise investment by £4bn. Calculate the total change in national income.", "steps": [{"do": "Add the withdrawals: MPW = 0.2 + 0.1 + 0.1 = 0.4.", "why": "Saving, tax and imports all leave the circular flow of income."}, {"do": "Find the multiplier: $k=\\frac{1}{0.4}=2.5$.", "why": "The multiplier is 1 divided by the MPW."}, {"do": "Multiply: change in income = 2.5 × £4bn = £10bn.", "why": "The multiplier scales the initial injection to the final change."}, {"do": "Check: MPC on UK goods = 1 − 0.4 = 0.6, and $\\frac{1}{1-0.6}=2.5$.", "why": "Both formulas must agree, which confirms the working."}], "answer": "National income rises by £10bn (k = 2.5, from MPW = 0.4, applied to the £4bn investment injection)."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example add MPS, MPT and MPM before finding the multiplier?", "o": ["Each is a share of extra income that is not re-spent on UK output", "Each is a share of extra income that is spent on UK goods", "The three must always add up to 1 for the formula to work", "Only the largest withdrawal affects the size of the multiplier"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. All three are withdrawals from the circular flow, so together they set how much is passed on.", "Wrong: these are the leakages. The share spent on UK goods is what remains after withdrawals.", "Wrong: they sum to MPW, which is below 1. Only MPW plus domestic MPC equals 1.", "Wrong: every withdrawal matters. Using only the biggest one overstates the multiplier."]}, "faded": {"task": "MPS = 0.1, MPT = 0.1 and MPM = 0.05. Government spending rises by £3bn. Calculate the total change in national income.", "given": ["MPW = 0.1 + 0.1 + 0.05 = 0.25"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["k = 0.25 × 3 = 0.75", "k = 1/0.75 ≈ 1.33", "k = 1 − 0.25 = 0.75", "k = 1/0.25 = 4"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong: this multiplies MPW by the injection. The multiplier is 1 divided by MPW, and you apply it afterwards.", "Wrong: 0.75 is the share not withdrawn. Dividing 1 by it gives the wrong multiplier.", "Wrong: 1 − MPW is not the multiplier. You must take 1 divided by MPW.", "Right. k = 1/MPW = 4. Then multiply by £3bn to get a £12bn rise in national income."]}, "trap": "Leaving out one withdrawal, usually MPM or MPT, or stopping at k. Add all three withdrawals, invert the total, then multiply by the injection.", "recap": "Multiplier = 1/(MPS + MPT + MPM); multiply it by the injection to find the total change in national income."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.5.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions on growth often ask you to distinguish actual from potential growth, and to explain causes using AD/AS or the PPF. Doing so accurately earns knowledge and analysis marks.", "hook": {"q": "A shop owner finds customers buying more toys but cannot stock more shelves. Which idea does this match best?", "o": ["Demand rose but capacity limits output", "Productive capacity has risen", "Only prices matter", "Supply and demand both fell"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: more demand can raise output only while spare capacity exists. Growth in capacity is a separate thing.", "Capacity has not risen; the shelves are the same, so this misreads the example.", "Output and capacity matter as well as prices.", "Demand rose in the example, so it did not fall."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Actual growth", "b": "**Actual growth** is an increase in real GDP over time. It is shown by a move towards the **PPF**, or by AD shifting right along the SRAS curve. It can come from higher consumption, investment, government spending or net exports."}, {"h": "Potential growth", "b": "**Potential growth** is an increase in the economy's productive capacity. It is shown by an outward shift of the **PPF** or LRAS. Causes include more or better factors of production: investment in capital, education, technology and migration."}, {"h": "Output gap link", "b": "If actual growth is faster than potential growth, a negative **output gap** narrows. In July 2026 the Bank of England estimated UK potential supply growth at around 0.3% to 0.4% per quarter."}, {"h": "Export-led growth", "b": "**Export-led growth** occurs when rising exports raise AD, since AD includes (X − M). It can also raise investment and productivity. Risks are **overdependence** on foreign demand and exchange rate rises that make exports less competitive."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how investment in new machinery could cause both actual and potential growth.", "steps": [{"do": "Investment is a component of AD, so spending on machinery shifts AD to the right.", "why": "This links the cause to short-run actual growth."}, {"do": "Higher AD raises real GDP if there is spare capacity, giving actual growth.", "why": "Shows the move towards the PPF."}, {"do": "The machinery adds to the capital stock and raises labour productivity.", "why": "This explains the effect on capacity."}, {"do": "Higher capacity shifts LRAS and the PPF outwards, giving potential growth.", "why": "Shows the long-run effect on supply."}], "answer": "Investment raises AD, so real GDP rises (actual growth). The new capital raises productivity and the capital stock, shifting LRAS and the PPF outwards (potential growth)."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention both AD and LRAS for investment?", "o": ["Investment is both a demand component and adds to capacity", "LRAS only responds to spending", "AD and LRAS are the same curve", "Investment has no effect on AD"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: investment spending raises AD at once and also adds capital, so capacity rises over time.", "LRAS depends on capacity, not on spending.", "They are different curves, showing demand and capacity.", "Investment is a component of AD, so it does affect AD."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how export-led growth could raise actual GDP.", "given": ["Step 1: Exports (X) are a component of AD."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["AD rises, so real GDP rises if there is spare capacity", "LRAS shifts out as exports rise", "Imports must fall to the same degree", "Potential output always rises equally"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: higher X shifts AD right, raising real GDP when spare capacity exists.", "Exports shift AD, not LRAS; this confuses actual and potential growth.", "Imports need not change; this is not part of the logic.", "Higher AD does not guarantee more capacity, so 'always' is wrong."]}, "trap": "Do not call a rise in AD potential growth. Higher demand gives actual growth; only more or better factors of production shift LRAS or the PPF outwards.", "recap": "Actual growth is a rise in real GDP; potential growth is a rise in productive capacity, shown by LRAS or the PPF shifting outwards."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.5.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Output gaps link growth to inflation and unemployment. Expect to explain them, draw them on AD/AS and discuss why they are hard to measure in 6- to 12-mark answers.", "hook": {"q": "An economy produces well above its sustainable level of output. What are you most likely to see?", "o": ["Firms struggling to find workers and prices rising faster", "Unemployment well above its natural rate", "Large amounts of unused factory capacity", "Prices falling across the economy"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: output above sustainable levels strains labour and resources, so shortages and rising prices follow.", "Wrong: high unemployment suggests output below potential, not above it.", "Wrong: unused capacity is a sign of output below potential.", "Wrong: falling prices fit weak demand, not an overheating economy."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What an output gap is", "b": "The **output gap** is the difference between actual output and **potential output** (the maximum sustainable output when resources are fully employed). As a percentage: $\\frac{Y - Y^*}{Y^*}\\times 100$. Always divide by potential output."}, {"h": "Positive and negative gaps", "b": "A **positive output gap** means actual output exceeds potential: resources are over-used, unemployment is below its natural rate and **inflationary pressure** builds. A **negative output gap** means **spare capacity**: cyclical unemployment and downward pressure on inflation. Growth can occur while a gap is negative."}, {"h": "On an AD/AS diagram", "b": "Draw a vertical **LRAS** at potential output Y*. Equilibrium is where AD meets **SRAS**. If that point is to the right of LRAS, the gap is positive. If it is to the left, the gap is negative. If it is on LRAS, the gap is zero."}, {"h": "Why gaps are hard to measure", "b": "**Potential output** cannot be observed; it is estimated, and estimates differ. Actual GDP is also **revised**: ONS analysis published in 2026 found a mean absolute revision of 0.27 percentage points from first to final estimates since Q1 2000. Real-time gaps are therefore uncertain."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a positive output gap may lead to inflation.", "steps": [{"do": "Define the gap: actual output is above potential output, so firms are producing beyond their normal sustainable capacity.", "why": "Starts with the definition, which shows knowledge."}, {"do": "Labour and other resources become scarce, and unemployment falls below its natural rate.", "why": "Shows the link from over-use of resources to the labour market."}, {"do": "Firms compete for workers, so wages rise. Firms' costs rise as wages and input prices increase.", "why": "Explains the mechanism through costs."}, {"do": "Firms pass on higher costs as higher prices, and strong demand lets them do so. The price level rises.", "why": "Completes the chain to inflation."}], "answer": "With actual output above potential, resources are over-used and unemployment falls below its natural rate. Firms compete for scarce labour, so wages and costs rise. Strong demand lets firms raise prices, so the price level rises and inflationary pressure builds."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example say wages rise when unemployment is below its natural rate?", "o": ["Workers are scarce, so firms compete to hire and keep them", "The government sets higher wages when output is above potential", "Potential output falls, so workers produce less", "Lower unemployment cuts firms' demand for labour"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: scarce labour means firms bid up wages to recruit and retain workers.", "Wrong: wages are set by labour market forces, not by a government rule tied to the gap.", "Wrong: potential output does not fall in a positive gap; actual output is above it.", "Wrong: firms' demand for labour is high in a positive gap, which is why unemployment is low."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a negative output gap may cause cyclical unemployment.", "given": ["Actual output is below potential output, so firms have spare capacity."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Firms need fewer workers for the lower output, so they cut jobs and unemployment rises", "Firms hire more workers to use up their spare capacity", "Workers lack the skills firms need, so structural unemployment rises", "Wages fall, so unemployment falls back to the natural rate automatically"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: weak demand means less output is needed, so labour demand falls. This is cyclical (demand-deficient) unemployment.", "Wrong: firms with spare capacity have no need to hire more; this reverses the link.", "Wrong: this describes structural unemployment, not the demand-led cyclical type linked to a gap.", "Wrong: wages are often slow to fall, and the question asks for the cause of unemployment, not an automatic fix."]}, "trap": "Do not treat a positive gap as simply good. Actual output above potential is unsustainable and fuels inflation. Also divide by potential output, not actual output.", "recap": "Output gap = actual minus potential output; positive means inflation pressure, negative means spare capacity, and potential output can only be estimated."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.5.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions on the trade cycle ask you to describe booms and recessions and link their features to AD, unemployment, inflation and government finances, in multiple-choice and short answers.", "hook": {"q": "A toy shop sees sales rise as customers feel secure in their jobs. If this happens across the whole economy for a long time, what is most likely to happen to prices?", "o": ["They fall, as firms sell more and cut prices", "They rise, as demand pushes against firms' capacity", "They stay fixed, as prices do not depend on demand", "They fall, as unemployment rises"], "a": 1, "f": ["Higher demand does not usually make firms cut prices; it lets them raise them.", "Right. Strong demand pushing against limited capacity lets firms raise prices, so inflation pressure builds in a boom.", "Prices respond to demand and costs; they are not fixed.", "Unemployment would be falling in this situation, not rising."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The trade cycle", "b": "The **trade (business) cycle** is the pattern of fluctuations of actual GDP around its **trend** rate of growth. It has four phases: **boom** (peak), slowdown, **recession** (trough) and recovery. Output can still rise during a slowdown."}, {"h": "Features of a boom", "b": "In a boom, real GDP grows faster than trend and the **output gap** is positive. Consumer and business confidence are high, investment and imports rise, **unemployment** falls and firms make higher profits. Demand-pull **inflation** pressure builds. Tax revenues rise and government borrowing falls."}, {"h": "Features of a recession", "b": "A **technical recession** is two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP. Consumption and investment fall, firms fail, **unemployment** rises and inflation falls. Tax revenue falls and benefit spending rises, so government **borrowing** increases (automatic stabilisers)."}, {"h": "Recovery takes time", "b": "After a trough, growth returns, but output may stay below its old peak. ONS analysis published on 3 August 2026 noted UK GDP took from Q2 2008 to Q3 2013 to surpass its pre-recession peak after the financial crisis, versus Q1 2020 to Q3 2021 during COVID-19."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a recession affects unemployment and government borrowing.", "steps": [{"do": "Define the recession: real GDP falls for two consecutive quarters, so output and aggregate demand are falling.", "why": "A clear starting point shows you know what a recession is."}, {"do": "Firms see demand for goods fall, so they cut output and reduce their workforce.", "why": "The demand for labour is derived from demand for output."}, {"do": "Unemployment rises, so fewer people pay income tax and incomes and spending fall, reducing VAT. Profits fall, cutting corporation tax.", "why": "This links higher unemployment to lower tax revenue."}, {"do": "More people claim benefits such as Universal Credit, so government spending rises.", "why": "This is the second part of the effect on government finances."}, {"do": "Lower revenue and higher spending widen the budget deficit, so government borrowing rises.", "why": "This completes the chain to the conclusion."}], "answer": "In a recession, real GDP falls, so firms cut output and jobs and unemployment rises. Falling incomes and profits reduce tax revenue while benefit spending rises. The budget deficit widens, so government borrowing increases."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer link falling output to rising unemployment?", "o": ["Because workers choose to leave jobs when GDP falls", "Because the demand for labour is derived from the demand for output", "Because falling output always raises the price of labour", "Because unemployment is measured by quarterly GDP"], "a": 1, "f": ["Recession unemployment is mostly involuntary; workers are laid off, they do not choose to leave.", "Right. Firms hire labour to produce goods, so when demand for output falls, demand for labour falls too.", "Falling demand for labour tends to reduce, not raise, wage pressure.", "Unemployment is measured by surveys such as the Labour Force Survey, not by GDP."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a boom can cause inflation.", "given": ["In a boom, real GDP grows faster than trend and consumer confidence and incomes rise.", "So aggregate demand rises quickly and spare capacity in the economy falls."], "q": "What is the next step in the chain of reasoning?", "o": ["Firms face limited capacity and rising costs, so they raise prices: demand-pull inflation", "Prices fall as firms benefit from higher sales and lower unit costs", "Unemployment rises, so wage costs fall and prices fall", "Government borrowing rises as benefit spending increases, so prices rise"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. With less spare capacity, demand outruns supply, so firms can and must raise prices, creating demand-pull inflation.", "Prices rarely fall in a boom; demand pressure and capacity limits push them up.", "Unemployment falls in a boom, not rises, so wage costs tend to rise.", "Benefit spending falls in a boom as unemployment falls; this is a recession feature."]}, "trap": "Calling any slowdown a recession. Falling growth is not falling output. Check whether real GDP actually fell for two consecutive quarters before you write 'recession'.", "recap": "A boom has fast growth, falling unemployment and rising inflation; a recession has two or more quarters of falling real GDP, rising unemployment and weaker inflation."}
{"why": "The impact of growth is examined in extended answers where you must weigh benefits against costs for different groups, and reach a justified judgement.", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.4", "kind": "writing", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Benefits to consumers and firms", "b": "Growth raises **real incomes** and purchasing power, so consumers can buy more and a wider range of goods. Firms see higher demand, **profits** and scope to invest, which can raise future capacity. Both gains depend on who receives the extra income."}, {"h": "Government and living standards", "b": "Higher incomes and employment raise **tax revenue** and cut welfare spending, easing the budget deficit. This can fund health and education, raising **living standards**. Real GDP per head is only an average and ignores distribution."}, {"h": "Costs of growth", "b": "Growth may widen **inequality**, cause **demand-pull inflation** if demand outruns capacity, and deplete resources. Production and consumption create **negative externalities** such as pollution and congestion. These are often not counted in GDP."}, {"h": "Evaluating the impact", "b": "Judge using: **type of growth** (sustainable or not), its **size and speed**, who gains, and whether it is **decoupled** from emissions. Time frame matters: costs may be short run, benefits long run. Reach a clear conclusion."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate the benefits and costs of economic growth for the UK government and the environment.", "steps": [{"do": "Growth raises incomes and employment, so the government receives more income tax and VAT and pays less in unemployment benefits.", "why": "Knowledge and application: states the benefit with a clear chain to the fiscal position."}, {"do": "This narrows the budget deficit and allows more spending on health and education, which can raise long-run living standards.", "why": "Analysis: extends the chain to an end result."}, {"do": "However, higher output can raise resource use and emissions, a negative externality that imposes costs on third parties not included in GDP.", "why": "Analysis of a cost, using the key term externality."}, {"do": "In 2025, provisional DESNZ data showed UK territorial emissions fell 1.8% while renewables supplied a record 52% of electricity, suggesting growth can be decoupled from emissions.", "why": "Application with a real dated figure, used to qualify the cost."}, {"do": "Overall, benefits to government are likely to outweigh costs if growth is sustainable, but this depends on continued decoupling and on inequality being managed.", "why": "Evaluation: a justified judgement with conditions."}], "answer": "Growth lifts tax revenue and lowers benefit spending, easing the deficit. It risks externalities, but in 2025 UK emissions fell 1.8% as renewables supplied 52% of electricity (provisional DESNZ). Overall, gains outweigh costs if growth stays sustainable."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention that emissions fell 1.8% in 2025?", "o": ["To prove growth never harms the environment", "To qualify the environmental cost with evidence of decoupling", "To show that GDP fell in 2025", "To define the term externality"], "a": 1, "x": "", "f": ["One year of data cannot prove 'never'; that would be overgeneralising.", "Correct: the data show output can grow while emissions fall, supporting evaluation of the cost.", "The figure is about emissions, not GDP, so it says nothing about GDP falling.", "It is evidence, not a definition; the definition was given in the earlier step."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate the effects of economic growth on consumers and firms.", "given": ["Growth raises real incomes, so consumers can afford more goods and services.", "Higher demand raises firms' sales and profits, encouraging investment."], "q": "Which is the best next step for evaluation?", "o": ["Say growth is always good, so no counterpoint is needed.", "Add that gains depend on distribution and on firms' rising costs.", "Say all consumers benefit equally from higher output.", "Say profits are irrelevant to consumers' living standards."], "a": 1, "f": ["Evaluation needs a counterpoint and a judgement; asserting 'always good' earns no evaluation marks.", "Correct: it adds a counterargument with conditions, which is what evaluation rewards.", "This ignores unequal distribution; it is unsupported and one-sided.", "Profits can fund investment and raise capacity, so dismissing them lacks analysis."]}, "trap": "Treating real GDP per head as everyone's experience. Always add that it is an average, and that distribution, inflation and environmental costs shape who actually gains.", "recap": "Growth usually brings higher incomes, profits and tax revenue, but evaluate by who gains, how fast, and whether costs like inequality and pollution outweigh them."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.6.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Objectives appear in 'explain' and 'evaluate' questions across Papers 1 and 3, because every policy is judged against them. You need each goal, its measure and how it can clash.", "hook": {"q": "A government wants a fairer distribution of income. Which figure would best show progress?", "o": ["CPI inflation falling towards 2%", "The Gini coefficient falling", "The unemployment rate rising", "The current account deficit narrowing"], "a": 1, "f": ["Falling inflation helps the price stability objective, not the fairness of income distribution.", "A lower Gini coefficient means incomes are spread more equally. It is the usual measure for the equality objective.", "Rising unemployment is a failure on another objective and would usually widen inequality.", "This is progress on the current account objective, not equality."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The seven objectives", "b": "Edexcel lists: **economic growth**, **low unemployment**, **low and stable inflation**, **balance of payments equilibrium** on the current account, a **balanced government budget**, **protection of the environment** and **greater income equality** (redistribution). Governments rarely hit all seven together."}, {"h": "How each is measured", "b": "Growth: the rate of change of real GDP. Unemployment: the rate from the Labour Force Survey. Inflation: CPI against the **2% target**. Current account: balance of exports and imports, income and transfers. Budget: deficit or surplus. Equality: the **Gini coefficient**. Environment: indicators such as emissions."}, {"h": "Objectives can conflict", "b": "Policies that help one goal can harm another. Faster growth can pull in imports and widen the **current account deficit**. Rising demand can lift **inflation**. Cutting a deficit can slow growth. This is why governments must prioritise and why evaluation matters."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why low and stable inflation is a macroeconomic objective for the government.", "steps": [{"do": "Define it: inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level, and the government's target is 2% CPI.", "why": "A clear definition and the target earn knowledge marks."}, {"do": "High or unpredictable inflation makes future prices uncertain, so firms are less willing to invest.", "why": "This links inflation to investment and growth: a chain of reasoning."}, {"do": "If UK inflation exceeds that of trading partners, exports become less price competitive and the current account may worsen.", "why": "This links to a second objective, showing inflation's wider effects."}, {"do": "If wages rise more slowly than prices, real incomes fall, hurting people on fixed incomes.", "why": "This brings in living standards and the equality objective."}], "answer": "Stable inflation near the 2% target keeps prices predictable, supporting investment and growth. It protects export competitiveness and the current account, and prevents falling real incomes. So it supports several other objectives."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer link inflation to the current account in step 3?", "o": ["Because inflation is part of the current account balance itself", "Because high inflation always makes imports cheaper", "Because higher relative prices reduce export competitiveness, so objectives are connected", "Because the 2% target is set by the current account"], "a": 2, "x": "", "f": ["Inflation is not a component of the current account. The link works through price competitiveness.", "Higher UK prices make imports relatively cheaper, but that raises import demand and worsens the balance. The step is about exports.", "Right. If UK prices rise faster than abroad, exports become dearer and sell less, so a price objective affects the external balance.", "The inflation target is set by the government for price stability, not by the current account."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why low unemployment is a macroeconomic objective for the government.", "given": ["Unemployment is people without a job who are actively seeking and available for work.", "Measured by the unemployment rate: the unemployed as a share of the economically active."], "q": "Which is the best next point?", "o": ["Unemployment raises tax revenue, so the budget moves towards balance.", "Unemployed workers import less, so the current account improves.", "Unemployment adds to labour supply, so output rises.", "Unemployed workers are unused resources, so output is below potential and GDP is lost."], "a": 3, "f": ["Unemployment cuts income tax and raises benefit spending, so the budget worsens. This is the wrong direction.", "Lower incomes may cut imports, but this is not the main reason for the objective and is not reliable.", "Unemployed people are not producing, so they do not add to output. Confuses labour supply with employment.", "Right. Unemployment wastes labour, so actual output falls below potential. This lost output is a core reason for the objective."]}, "trap": "Do not treat 100% minus the employment rate as unemployment, or a higher Gini as more equal. Define each objective's measure precisely and say which way it moves.", "recap": "Know the seven objectives, how each is measured, and that pursuing one can undermine another."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.6.2", "kind": "writing", "why": "Demand-side policy appears in extended evaluation questions, where you must analyse a tool and judge how effective it is in a given context.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Monetary policy tools", "b": "The **Monetary Policy Committee** at the Bank of England sets **Bank Rate** to meet the CPI inflation target of 2%. It can also use **quantitative easing**, creating money to buy bonds, which lowers long-term yields and boosts liquidity."}, {"h": "Fiscal policy tools", "b": "**Fiscal policy** changes **government spending** and **taxation**. Spending more or taxing less is expansionary and widens the **budget deficit**. The reverse is contractionary. Annual deficits add to the **national debt**, the accumulated stock."}, {"h": "Transmission to AD", "b": "Lower Bank Rate cuts borrowing costs, raising consumption (C) and investment (I), and weakens the pound, helping net exports. Tax cuts raise **disposable income**. Spending raises G directly. AD shifts right, with a **multiplier** effect."}, {"h": "Evaluating effectiveness", "b": "Judge by: **time lags**, **confidence**, **spare capacity** (output gap), size of the multiplier, **crowding out**, and debt levels. After the 2008 crisis, rates near zero and QE had limited effect where banks restricted lending, so effectiveness varies."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate the use of quantitative easing to raise aggregate demand in a recession. (Model answer built in parts.)", "steps": [{"do": "QE is when the Bank of England creates electronic money to buy government bonds from financial institutions, which raises bond prices and lowers yields.", "why": "Defines the tool accurately and shows knowledge of how it works."}, {"do": "Lower yields cut long-term borrowing costs and extra liquidity may increase lending, so investment and consumption rise, shifting AD to the right.", "why": "A clear chain of analysis linking QE to AD."}, {"do": "In a deep recession with a large negative output gap, higher AD would raise real GDP and reduce unemployment without much inflation.", "why": "Applies analysis to the context given."}, {"do": "However, if confidence is low or banks hoard the cash, spending may barely rise, and asset price rises can widen wealth inequality.", "why": "Evaluation: limits of the policy, with reasoning."}, {"do": "Overall, QE helps most when combined with fiscal support and when banks are willing to lend, so its success depends on confidence.", "why": "A judgement supported by a condition."}], "answer": "QE lowers yields and adds liquidity, raising AD, but works only if confidence and lending respond, so it is most effective alongside fiscal support."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention the output gap when judging QE?", "o": ["It shows spare capacity, so higher AD raises output rather than just prices", "It proves QE will definitely work", "It shows the government's budget is balanced", "It shows inflation must be above target"], "a": 0, "x": "", "f": ["With spare capacity, extra demand mostly lifts real GDP, so the policy is more likely to be appropriate.", "Context only makes success more likely, never certain; claiming certainty is poor evaluation.", "The output gap measures actual versus potential output, not the budget position.", "A negative gap usually goes with weak, not high, inflation."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate the use of a cut in income tax to reduce unemployment.", "given": ["Define fiscal policy: tax cuts raise disposable income.", "Analysis: higher consumption raises AD, so firms hire more workers."], "q": "Which is the best next sentence, offering evaluation?", "o": ["The effect depends on the marginal propensity to consume, since savers or high earners spend less of the extra income.", "Income tax is a tax on income paid by workers.", "Unemployment is when people want work but cannot find it.", "Therefore, unemployment will certainly fall."], "a": 0, "x": "", "f": ["It gives a reasoned limitation tied to the multiplier, which earns evaluation marks.", "This repeats a definition and adds no evaluation.", "This is a definition of unemployment, adding no judgement.", "Asserting certainty with no justification is unsupported and not evaluation."]}, "trap": "Listing tools without judging them. Always add a limit (lags, confidence, spare capacity, debt) and a conclusion that depends on context.", "recap": "Demand-side policy shifts AD through Bank Rate, QE, taxes and spending, but its success depends on context and confidence."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.6.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Supply-side policies appear in short explain questions and in 12- and 25-mark essays, where you must explain how they work and evaluate them.", "hook": {"q": "A government pays to train 100,000 unemployed workers in skills that firms need. What is the main long-run effect?", "o": ["Aggregate demand rises permanently, so prices keep rising", "The economy's productive capacity rises", "Potential output falls as fewer people work", "Nothing changes, because markets already set the right skills"], "a": 1, "f": ["Any demand boost is short-run; the point of training is to raise capacity, not just spending.", "Better skills raise labour productivity and the quality of labour, so potential output rises.", "Training raises the supply and quality of labour, so potential output rises rather than falls.", "Training is an answer to market failure: firms under-provide training, so the market does not always set the right skills."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What they aim to do", "b": "**Supply-side policies** aim to raise the economy's **productive capacity** by increasing the quantity or quality of factors of production. On AD/AS this shifts **LRAS** right. The aims are higher growth, lower structural unemployment and lower inflationary pressure."}, {"h": "Market-based policies", "b": "**Market-based** policies free markets and strengthen **incentives**. Examples: lower taxes, privatisation, deregulation, competition policy, labour market reform. The UK made full expensing permanent (Autumn Finance Bill 2023): a 100% first-year allowance on qualifying plant and machinery, to encourage **investment**."}, {"h": "Interventionist policies", "b": "**Interventionist** policies use government spending or action to correct **market failure**: education and training, infrastructure, R&D, subsidies. A £2,000 hiring payment starting from October 2026 for non-levy employers hiring 16–24-year-old apprentices aims to raise **skills**."}, {"h": "Evaluating them", "b": "Judge each policy on **time lags**, cost and **opportunity cost**, the size of the response, and side effects. Market-based policies may widen inequality or harm the environment; interventionist ones risk **government failure** and may not match the skills firms need."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a cut in corporation tax could shift LRAS to the right. (6 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "A cut in corporation tax raises firms' after-tax profits.", "why": "It identifies the policy as a market-based incentive and states its first effect."}, {"do": "Higher expected returns encourage firms to invest more in machinery, technology and premises.", "why": "Links the incentive to a change in firms' behaviour."}, {"do": "Greater investment raises the capital stock and often brings newer technology.", "why": "Shows the change in a factor of production."}, {"do": "Labour productivity rises, so the economy can produce more with its resources: potential output rises.", "why": "Explains why capacity increases, the key supply-side link."}, {"do": "So LRAS shifts right, allowing higher non-inflationary growth.", "why": "Finishes with the diagram effect and the macro result."}], "answer": "Lower corporation tax raises after-tax profits and the returns on investment. Firms invest more, increasing the capital stock and productivity. Potential output rises, so LRAS shifts right, giving higher growth without extra inflationary pressure, though investment takes time to respond."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer move from higher investment to higher productivity before mentioning LRAS?", "o": ["Because investment is a demand-side change that must be reversed first", "Because LRAS shifts only when taxes fall, not when productivity rises", "Because LRAS shifts only if capacity rises, and productivity is how investment raises capacity", "Because productivity is shown on the AD curve"], "a": 2, "x": "", "f": ["Investment is also part of AD, but here the key effect is on capacity, not a demand boost.", "LRAS depends on productive capacity; tax cuts only matter through their effect on it.", "Without a rise in capacity, LRAS would not move, so the chain from investment to productivity to potential output is needed.", "Productivity is a supply-side feature, shown by LRAS, not by the AD curve."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how government-funded apprenticeships could raise LRAS. (6 marks)", "given": ["Funding lowers the cost to firms of hiring and training young workers, so more apprentices are trained."], "q": "What is the best next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Workers spend more, so AD shifts right and LRAS rises with it", "Taxes rise to pay for the scheme, so firms invest less and LRAS shifts left", "Training shrinks the labour force, which raises wages and raises potential output", "Trained workers are more skilled, raising labour productivity and the quality of labour, so potential output rises"], "a": 3, "x": "", "f": ["This is a demand-side effect; LRAS shifts through higher capacity, not higher spending.", "Tax effects are an evaluation point, not the next step in the causal chain to LRAS.", "Training raises the quality of labour; it does not shrink the labour force, and higher wages are not capacity.", "Higher skills raise labour productivity, so the economy can produce more: that is the link to LRAS."]}, "trap": "Do not stop at 'LRAS shifts right'. Explain the chain: what changes in firms' or workers' behaviour, how capacity rises, and why. Then add a limit, such as time lags or cost.", "recap": "Supply-side policies raise productive capacity and shift LRAS right; market-based ones use incentives and competition, interventionist ones use government spending."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.6.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Evaluation marks in 9EC0 policy questions often come from seeing that a policy helps one objective but harms another, so you need trade-offs ready.", "hook": {"q": "A government boosts AD to cut unemployment when the economy has spare capacity. What is most likely to happen to the inflation rate?", "o": ["It falls, because output is higher", "It rises, as demand pressure builds on wages and prices", "It stays exactly the same", "It falls, because unemployment is lower"], "a": 1, "f": ["Higher output does not by itself lower prices; extra demand tends to push them up.", "Right: higher AD raises output and employment, then pressure on wages and prices builds, so inflation tends to rise.", "Inflation usually responds to higher demand, especially as spare capacity is used up.", "Lower unemployment is linked with higher wage pressure, so inflation tends to rise, not fall."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why objectives clash", "b": "Governments pursue several **macroeconomic objectives**: growth, low inflation, low unemployment, a sustainable current account and a manageable budget deficit. One **policy instrument** often affects several at once. A policy that helps one objective can therefore harm another. This is a **trade-off**."}, {"h": "Short-run Phillips curve", "b": "The **short-run Phillips curve (SRPC)** plots inflation against unemployment. It slopes downwards. Higher AD raises output and employment, so unemployment falls, but wage and price pressure raises inflation. Moving along the curve is a **trade-off** between the two."}, {"h": "Shifting the SRPC", "b": "The SRPC shifts outwards (right) if **inflation expectations** rise or SRAS falls, for example from an energy cost shock. Each unemployment rate then comes with higher inflation. High inflation with weak growth is **stagflation**. Inwards shifts give a better trade-off."}, {"h": "Other common conflicts", "b": "Growth can worsen the **current account**, as higher incomes raise imports. Growth may harm the environment or widen inequality. **Fiscal tightening** to cut the deficit lowers AD, reducing short-run output and employment. Raising interest rates to cut inflation can slow growth."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why raising Bank Rate to reduce inflation may conflict with other macroeconomic objectives.", "steps": [{"do": "State the aim: on 4 November 2026 the Bank of England voted 8–1 to raise Bank Rate to 4% because forecast CPI inflation was moving towards 5%.", "why": "Uses real context and shows the objective is price stability."}, {"do": "Higher Bank Rate raises borrowing costs and the reward for saving, so consumption and investment fall.", "why": "Links the policy to components of AD."}, {"do": "AD shifts left, so real GDP growth slows and the output gap moves negative.", "why": "Shows the effect on the growth objective."}, {"do": "Lower output means firms need fewer workers, so cyclical unemployment may rise.", "why": "Links to a second conflicting objective, unemployment."}, {"do": "So lower inflation is gained at the cost of growth and jobs: a trade-off, like a move down the SRPC.", "why": "Concludes with the conflict named and linked to theory."}], "answer": "Raising Bank Rate lowers consumption and investment, shifting AD left. This helps cut inflation, but real GDP growth slows and cyclical unemployment may rise. The Bank therefore trades lower inflation against growth and employment."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer link higher Bank Rate to falling consumption and investment?", "o": ["Because consumption and investment are components of AD that respond to the cost of borrowing", "Because higher interest rates raise firms' profits, encouraging more investment", "Because Bank Rate is a fiscal tool that changes tax revenue", "Because consumption and investment are components of AS only"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: higher borrowing costs and better returns to saving reduce spending on these AD components, which cuts demand-pull pressure.", "Higher rates raise costs and lower expected returns on investment, so investment tends to fall.", "Bank Rate is a monetary instrument; it does not directly change tax revenue.", "Consumption and investment are components of AD, not just AS."]}, "faded": {"task": "The government cuts public spending to reduce the budget deficit. Explain a conflict with another objective.", "given": ["Government spending is a component of AD.", "Cutting it shifts AD to the left."], "q": "What is the next step in the chain of reasoning?", "o": ["Inflation rises because demand has fallen", "Real GDP falls, so firms cut output and cyclical unemployment may rise", "Tax revenue rises automatically, so the deficit disappears at once", "The long-run Phillips curve shifts right, so inflation expectations fall"], "a": 1, "f": ["Falling AD reduces, not raises, demand-pull inflation.", "Right: lower AD cuts output and employment in the short run, so deficit reduction conflicts with growth and unemployment objectives.", "Lower output usually reduces tax revenue and raises welfare spending, so the deficit may not fall quickly.", "The long-run curve is vertical and is not part of this chain; expectations are not the issue."]}, "trap": "Treating the Phillips curve as a permanent trade-off. Remember it is short-run only, and a rise in inflation expectations or a fall in SRAS shifts the curve rather than moving along it.", "recap": "Policies aimed at one objective often harm another; the short-run Phillips curve shows the inflation–unemployment trade-off, and it shifts with expectations or supply shocks."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.1.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Explain questions on why firms grow or stay small, the principal-agent problem and types of firm appear in Paper 1 and Paper 3, often as 4 to 6 mark explanations.", "hook": {"q": "A large company is owned by thousands of shareholders but run day to day by hired managers. What is most likely?", "o": ["Managers will always maximise profit because shareholders watch them closely", "Shareholders will decide every business decision", "Managers will have no power over the firm's spending", "Managers may pursue goals such as pay, status or sales growth that differ from the owners' aims"], "a": 3, "f": ["Shareholders cannot easily monitor every decision, so managers may follow their own aims. 'Always' is too strong.", "Thousands of owners cannot run daily decisions; control passes to managers.", "Managers hold considerable control over spending and so can use it for their own aims.", "Right. Ownership and control are separate, and managers may have different objectives from owners."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why firms grow", "b": "Firms grow to gain **economies of scale**, which lower unit costs. They also seek greater **market power**, spread risk by selling more products or in more markets, and aim for higher profit. Growth can come internally or by merger."}, {"h": "Why firms stay small", "b": "Many firms stay small. At the start of 2026, SMEs were 99.85% of the UK's 5.6 million private sector businesses. Reasons include a **niche market** with limited demand, limited finance, owners valuing control, and **diseconomies of scale**."}, {"h": "Principal-agent problem", "b": "In large firms, **ownership and control are separated**. Shareholders (**principals**) hire managers (**agents**), who know more about the firm. Managers may chase pay, status or sales rather than profit. Remedies: performance-related pay, share options, monitoring and shareholder votes."}, {"h": "Public, private, not-for-profit", "b": "**Private sector** firms are privately owned, mostly aiming for profit: sole traders, partnerships, companies. **Public sector** organisations are owned or controlled by government, such as public corporations. **Not-for-profit** bodies, such as charities, reinvest any surplus in their aims."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the principal-agent problem may arise in a large public limited company.", "steps": [{"do": "State that a plc has thousands of shareholders who own it but do not run it. Managers are hired to make decisions.", "why": "This shows the separation of ownership and control, the root cause of the problem."}, {"do": "Name shareholders as principals and managers as agents acting on their behalf.", "why": "Using the correct terms earns knowledge marks."}, {"do": "Say managers know more about the firm than shareholders do.", "why": "This information gap makes monitoring hard, so agents can act in their own interest."}, {"do": "Link to aims: shareholders want profit and dividends, but managers may want higher pay, status or sales growth.", "why": "This shows the conflict of objectives, which is the problem itself."}, {"do": "Conclude that the managers' decisions may therefore not maximise shareholder wealth.", "why": "A closing consequence completes the chain of reasoning."}], "answer": "A plc's owners are separate from those who control it. Shareholders (principals) hire managers (agents) who know more about the firm. Managers may seek higher pay, status or sales growth instead of profit, so decisions may not maximise shareholder wealth."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer call shareholders the principals rather than the managers?", "o": ["Because shareholders earn more than managers", "Because shareholders own the firm and appoint managers to act for them", "Because shareholders make the daily decisions", "Because principals always have better information"], "a": 1, "f": ["Pay levels do not define the roles; managers may well earn more.", "Right. The principal owns the firm and hires the agent to act on their behalf.", "Managers take the daily decisions; that is why they are the agents.", "In the problem, the agent (manager) usually has better information, not the principal."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a small independent toy shop might choose not to grow.", "given": ["Point 1: Many small firms serve a niche or local market.", "Point 2: Demand there is limited, so extra output may be hard to sell."], "q": "What is the best next linked point?", "o": ["Growth always lowers unit costs, so the owner would expand immediately.", "The shop has a principal-agent problem because it employs managers.", "The owner may value control and flexibility, and may not want the extra risk and debt of expanding.", "The government prevents small firms from growing."], "a": 2, "f": ["This contradicts the argument. Economies of scale are a reason to grow, and may not apply to a niche shop.", "An owner-run shop has no separation of ownership and control, so the problem does not arise.", "Right. Owner objectives such as control, and the risk and finance needed for expansion, are standard reasons to stay small.", "No such rule exists; constraints come from market size, finance and owner choice."]}, "trap": "Do not say the principal-agent problem is workers against managers. It is owners (shareholders) against the managers hired to run the firm.", "recap": "Ownership and control separate in large firms, so managers (agents) may not pursue what shareholders (principals) want."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.1.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions ask you to identify types of growth, explain their benefits and limits, and apply them to firms. You need precise terms to earn knowledge and application marks.", "hook": {"q": "A supermarket opens 20 new stores built with its own profits. How is this growth best described?", "o": ["Organic growth", "Horizontal integration", "Conglomerate integration", "A takeover"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. The firm expands using its own resources, with no other firm acquired.", "Horizontal integration needs a merger or takeover of a firm at the same stage; nothing is acquired here.", "Conglomerate integration joins unrelated firms; no other firm is involved here.", "A takeover means buying another firm; the supermarket built its own stores."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Organic or external growth", "b": "**Organic growth** (internal) comes from a firm's own resources: new outlets, new products, more sales. It is usually slower but keeps control. **External growth** comes from a **merger** or **takeover** of another firm, and is faster."}, {"h": "Horizontal and vertical", "b": "**Horizontal integration** joins firms at the same stage of the same industry. **Vertical integration** joins firms at different stages: **backward** is towards suppliers, **forward** is towards customers or retailers."}, {"h": "Conglomerate integration", "b": "**Conglomerate integration** joins firms in unrelated markets. The main benefit is **risk spreading**: weak profit in one market may be offset elsewhere. There are few cost savings from shared production."}, {"h": "What limits growth", "b": "Limits include the **size of the market**, shortage of **finance**, **diseconomies of scale**, owner objectives, and **regulation**, such as CMA review of mergers. Takeovers may also bring **culture clashes**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain one benefit and one limit for a toy shop chain that buys a toy manufacturer.", "steps": [{"do": "Name the type: the chain buys its supplier, so this is backward vertical integration.", "why": "Naming the type correctly earns knowledge marks."}, {"do": "Benefit: it secures its supply of toys, so shelves stay stocked.", "why": "Links the type to a specific benefit."}, {"do": "It can also cut costs, because it no longer pays the manufacturer's profit margin.", "why": "Explains the chain of reasoning to lower costs."}, {"do": "Limit: the shop may lack skill in manufacturing, so costs could rise.", "why": "A limit must link to this deal."}, {"do": "Combining the two firms could cause diseconomies of scale, such as poor communication, which raise average costs.", "why": "Gives a second developed limit on growth."}], "answer": "This is backward vertical integration. It secures supply and removes a supplier's margin, lowering costs. However, the shop may lack manufacturing expertise, and a bigger firm may suffer diseconomies of scale."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the toy shop chain buying a toy manufacturer backward, not forward, integration?", "o": ["The manufacturer is at an earlier stage, a supplier", "The manufacturer is at a later stage, closer to customers", "The two firms are in the same industry stage", "The two firms sell unrelated products"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Backward means moving back along the supply chain towards suppliers.", "Customers are reached by retailers; the manufacturer is further from customers than the shop, so this is reversed.", "Different stages means vertical integration; the same stage would be horizontal.", "Toys and a toy manufacturer are related, so this is not conglomerate."]}, "faded": {"task": "A car maker buys a chain of car showrooms. Explain one benefit.", "given": ["Type: the maker moves towards its customers, so this is forward vertical integration."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["Benefit: it controls how its cars are sold, and gains the retailer's margin", "Benefit: it spreads risk across unrelated markets", "Benefit: it secures its supply of steel", "Benefit: it removes a rival and raises market share"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Forward integration gives control of distribution and captures the retail margin.", "Risk spreading is the conglomerate benefit; showrooms selling the maker's cars are related.", "Secure supplies come from backward integration; showrooms are not suppliers.", "Removing a rival is horizontal integration; showrooms are customers' side, not rivals."]}, "trap": "Do not mix up forward and backward: backward is towards suppliers, forward towards customers. Always say which direction and link the benefit to the deal.", "recap": "Organic growth is internal; integration is by merger or takeover, as horizontal, vertical (forward or backward) or conglomerate, and each has specific benefits and limits."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.1.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Demergers appear in short explain questions and in evaluation of business growth. You must give reasons and effects on firms, workers and consumers, then judge which matter most.", "hook": {"q": "A group runs a bakery chain and a software firm. Head office struggles to manage both. What is the most likely result of splitting them into two separate firms?", "o": ["Both firms immediately gain more economies of scale", "Each management team can focus on one market", "The group becomes a monopoly in both markets", "Both firms must charge higher prices"], "a": 1, "f": ["Splitting usually loses shared economies of scale rather than gaining them.", "Right: separate firms let managers concentrate on one business, which is a main reason to demerge.", "Splitting reduces the group's size, so it cannot create a monopoly.", "Prices depend on costs and competition; higher prices are not an automatic result."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What a demerger is", "b": "A **demerger** is when one firm splits into two or more separate businesses. It is the reverse of a merger. Shareholders often receive shares in each new firm. On 31 May 2025, Anglo American plc demerged its platinum group metals business, renamed Valterra Platinum."}, {"h": "Why firms demerge", "b": "Large firms can suffer **diseconomies of scale**: slow communication, weak control and low motivation. Splitting gives managers a clearer **focus** on a core business. A demerger can also release value for shareholders and may be required by competition authorities if a firm has too much market power."}, {"h": "Effects on firms and workers", "b": "Firms may gain lower average costs and quicker decisions, but lose **economies of scale** and the spreading of risk from diversification. Workers may gain better communication and motivation in a smaller firm. They may lose jobs where roles are duplicated, or lose internal promotion routes."}, {"h": "Effects on consumers", "b": "If the new firms compete, consumers may gain lower prices, more **choice** and better quality. If costs rise from lost economies of scale, prices may rise. The result depends on market structure, how much the firms compete, and the size of any cost changes."}], "example": {"task": "Suppose a group owns a toy shop chain and an unrelated online games studio. Explain one reason it might demerge and one effect on workers.", "steps": [{"do": "A demerger splits the group into two separate firms, one for the toy shops and one for the games studio.", "why": "Defining the term shows knowledge."}, {"do": "One reason is diseconomies of scale: the group is hard to manage because the two businesses are unrelated.", "why": "A clear reason, applied to the context."}, {"do": "Splitting lets each management team focus on one market and make faster decisions.", "why": "This is the first link of analysis."}, {"do": "This cuts average costs caused by poor coordination, so each firm can be more competitive.", "why": "Linking to costs completes the chain of reasoning."}, {"do": "For workers, a smaller firm may improve communication and motivation, but duplicated head-office roles could be made redundant.", "why": "Gives an effect on workers with a balancing point."}], "answer": "The group could demerge because diseconomies of scale arise from managing unrelated businesses. Separate firms allow focus and faster decisions, lowering average costs. Workers may gain from better communication and motivation, but duplicated head-office roles could be lost, so the effect depends on how many jobs are cut."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example link the reason to diseconomies of scale rather than to economies of scale?", "o": ["Because demergers always make a firm bigger", "Because economies of scale are only for small firms", "Because unrelated divisions make a big group hard to coordinate, raising average costs", "Because workers prefer smaller firms"], "a": 2, "x": "", "f": ["A demerger makes firms smaller, not bigger.", "Economies of scale can occur at any size; the point is that this firm suffers the opposite problem.", "Right: poor coordination of unrelated divisions raises average costs, a diseconomy of scale that splitting can reduce.", "Worker preference may matter, but it is not the economic reason for a fall in costs."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain one effect of a demerger on consumers when a firm with an 80% market share splits into two rivals.", "given": ["A demerger splits one firm into separate businesses.", "Here the two new firms sell similar products in the same market."], "q": "What is the next step in a good answer?", "o": ["Competition between the two firms should increase, which may lower prices and widen choice.", "Consumers face higher prices because the firms now have monopoly power.", "Prices fall automatically because economies of scale rise.", "Consumers are unaffected because the products stay the same."], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: rivalry between the new firms puts pressure on prices, quality and variety, which is the consumer benefit.", "Market concentration falls after the split, so monopoly power falls.", "Splitting usually loses economies of scale, so this misreads their direction.", "Competition changes firms' behaviour, so consumers can be affected even if products are similar."]}, "trap": "Do not assume a demerger always lowers costs and prices. It removes diseconomies of scale but may lose economies of scale, so say which effect is stronger and why.", "recap": "A demerger splits a firm to regain focus and cut diseconomies of scale, but it may lose economies of scale and jobs."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Business objectives are examined as short explain questions and as diagram-based analysis, often within longer essays on whether firms really maximise profit.", "hook": {"q": "A toy shop sells one more board game. The extra sale adds £6 to revenue and £9 to costs. What happens to profit?", "o": ["It rises, because revenue has gone up", "It falls by £3, because MC is above MR", "It stays the same, because the sale is only one unit", "It rises by £15, because revenue and costs add together"], "a": 1, "f": ["Revenue rising is not enough. You must compare the extra revenue with the extra cost.", "Right. MC (£9) exceeds MR (£6), so this unit lowers profit by £3. This is the logic of MC = MR.", "One unit still changes profit. Marginal decisions are about the effect of each extra unit.", "Costs are subtracted from revenue, not added to it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Profit maximisation", "b": "**Profit maximisation** is at the output where **MC = MR**, with MC cutting MR from below. Before it, MR > MC, so each unit adds to profit. Read the price off the AR curve above that output. Supernormal profit = (AR − AC) × output."}, {"h": "Revenue maximisation", "b": "**Revenue maximisation** is where **MR = 0**, the peak of the total revenue curve. Beyond it, MR is negative and revenue falls. As MC is positive, output is higher and price lower than at profit maximisation."}, {"h": "Sales maximisation", "b": "**Sales maximisation** means the highest output possible without making a loss. On the diagram this is where **AR = AC**, earning only normal profit. Output is usually higher than at profit maximisation. A minimum profit target would stop output sooner."}, {"h": "Satisficing", "b": "**Satisficing** means aiming for an acceptable level of profit, not the maximum. It arises when ownership and control are separate (the **principal–agent problem**). Managers may pursue pay, status or staff goals while keeping shareholders content."}], "example": {"task": "Explain, using a diagram, why a firm that maximises revenue produces more than one that maximises profit.", "steps": [{"do": "Draw downward-sloping AR and MR curves, with a rising MC curve. Profit-maximising output Qp is where MC cuts MR from below.", "why": "MC = MR is the profit-maximising rule."}, {"do": "Revenue-maximising output Qr is where MR cuts the horizontal axis, so MR = 0.", "why": "Total revenue peaks when an extra unit adds nothing."}, {"do": "At Qp, MC is positive, so MR is also positive there. MR falls as output rises, so MR = 0 only occurs at a higher output.", "why": "This is why Qr lies to the right of Qp."}, {"do": "Between Qp and Qr each extra unit adds to revenue (MR > 0) but costs more (MC > MR), so profit falls.", "why": "Shows the trade-off: more revenue, less profit."}, {"do": "Reading up to AR, the price at Qr is lower than at Qp.", "why": "Larger output sells only at a lower price on a downward-sloping demand curve."}], "answer": "Profit maximisation is where MC = MR, and MR is still positive there because MC is positive. Revenue maximisation needs MR = 0. As MR falls with output, this is at a higher output, with a lower price. Profit is lower."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer place the revenue-maximising output to the right of the profit-maximising output?", "o": ["MR falls as output rises, so MR = 0 comes after MR = positive MC", "Total revenue is always higher at the profit-maximising output", "Average cost is zero at the revenue-maximising output", "Demand becomes perfectly inelastic beyond the profit-maximising output"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. MR slopes down and MC is positive, so MR reaches zero only at a higher output than where it equals MC.", "Total revenue peaks at MR = 0, so it is higher at the revenue-maximising output.", "Average cost is never zero here. Revenue maximisation ignores costs, it does not remove them.", "Demand elasticity is not the reason; the MR curve falling to zero is."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain, using a diagram, why a sales-maximising firm produces more than a profit-maximising firm.", "given": ["Draw AR, MR, MC and AC. Profit maximisation is at MC = MR, where AR is above AC, so the firm earns supernormal profit."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Output keeps rising until MR = 0, the peak of total revenue", "Output rises until AR = AC, where supernormal profit falls to normal profit", "Output rises until MC reaches zero, so all costs disappear", "Output rises until AC is at its lowest point, where AC meets MC"], "a": 1, "f": ["MR = 0 is revenue maximisation, not sales maximisation. Sales maximisation is limited by AR = AC.", "Right. The firm sells more at lower prices until AR meets AC, the highest output without a loss, giving normal profit only.", "MC does not fall to zero, and the limit is about profit, not costs vanishing.", "Minimum AC is productive efficiency. The sales maximiser's limit is where AR = AC."]}, "trap": "Using MC = MR for every objective. Revenue maximisation uses MR = 0 and sales maximisation uses AR = AC. Name the rule first, then draw the output.", "recap": "Profit max: MC = MR; revenue max: MR = 0; sales max: AR = AC; satisficing: only acceptable profit."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.3.1", "kind": "method", "why": "Revenue calculations and the TR–PED link appear in short calculation questions, and they underpin profit maximisation (MR = MC) in later topics.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Three revenue measures", "b": "**Total revenue (TR)** = price × quantity. **Average revenue (AR)** = TR ÷ Q, which is always equal to the price, so the AR curve is the firm's demand curve. **Marginal revenue (MR)** = ΔTR ÷ ΔQ, the extra revenue from selling one more unit."}, {"h": "Why MR lies below AR", "b": "A firm with market power must cut the price to sell more. The lower price applies to all units sold, not just the extra one. So **MR** is below **AR**. With a straight-line demand curve, MR falls twice as steeply and cuts the quantity axis halfway along."}, {"h": "Revenue and elasticity", "b": "Where demand is **price elastic** (PED beyond −1), a price cut raises TR and MR is positive. Where it is **price inelastic**, a price cut lowers TR and MR is negative. At **unit elasticity**, TR is at its maximum and MR = 0."}, {"h": "Price takers", "b": "A **price taker** sells every unit at the market price. Extra units add exactly the price to revenue. So AR = MR = price, a horizontal line. TR rises in a straight line from the origin as output rises."}], "example": {"task": "A shop's demand schedule for model trains: £50 → 10 sold; £45 → 12; £40 → 14; £35 → 16. Find TR, the MR of each move, and say what happens to TR.", "steps": [{"do": "Work out TR = P × Q: £500, £540, £560, £560.", "why": "TR is price times quantity at each point."}, {"do": "MR for £50 → £45: ΔTR = £40, ΔQ = 2, so MR = £20.", "why": "MR = ΔTR ÷ ΔQ, using the change in quantity."}, {"do": "MR for £45 → £40: ΔTR = £20, ΔQ = 2, so MR = £10. For £40 → £35: ΔTR = £0, so MR = £0.", "why": "Same formula for each move. MR keeps falling."}, {"do": "AR is the price each time (£50, £45, £40, £35). MR is below AR throughout.", "why": "AR = TR ÷ Q = price, and a price cut applies to all units."}, {"do": "TR rises while MR is positive, then is flat when MR = 0 between £40 and £35.", "why": "TR is at a maximum where MR = 0, near unit elasticity."}], "answer": "TR is £500, £540, £560 and £560. MR for the three moves is £20, £10 and £0. AR is the price (£50 to £35). TR rises while demand is elastic and peaks where MR = 0, around unit elasticity."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is MR for the first move (£20) far below the new price of £45?", "o": ["Because MR is always half of the old price", "Because the cut applies to all units, losing revenue on the 10 that sold at £50", "Because the formula divides by the price change instead of quantity", "Because MR is the total revenue after the price cut"], "a": 1, "x": "", "f": ["MR is not a fixed fraction of price. It comes from the actual change in TR for the extra units.", "Right. The firm gains from the 2 extra trains but loses £5 on each of the 10 it already sold, so MR is below the price.", "MR divides ΔTR by the change in quantity, not by the price change.", "TR after the cut is £540. MR is the change in TR per extra unit, not the TR itself."]}, "faded": {"task": "Demand schedule: £8 → 5 sold; £7 → 7; £6 → 8. Find the MR of moving from £7 to £6.", "given": ["TR at £8 = 5 × £8 = £40", "TR at £7 = 7 × £7 = £49", "TR at £6 = 8 × £6 = £48"], "q": "What is the next step, and the MR for the move from £7 to £6?", "o": ["MR = £48 ÷ 8 = £6, since MR equals the price", "MR = (£48 − £49) ÷ 1 = −£1 per extra unit", "MR = (£49 − £48) ÷ 1 = £1 per extra unit", "MR = (£48 − £40) ÷ 3 = £2.67"], "a": 1, "x": "", "f": ["£6 is the price (AR), not MR. MR is ΔTR ÷ ΔQ.", "Right. ΔTR = 48 − 49 = −£1 and ΔQ = 8 − 7 = 1, so MR = −£1. TR falls, so demand is inelastic here.", "The sign is reversed. TR falls from £49 to £48, so the change is −£1, not +£1.", "This uses the wrong pair of points (£8 to £6). The move asked for is £7 to £6."]}, "trap": "Do not give price as MR, and do not assume a price cut always raises TR. Calculate ΔTR ÷ ΔQ; if TR falls, MR is negative and demand is inelastic.", "recap": "TR = P × Q, AR = price, MR = ΔTR ÷ ΔQ; MR is positive where demand is elastic, zero at unit elasticity, negative where inelastic."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.3.2", "kind": "method", "why": "Cost calculations and the link between MC and average costs appear in multiple-choice and short-answer questions, and the cost curves underpin profit-maximisation diagrams.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Types of cost", "b": "**Fixed costs** (FC) do not change with output, such as rent. **Variable costs** (VC) rise with output, such as materials. **Total cost** is TC = FC + VC. Fixed costs exist only in the **short run**, when at least one factor is fixed."}, {"h": "The formulas", "b": "**Average total cost**: ATC = TC ÷ Q. Also AFC = FC ÷ Q and AVC = VC ÷ Q, so ATC = AFC + AVC. **Marginal cost**: MC = ΔTC ÷ ΔQ. Fixed costs do not change, so ΔTC = ΔVC."}, {"h": "Short-run shapes", "b": "With a fixed factor, **diminishing marginal returns** set in, so MC falls and then rises. AVC and ATC are U-shaped. **MC cuts AVC and ATC at their lowest points**. If MC is below the average, the average falls. If above, it rises. AFC keeps falling."}, {"h": "Long-run shapes", "b": "In the long run all factors vary. The **long-run average cost** curve is U-shaped. It falls due to **economies of scale** and rises due to **diseconomies of scale**. The lowest point is the minimum efficient scale. The cause is scale, not diminishing returns."}], "example": {"task": "A firm has fixed costs of £60. Total variable cost is £66 at 3 units and £88 at 4 units. Find the MC of the 4th unit and ATC at 3 and 4 units. Is ATC falling?", "steps": [{"do": "MC of the 4th unit = ΔTVC ÷ ΔQ = (£88 − £66) ÷ 1 = £22.", "why": "MC is the extra cost of one more unit. Fixed costs do not change, so only TVC matters."}, {"do": "TC at 3 units = £60 + £66 = £126.", "why": "Add fixed costs to variable costs to get total cost."}, {"do": "ATC at 3 units = £126 ÷ 3 = £42.", "why": "Average cost is total cost divided by output."}, {"do": "TC at 4 units = £60 + £88 = £148. ATC = £148 ÷ 4 = £37.", "why": "Same method at the new output level."}, {"do": "ATC falls from £42 to £37, because MC (£22) is below ATC (£42).", "why": "A marginal cost below the average pulls the average down."}], "answer": "MC of the 4th unit is £22. ATC is £42 at 3 units and £37 at 4 units, so ATC is falling because MC is below it."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why can the MC of the 4th unit be found from the change in TVC alone, ignoring the £60 fixed cost?", "o": ["Fixed costs are included in average cost only, never in total cost", "Fixed costs do not change with output, so ΔTC equals ΔTVC", "Marginal cost is defined as a variable cost per unit sold", "Fixed costs are zero in the short run"], "a": 1, "x": "", "f": ["Fixed costs are part of total cost; they just do not change as output rises.", "Fixed costs stay the same at every output, so the whole change in TC comes from variable costs.", "MC is defined as ΔTC ÷ ΔQ, not as a cost per unit sold; it equals ΔTVC only because FC is constant.", "Fixed costs exist in the short run and are not zero; they simply do not vary with output."]}, "faded": {"task": "A firm has fixed costs of £100. TVC is £150 at 5 units and £186 at 6 units. Find the MC of the 6th unit and ATC at 6 units.", "given": ["TC at 5 units = £100 + £150 = £250.", "ATC at 5 units = £250 ÷ 5 = £50."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["MC of the 6th unit = £286 ÷ 6", "MC of the 6th unit = £186 ÷ 6 = £31", "MC of the 6th unit = £186 − £150 = £36", "MC of the 6th unit = £250 − £186 = £64"], "a": 2, "f": ["That divides total cost by output, which gives average total cost, not marginal cost.", "That divides TVC by output, giving AVC; MC is the change in cost for the extra unit.", "MC = ΔTVC ÷ ΔQ = (£186 − £150) ÷ 1 = £36. Fixed costs do not change, so they drop out.", "That subtracts figures from different outputs in the wrong way; take the change in the same type of cost."]}, "trap": "Confusing marginal and average cost. MC is the change in total cost for one more unit; AC is total cost divided by output. Always check which one is asked.", "recap": "MC = ΔTC ÷ ΔQ and ATC = TC ÷ Q; MC cuts the average curves at their minimum points."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.3.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Economies and diseconomies of scale appear in short-answer and essay questions on costs, market structure and competition policy, often needing you to name a type and explain its effect on LRAC.", "hook": {"q": "A small toy shop doubles in size and buys twice as much stock from its supplier. What might happen to the price it pays per toy?", "o": ["It may fall, because the supplier offers a bulk discount", "It must rise, because the shop is bigger", "It stays the same whatever the order size", "It becomes zero for extra toys"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: bulk discounts lower unit costs. This is a purchasing economy of scale.", "Not necessarily: larger orders usually earn discounts, so unit price tends to fall.", "Suppliers often reward bigger orders, so price may well change.", "Prices do not fall to zero; discounts only reduce unit cost somewhat."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Internal economies of scale", "b": "**Internal economies of scale** are falls in long-run average cost as a firm itself grows. Types: **technical** (larger, efficient machines), **purchasing** (bulk discounts), **financial** (cheaper borrowing), **managerial** (specialist managers), **marketing** (advertising spread over more output) and **risk-bearing** (diversifying)."}, {"h": "External economies of scale", "b": "**External economies of scale** lower average costs for all firms because the industry grows or clusters in an area. Examples: shared infrastructure, skilled labour pools and specialist suppliers. As at November 2026, Harwell Campus in Oxfordshire hosts over 100 space companies sharing dedicated infrastructure. The firm's own size is not the cause."}, {"h": "Diseconomies of scale", "b": "**Diseconomies of scale** raise LRAC as output grows. Internal causes: communication problems, poor coordination and worker alienation. External causes: congestion and rising local wage or rent costs as an industry clusters. On the LRAC curve, economies mean a downward slope and diseconomies an upward slope."}, {"h": "Minimum efficient scale", "b": "**Minimum efficient scale (MES)** is the lowest output at which LRAC is at its minimum. If MES is small relative to market demand, many firms can be efficient. If MES is large relative to demand, few firms survive, and a **natural monopoly** may arise."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how easyJet could lower average costs by retiring smaller planes for larger ones, and why this is an internal economy.", "steps": [{"do": "Identify the type: replacing small A319 planes with larger aircraft is a technical economy of scale.", "why": "Naming the exact type shows precise knowledge."}, {"do": "Explain the mechanism: a larger plane carries more passengers, and fixed costs such as crew and fuel per flight are spread across more seats.", "why": "Linking the change to cost per unit is the core explanation."}, {"do": "Link to costs: cost per passenger falls, so long-run average cost falls as scale of operation rises.", "why": "Connects the economy to LRAC, as the specification requires."}, {"do": "State why it is internal: the saving comes from easyJet's own decision to grow its fleet, not from industry size.", "why": "This distinguishes internal from external economies."}, {"do": "Add context: in May 2026 easyJet announced it would retire all A319s by FY29, targeting roughly £250 million in annual cost efficiencies across FY27 and FY28 (company target).", "why": "Using the real figure with its date grounds the point."}], "answer": "Larger aircraft are a technical economy of scale. Fixed costs per flight are spread over more passengers, lowering LRAC. It is internal because it follows from easyJet's own growth."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the saving is internal?", "o": ["Because the fall in cost results from the firm's own decision and size", "Because the cost saving is spread across the whole airline industry", "Because the planes are bought inside the UK", "Because the saving only occurs in the short run"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: internal economies arise from a single firm's own growth.", "That would be external; here the saving belongs to one firm.", "Where inputs are bought is irrelevant to internal versus external.", "Economies of scale are long-run; scale changes are not short-run."]}, "faded": {"task": "A supermarket chain grows and negotiates cheaper loans because banks see it as lower risk. Identify the economy and explain.", "given": ["Step 1: The saving is on borrowing costs, so it is a financial economy of scale."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["Explain that larger firms are seen as lower risk, so pay lower interest, reducing average cost", "Explain that lots of supermarkets locating together share a labour pool", "Explain that its machines are larger and cut unit costs", "Explain that costs rise because managers communicate badly"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: lower interest on borrowing lowers costs per unit as the firm grows, and it is internal.", "That describes an external economy, which is not the cause here.", "That is a technical economy, not the financial economy identified.", "That is a diseconomy of scale, but costs fall in this case."]}, "trap": "Do not call any cost fall an economy of scale. Check whether it arises from the firm's own growth (internal) or industry size (external), and whether it is long-run.", "recap": "Internal economies come from one firm's growth, external from the industry's size; both lower LRAC, while MES is the lowest output reaching minimum LRAC."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.3.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Profit maximisation, types of profit and the shutdown decision appear in short-answer and data-response questions, and underpin analysis of firms' behaviour in every market structure.", "hook": {"q": "A toy shop's revenue just covers all its costs, including the wage the owner could earn working elsewhere. Will the owner probably keep the shop open in the long run?", "o": ["Yes, because the owner earns as much as the next best use of their time", "No, because there is no profit left over at all", "Only if the price of toys rises", "No, because a firm needs supernormal profit to stay open"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Opportunity cost is covered, so staying is as good as the next best option. This is normal profit.", "Wrong. Costs include the owner's opportunity cost, so there is a profit that is just enough to stay.", "Wrong. A price rise is not needed; the current position already keeps the owner in the market.", "Wrong. Supernormal profit is above the minimum; normal profit alone is enough to stay."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Profit maximising output", "b": "A firm maximises profit where **MC = MR**, with MC cutting MR from below. If MR > MC, an extra unit adds more to revenue than to cost, so output rises. If MC > MR, an extra unit reduces profit, so output falls."}, {"h": "Normal and supernormal profit", "b": "**Normal profit** is the minimum profit needed to keep a firm in its current line of production. It covers opportunity cost, and occurs where AR = AC. **Supernormal profit** is profit above normal, where AR > AC. Its area is (AR − AC) × Q."}, {"h": "Losses", "b": "A firm makes a **loss** where AR < AC at its output. The loss is shown by the area (AC − AR) × Q. Total revenue is below total cost, so the firm earns less than normal profit."}, {"h": "The shutdown rule", "b": "In the **short run**, a firm shuts down if AR < **AVC**, because it cannot cover even its variable costs. If AR ≥ AVC it keeps producing and contributes towards fixed costs. In the **long run**, it exits if AR < AC."}], "example": {"task": "At 100 units a firm has AR £10, AVC £8 and AC £12. Explain why it may continue producing in the short run.", "steps": [{"do": "AR (£10) is below AC (£12), so the firm makes a loss of (£12 − £10) × 100 = £200.", "why": "Identifies the loss using the area (AC − AR) × Q."}, {"do": "AR (£10) is above AVC (£8), so revenue covers all variable costs.", "why": "The short-run shutdown test compares AR with AVC."}, {"do": "Each unit adds £2 above its variable cost, a total contribution of £200 towards fixed costs.", "why": "Shows that producing still helps pay fixed costs."}, {"do": "Fixed costs are (£12 − £8) × 100 = £400 and are paid even at zero output. Closing loses £400; producing loses £200.", "why": "Compares the two losses to show producing is better."}, {"do": "So it continues in the short run. If AR stays below AC in the long run, it should exit.", "why": "Conclusion, distinguishing short run from long run."}], "answer": "The firm makes a £200 loss as AR is below AC. AR exceeds AVC, so it earns £200 towards fixed costs of £400. Closing would lose £400, so it continues in the short run, but exits in the long run if AR stays below AC."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example compare AR with AVC rather than AC to decide on short-run shutdown?", "o": ["Fixed costs must be paid even if output is zero, so only variable costs can be avoided", "AVC is always higher than AC, so it is the stricter test", "Profit is maximised where AR = AVC", "Fixed costs do not count as costs in the short run"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Fixed costs are unavoidable in the short run, so the choice depends on whether revenue covers avoidable variable costs.", "Wrong. AVC is always below AC, because AC includes fixed cost per unit.", "Wrong. Profit is maximised where MC = MR; AR = AVC is only the shutdown point.", "Wrong. Fixed costs are real costs; they are just unavoidable in the short run."]}, "faded": {"task": "At 50 units a firm has AR £5, AVC £6 and AC £9. Should it continue in the short run?", "given": ["Loss per unit is £9 − £5 = £4, so the total loss is £4 × 50 = £200.", "AR (£5) is below AVC (£6), so revenue does not cover variable costs."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Continue; revenue of £250 is positive, so some fixed costs are covered", "Shut down; producing loses £200 but closing loses only the £150 fixed costs", "Continue; the £200 loss is smaller than the £450 total cost", "Shut down only in the long run, because AR is below AC rather than AVC"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Revenue of £250 is less than variable costs of £300, so it covers none of the fixed costs.", "Right. Fixed costs are (£9 − £6) × 50 = £150. Producing loses £200, closing loses £150, so shut down.", "Wrong. Comparing the loss with total cost is meaningless. The comparison is with fixed costs lost on closing.", "Wrong. AR below AVC is already the short-run shutdown condition."]}, "trap": "Shutting down whenever AR < AC. In the short run, compare AR with AVC. Only if AR < AVC should the firm close; the long-run test is AR < AC.", "recap": "Maximise profit where MC = MR; shut down in the short run if AR < AVC, and leave in the long run if AR < AC."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Efficiency is used to evaluate every market structure, and it appears in short-answer and essay questions that ask whether competition or monopoly is better for consumers.", "hook": {"q": "A monopoly faces no close rivals. Compared with a firm facing fierce competition, what is it most likely to do with its costs?", "o": ["Keep them lower, because it is bigger", "Let them drift above the minimum, because there is little pressure to cut them", "Keep them exactly the same, because costs are fixed by technology", "Always cut them, because it earns supernormal profit"], "a": 1, "f": ["Size can give economies of scale, but lack of rivals removes the pressure to keep costs low.", "Right. Without competitive pressure, management has less reason to control costs. This is called X-inefficiency.", "Costs are not fixed. Management effort and control change them.", "Supernormal profit removes the need to cut costs. It does not force a firm to cut them."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Allocative efficiency", "b": "**Allocative efficiency** occurs where price equals marginal cost (P = MC). The value consumers place on the last unit equals the cost of the resources used to make it. If P > MC, society values more output than is produced, so the firm underproduces."}, {"h": "Productive efficiency", "b": "**Productive efficiency** occurs where a firm produces at the lowest point of its average cost curve, where MC = AC. No more output can be made at a lower cost per unit. Producing left of this point means unused capacity and higher unit costs."}, {"h": "Dynamic and X-inefficiency", "b": "**Dynamic efficiency** is a long-run gain from investment and innovation that lowers costs or improves products. In 2024, total UK R&D spending reached £79.4 billion (ONS, released 7 August 2026). **X-inefficiency** is when weak competition lets average costs sit above the minimum possible."}, {"h": "Comparing market structures", "b": "Perfect competition: allocatively and productively efficient in the long run, but little supernormal profit for R&D. **Monopoly**: P > MC and output below minimum AC, with X-inefficiency possible. Supernormal profit may fund R&D, so dynamic efficiency is possible. Monopolistic competition: P > MC and excess capacity."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a profit-maximising monopoly is allocatively inefficient.", "steps": [{"do": "The monopoly produces where MR = MC to maximise profit.", "why": "This sets the output the firm chooses. It is the starting point for the analysis."}, {"do": "It reads the price from the AR (demand) curve at that output. This price is above MC.", "why": "Demand slopes downward, so AR lies above MR. Price is therefore above MC."}, {"do": "Since P > MC, the value consumers place on the last unit exceeds its cost.", "why": "This links the price gap to marginal benefit and marginal cost."}, {"do": "Output is below the level where P = MC, so there is underproduction and a welfare loss.", "why": "This shows the consequence: resources are not allocated to maximise welfare."}], "answer": "A monopoly produces where MR = MC but charges the price on the AR curve, which is above MC. Because P > MC, consumers value the last unit more than it costs. Output is below the P = MC level, causing a welfare loss and allocative inefficiency."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the monopoly's price end up above MC at the profit-maximising output?", "o": ["Price is read from the AR curve, which lies above the MR curve", "MR = MC means price must equal MC", "Monopolies always set price equal to average cost", "The MC curve always lies below the AC curve"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. With downward-sloping demand, MR is below AR. At MR = MC the price on AR is therefore above MC.", "MR = MC is not P = MC. That only holds when MR = AR, which is perfect competition.", "Price equal to AC would give normal profit only. A monopoly charges what demand allows at its chosen output.", "MC lies below AC only while AC is falling. This does not explain the price gap."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a perfectly competitive firm is productively efficient in the long run.", "given": ["The firm is a price taker, so AR = MR = P.", "Free entry and exit remove supernormal profit in the long run, so AR = AC."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Profit maximisation at MC = MR means MC = AR = AC, so MC cuts AC at its minimum", "MR = MC means P = MC, so the firm is productively efficient", "AR above AC gives the firm supernormal profit to reinvest", "The firm moves right of minimum AC to gain economies of scale"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. MC = AC only at the lowest point of AC. So output is at minimum AC, which is productive efficiency.", "P = MC shows allocative efficiency. Productive efficiency needs MC = AC at the lowest point.", "This contradicts the previous step. In the long run AR = AC, so supernormal profit is zero.", "Beyond minimum AC, diseconomies of scale start. The firm stays at the lowest point."]}, "trap": "Do not say perfect competition is efficient in every way. It is allocatively and productively efficient in the long run, but low profits mean it is not dynamically efficient.", "recap": "Allocative efficiency is P = MC, productive efficiency is lowest AC, dynamic efficiency is long-run innovation, and X-inefficiency is slack costs caused by weak competition."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Perfect competition is the benchmark model. Exam questions ask you to explain its features and to show short-run and long-run equilibrium on a diagram, then compare other markets with it.", "hook": {"q": "Many farmers sell identical wheat to many buyers who know the market price. One farmer asks for 5% more than that price. What do you expect?", "o": ["Buyers pay it, because wheat is a necessity", "Buyers turn to other farmers, so she sells nothing", "Other farmers copy her and the price rises", "She sells a bit less, as demand is inelastic"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wheat may be a necessity, but buyers can get identical wheat at the lower price elsewhere.", "Right. Identical goods and full information mean buyers switch to the market price. She is a price taker.", "One small farmer cannot lead the market. Others gain by selling at the existing price.", "Her own demand is perfectly elastic, not inelastic, because there are perfect substitutes."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The features", "b": "Many **buyers and sellers**, a **homogeneous product**, **perfect information**, and **freedom of entry and exit**. Firms are **price takers** and profit maximisers. The AHDB reported May-2027 UK feed wheat futures at £198.75 per tonne in the week ending 6 November 2026: farmers accept the market price."}, {"h": "Short-run equilibrium", "b": "The firm's demand curve is horizontal at the market price, so **D = AR = MR**. It maximises profit where **MC = MR**. If AR > AC it earns **supernormal profit**; AR = AC gives **normal profit**; AR < AC is a loss. It shuts down only if AR < **AVC**."}, {"h": "Long-run equilibrium", "b": "Supernormal profit attracts **entry**. Industry supply shifts right, price falls and profit shrinks. Losses cause **exit**, supply shifts left and price rises. The process stops at normal profit, where **P = MC = minimum AC**."}, {"h": "Efficiency", "b": "In the long run, P = MC gives **allocative efficiency**, and output at minimum AC gives **productive efficiency**. Both are benchmarks for judging other market structures. The model's assumptions are strict, so real markets only come close."}], "example": {"task": "Explain, using a diagram, why a perfectly competitive firm making supernormal profit in the short run earns only normal profit in the long run.", "steps": [{"do": "Draw the firm: horizontal D=AR=MR at price P1, output Q1 where MC cuts MR. AC at Q1 lies below P1.", "why": "This shows supernormal profit: the rectangle (P1 − AC) × Q1."}, {"do": "State that there are no barriers to entry and that perfect information tells other firms about the profit.", "why": "These features make entry possible and rapid."}, {"do": "New firms enter, so industry supply shifts right and market price falls.", "why": "More sellers of an identical product increase market supply."}, {"do": "The firm's horizontal demand curve shifts down. Entry continues until price equals AC.", "why": "Supernormal profit is the incentive, so entry stops when it is gone."}, {"do": "In the long run the firm produces at minimum AC, where P = AC = MC, earning normal profit.", "why": "This gives the final equilibrium and links to efficiency."}], "answer": "Supernormal profit attracts entry because there are no barriers. Supply shifts right, so price falls and the firm's AR=MR line moves down. Entry stops when P = AC, at minimum AC. The firm then earns only normal profit."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the industry supply curve shifts right when supernormal profit exists?", "o": ["Because existing firms raise output along their MC curves at a fixed price", "Because supernormal profit attracts new firms, and entry is free", "Because costs fall once the firm makes supernormal profit", "Because demand rises when profits are high"], "a": 1, "f": ["Output rising along existing MC curves is a movement, not the long-run shift. The shift comes from new firms.", "Right. Supernormal profit is the signal, and no barriers let new firms enter, so market supply increases.", "Supernormal profit does not lower costs. Entry changes price, not the AC curve.", "Profit does not shift demand. The adjustment works through supply."]}, "faded": {"task": "A market is in long-run equilibrium. Market demand then rises. Explain how the market returns to long-run equilibrium.", "given": ["Higher demand raises the market price above each firm's AC.", "Each firm now earns supernormal profit at its MC=MR output."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Firms raise their own prices to keep the profit", "New firms enter because there are no barriers, so industry supply shifts right", "Each firm's demand curve becomes downward sloping", "Firms leave until price rises above minimum AC"], "a": 1, "f": ["Price takers cannot set their own price. The market price changes through supply and demand.", "Right. Entry is free and profit signals are known, so supply shifts right and price falls back towards minimum AC.", "Demand stays horizontal for each price taker. A downward slope would imply market power.", "Exit happens with losses. Here there is supernormal profit, so firms enter, not leave."]}, "trap": "Do not say a loss-making firm always shuts down. In the short run it continues if AR ≥ AVC. In the long run, losses lead to exit and supernormal profit leads to entry.", "recap": "In perfect competition, free entry and exit remove supernormal profit and losses, leaving normal profit at P = MC = minimum AC."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Monopolistic competition appears in 9EC0 as short-answer and diagram questions, and as evidence in market structure essays comparing efficiency and outcomes.", "hook": {"q": "Many cafés in a town sell similar but not identical coffee, and all make high profits. Over time, what do you expect?", "o": ["Existing cafés raise prices further, as profits are high", "New cafés open, so each café's customers and profit fall", "Cafés close, because high profit shows the market is too small", "Nothing changes, because each café sells a different product"], "a": 1, "f": ["Extra customers per café are limited; new rivals will attract customers away, so prices do not keep rising.", "Right. Free entry draws in new firms, taking customers from existing ones and reducing each firm's profit.", "High profit encourages entry, not exit; firms leave when they make losses.", "Products are only differentiated, not unique, so close substitutes can still enter and compete."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Key features", "b": "There are **many firms** selling **differentiated products**, with **low barriers to entry and exit**. Firms use **non-price competition** such as branding and quality. Each is a price maker with a downward-sloping, fairly elastic demand (AR) curve, because close substitutes exist."}, {"h": "Short-run equilibrium", "b": "The firm produces where **MR=MC** to maximise profit, then reads price from the AR curve. If AR is above AC at that output, it earns **supernormal profit**. If AR is below AC, it makes a loss, and stays open only if AR covers average variable cost."}, {"h": "Long-run equilibrium", "b": "Supernormal profit attracts **entry**, which shifts each firm's demand left and makes it more elastic. Losses cause **exit**, which shifts demand right. This continues until AR is tangent to AC, so AR=AC and firms earn **normal profit**."}, {"h": "Efficiency and variety", "b": "In the long run the tangency is on the falling part of AC, so output is below minimum AC: **excess capacity**. Also P>MC, so there is no allocative efficiency. Benefits include variety. For example, in FY 2025/26 John Lewis launched 200 new brands."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a monopolistically competitive firm earns only normal profit in the long run.", "steps": [{"do": "Start with the short run: the firm produces where MR=MC and, if AR exceeds AC, earns supernormal profit.", "why": "This sets the starting point that creates the incentive to enter."}, {"do": "Barriers to entry are low, so the supernormal profit attracts new firms into the market.", "why": "Free entry is the feature that drives long-run adjustment."}, {"do": "New firms sell close substitutes, so each existing firm loses customers: its demand curve shifts left and becomes more elastic.", "why": "This links entry to the change in the AR curve."}, {"do": "Entry continues until AR falls to equal AC at the MR=MC output, so supernormal profit is zero.", "why": "This identifies the stopping point."}, {"do": "With AR=AC, the firm earns normal profit, just enough to stay in the market, so there is no further entry or exit.", "why": "This states the equilibrium conclusion."}], "answer": "Supernormal profit attracts entry because barriers are low. New rivals' substitutes shift each firm's demand left and make it more elastic. Entry stops when AR=AC at the MR=MC output, leaving only normal profit."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say entry shifts an existing firm's demand curve to the left?", "o": ["Because entry raises the firm's costs, so AC shifts up", "Because the firm decides to cut output to raise price", "Because new firms sell close substitutes and take away part of its customers", "Because consumers' incomes fall when more firms enter the market"], "a": 2, "f": ["Entry changes demand for each firm; it does not by itself raise costs.", "The firm's profit-maximising output is read off MR=MC; entry moves the curve and is not the firm's own choice.", "Right. Close substitutes divert customers, so the quantity demanded at each price falls for each existing firm.", "Incomes are not affected by entry; the demand shift comes from a smaller market share for each firm."]}, "faded": {"task": "A hairdresser in monopolistic competition makes a short-run loss, with AR below AC at every output. Explain the long-run adjustment.", "given": ["Firms in the market are making subnormal profit.", "Barriers to exit are low, so some firms leave."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Remaining firms' demand shifts right and becomes less elastic, until AR=AC", "Remaining firms' demand shifts left, so their losses increase", "The AC curve shifts down automatically until it touches AR", "Remaining firms raise output to the minimum-AC level, restoring efficiency"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Fewer substitutes and more customers each raise demand and make it less elastic. Exit continues until AR=AC and normal profit.", "Exit reduces competition, so demand shifts right, not left; left is the effect of entry.", "AC does not move because of exit. The adjustment comes from the AR curve shifting.", "The equilibrium is at the MR=MC output on the tangency, which is below minimum AC; it does not move to the minimum."]}, "trap": "Do not say long-run monopolistic competition is efficient like perfect competition. Tangency lies left of minimum AC and P>MC, so there is excess capacity and no allocative efficiency.", "recap": "Entry and exit shift AR until AR=AC, giving normal profit in the long run, with P>MC and excess capacity."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Oligopoly appears in short-answer and extended questions, often asking you to explain collusion, game theory or non-price competition using a payoff matrix or market-share data.", "hook": {"q": "Two petrol stations face each other on one road. One cuts its price by 3p. What is the most likely response from the other?", "o": ["It ignores the cut because its own price is unaffected", "It matches or beats the cut to avoid losing customers", "It raises its price to show it is higher quality", "It leaves the market at once"], "a": 1, "f": ["Ignoring the cut would lose it customers, so a rival with few competitors will usually respond.", "Right. With few rivals, each firm's decision affects the other, so firms react. This is interdependence.", "Raising price after a rival cuts would lose even more customers, so this is rarely the typical reaction.", "Firms rarely exit over a small price cut; they respond with their own pricing first."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Features and concentration", "b": "An **oligopoly** has a few large firms that dominate the market, so firms are **interdependent**. Dominance is measured by the **concentration ratio**: the combined market share of the top n firms. Three-firm ratio = (sales of top three ÷ total market sales) × 100."}, {"h": "Barriers to entry", "b": "High **barriers to entry** protect oligopolists' profits. Examples include **economies of scale**, strong **branding**, high start-up costs, control of distribution and **sunk costs**. They let firms earn **supernormal profit** in the long run, because new firms cannot easily enter."}, {"h": "Collusion", "b": "**Collusion** means firms act together to limit competition, for example by fixing prices or output. It can be formal (a **cartel**) or tacit. It raises joint profit, but each member can gain by **cheating**, so it is unstable. Collusion is generally illegal in the UK."}, {"h": "Game theory and competition", "b": "**Game theory** studies strategic choices between interdependent firms. In a **prisoners' dilemma**, each firm's **dominant strategy** leads both to a worse outcome than cooperation. **Price competition** invites matching and price wars. **Non-price competition** uses branding, quality and service."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why firms in an oligopoly may prefer non-price competition to price competition.", "steps": [{"do": "State that oligopolies have few large firms, so each is interdependent with its rivals.", "why": "This links the answer to the key feature of the market."}, {"do": "Explain that a price cut is easily seen and quickly matched by rivals.", "why": "Shows the chain: reaction by rivals means no lasting gain in market share."}, {"do": "Explain that if everyone cuts prices, a price war follows and revenue and profit per unit fall for all firms.", "why": "Develops the consequence of price competition."}, {"do": "Explain that branding, advertising and quality are harder to copy quickly, so they build loyalty and protect margins.", "why": "Links non-price competition to the benefit, completing the contrast."}], "answer": "Few large, interdependent firms notice and match price cuts, so no share is gained and a price war lowers profits. Non-price competition, such as branding or quality, is harder to copy quickly, builds loyalty and protects margins."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say rivals are likely to match a price cut?", "o": ["Because the products are identical and consumers have perfect information", "Because barriers to entry stop any rival from setting its own price", "Because there are few firms, so each notices and reacts to the others' decisions", "Because demand for each firm's product is perfectly elastic"], "a": 2, "f": ["Identical products with perfect information describe perfect competition, not oligopoly.", "Barriers to entry affect new firms, not how existing rivals respond to a price cut.", "Right. With few firms, interdependence means each firm sees and responds to rivals' price changes.", "Perfectly elastic demand is a perfect competition feature; oligopoly firms face downward-sloping demand."]}, "faded": {"task": "Two firms each choose Advertise or Not. Profits (£m, A then B): neither 8, 8; both 5, 5; only A advertises 11, 3; only B advertises 3, 11. Explain why both may advertise.", "given": ["If B does not advertise, A earns £11m by advertising instead of £8m.", "If B advertises, A earns £5m by advertising instead of £3m."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["A should not advertise, as both firms would then earn £8m, which is a stable outcome", "Advertising is A's dominant strategy, and the same is true for B, so both advertise and earn £5m each", "The firms will collude, so both advertise and each earns £11m", "A advertises only if B does not, so the outcome is uncertain"], "a": 1, "f": ["Not advertising is best jointly, but it is not stable because each firm gains by deviating.", "Right. Advertising is better whatever the rival does, so both do it and end up with £5m, worse than £8m.", "£11m is earned only when one firm advertises alone; both advertising gives £5m each.", "A earns more by advertising in both cases, so the choice is not uncertain; it is a dominant strategy."]}, "trap": "Treating the concentration ratio as an average or dividing by the wrong total. Always add the top n sales, divide by total market sales, then multiply by 100.", "recap": "Few interdependent firms, high barriers and rivals' reactions explain collusion's instability, game-theory outcomes and why non-price competition is often preferred."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.5", "kind": "idea", "why": "Monopoly questions in Paper 1 and Paper 3 ask you to explain equilibrium, price discrimination and efficiency, then evaluate costs and benefits, so you need each idea clearly.", "hook": {"q": "A monopolist faces a downward-sloping demand curve. To sell one more unit it must cut the price on all units. Compared with price, its marginal revenue is:", "o": ["Equal to price", "Lower than price", "Higher than price", "Always zero"], "a": 1, "f": ["Equal only for a price taker. A monopolist's price cut applies to every unit, which reduces the revenue gained.", "Right. The extra unit adds its price, but the price cut on earlier units subtracts revenue, so MR is below AR.", "MR would be higher only if cutting price raised the revenue on existing units, which it does not.", "MR is zero only at one point, where revenue is maximised. Generally it is positive and below price."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Monopoly equilibrium", "b": "A monopolist faces the market demand curve, which is its **AR**. **MR** lies below AR. It maximises profit where **MR = MC**, then charges the price on the AR curve at that output. Supernormal profit = (AR − AC) × Q."}, {"h": "Price discrimination", "b": "**Price discrimination** means charging different prices for the same good, for reasons not based on cost. It needs market power, **separate markets**, no resale and different **PED**. The firm charges more where demand is less elastic. Total revenue rises."}, {"h": "Costs and benefits", "b": "Costs: P > MC (**allocative inefficiency**), higher price, lower output, possible **X-inefficiency**. On 15 October 2025 Ofcom fined Royal Mail £21 million for missing 2024/25 delivery targets. Benefits: **economies of scale**, and supernormal profit funding R&D."}, {"h": "Natural monopoly", "b": "A **natural monopoly** has LRAC falling over the whole range of market demand, so one firm is cheapest. Competition would duplicate costs. Yet it can still exploit market power, so **regulation**, such as price caps, is often used."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a profit-maximising monopolist may be allocatively inefficient.", "steps": [{"do": "A monopolist faces a downward-sloping AR curve, so its MR curve lies below AR.", "why": "Selling more needs a lower price on all units, so MR < AR."}, {"do": "It maximises profit by producing at Qm, where MR = MC.", "why": "Below Qm, extra revenue exceeds extra cost. Above Qm, the reverse."}, {"do": "It charges Pm, found by going up from Qm to the AR curve.", "why": "AR shows the highest price consumers will pay for Qm."}, {"do": "At Qm, Pm is above MC, so the last unit is valued more than its cost. Output is below the P = MC level.", "why": "Allocative efficiency needs P = MC. This gap gives a welfare loss."}], "answer": "MR is below AR, so the monopolist produces where MR = MC at Qm. It reads the price from AR at Qm, giving Pm above MC. Since P > MC, output is too low and there is a welfare loss."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer read the price from the AR curve, not the MR curve, at Qm?", "o": ["AR shows the highest price consumers will pay for Qm units", "MR shows the price, but it is always negative at Qm", "The MC curve is the only curve that shows price", "Price is where MR = MC, so it can be read directly there"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. The demand (AR) curve gives the maximum price per unit that consumers are willing to pay for that quantity.", "MR is not a price. It is the extra revenue from one more unit, and it is positive at Qm.", "MC is the cost of the extra unit, not the price charged.", "MR = MC fixes the quantity. The value there is a marginal figure, not the price."]}, "faded": {"task": "Demand is P = 40 − Q (£) and MC = £10 constant. Find the profit-maximising price.", "given": ["MR = 40 − 2Q (MR is twice as steep as linear AR)", "Set MR = MC: 40 − 2Q = 10"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Q = 15; use MR to find price: 40 − 2(15) = £10", "Q = 30; price = 40 − 30 = £10", "Q = 15; substitute into demand: P = 40 − 15 = £25", "Q = 25; price = 40 − 25 = £15"], "a": 2, "f": ["Q = 15 is right, but MR is not the price. Substitute into the demand (AR) curve.", "Solving 40 − 2Q = 10 gives 2Q = 30, so Q = 15, not 30. You have forgotten to divide by 2.", "Right. Solving gives Q = 15. Then the price comes from the AR curve: 40 − 15 = £25.", "Q = 25 does not solve 40 − 2Q = 10. Check the algebra: 2Q = 30."]}, "trap": "Taking the price from the MR or MC curve at MR = MC. Find output from MR = MC, then go up to the AR (demand) curve for the price.", "recap": "A monopolist produces where MR = MC, charges the price on the AR curve at that output, and so sets P above MC."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.6", "kind": "idea", "why": "Monopsony appears in 9EC0 labour-market and buyer-power questions, often with a diagram and a judgement on minimum wages or exploitation of suppliers.", "hook": {"q": "A hospital is the only large employer of nurses in a remote area. Compared with a town with many employers, what wage is it most likely to pay?", "o": ["A higher wage, because it needs to attract scarce nurses", "A lower wage, because nurses have few other places to work", "The same wage, because nurses' skills fix the pay", "A wage that changes every month with sales of its product"], "a": 1, "f": ["Nurses have few alternatives, so the employer does not need to compete the wage up.", "With few alternative employers, workers have little bargaining power, so the employer can pay less. This is monopsony.", "Skills matter, but the number of competing employers affects wages too.", "Wages are linked to the value of workers' output, not monthly sales swings."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What monopsony means", "b": "A **monopsony** is a market where one firm is the main buyer. It can influence the price it pays for labour or inputs. In 2026/27, DHSC confirmed NHS pay awards of 3.3% for Agenda for Change staff, including nurses and midwives, showing a dominant employer. Large buyers also hold **buyer power** over suppliers."}, {"h": "Marginal cost of labour", "b": "A monopsonist faces the whole upward-sloping **labour supply curve**, which is its **average cost of labour**. To hire one more worker it must raise the wage for everyone. So the **marginal cost of labour** curve lies above supply and is steeper."}, {"h": "Wage and employment", "b": "The firm hires where **MRP = MC of labour**. It then pays the wage on the **supply curve** at that quantity, which is below MRP. Compared with a competitive market, **wage and employment are lower**. A minimum wage set between the monopsony and competitive wage can raise employment."}, {"h": "Costs and benefits", "b": "**Costs**: workers or suppliers are paid less, and supplier firms may lose profit or be de-listed. In the Groceries Code Adjudicator's 2026 survey, 15% of suppliers reported abrupt de-listing. **Benefits**: lower input costs and economies of scale, which may mean lower consumer prices if the firm passes them on."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a monopsony employer pays a lower wage and employs fewer workers than in a competitive labour market. (Suppose supply W = 2 + L, MRP = 14 − L.)", "steps": [{"do": "Supply is W = 2 + L, so total cost is 2L + L². This gives MC of labour = 2 + 2L, above the supply curve.", "why": "The firm must raise pay for all workers to hire one more, so MC exceeds the wage."}, {"do": "The firm hires where MRP = MC: 14 − L = 2 + 2L, so L = 4.", "why": "Profit is maximised where the extra revenue from a worker equals the extra cost."}, {"do": "It pays the wage on the supply curve: W = 2 + 4 = £6, not the £10 MC.", "why": "Every worker needs only the wage that attracts 4 workers, so supply sets the pay."}, {"do": "In a competitive market MRP = supply: 14 − L = 2 + L, so L = 6 and W = £8.", "why": "This gives the benchmark for comparison."}, {"do": "So the monopsony hires 4 not 6 workers and pays £6 not £8. Workers are paid below MRP (£10).", "why": "The result is lower wage and employment, with exploitation of workers."}], "answer": "MC of labour lies above supply, so the firm hires where MRP = MC (4 workers) and pays the supply wage (£6). Competition gives 6 workers at £8. So monopsony means lower wage and employment."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example read the wage off the supply curve rather than the MC curve?", "o": ["Supply shows the wage needed to attract 4 workers, which is what each is paid", "MC shows the wage and supply shows revenue, so either could be used", "The supply curve is always above MC for a monopsony", "MRP is the wage, and supply is only used to find the quantity"], "a": 0, "f": ["The supply curve gives the wage needed to attract that many workers, and all workers are paid that wage. MC includes extra pay for existing workers.", "MC is the cost of an extra worker, including higher pay to existing staff. It is not the wage.", "For a monopsony the MC curve is above supply, not below it.", "MRP is the value of a worker's output. Workers are paid below it under monopsony."]}, "faded": {"task": "Suppose supply W = 3 + L and MRP = 15 − L. Find the monopsony wage.", "given": ["Total labour cost = 3L + L², so MC of labour = 3 + 2L", "Set MRP = MC: 15 − L = 3 + 2L"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["L = 4; the wage is £7, read from the supply curve at L = 4", "L = 4; the wage is £11, read from MRP and MC at L = 4", "L = 6; the wage is £9, where MRP meets supply", "L = 4; the wage is £4, the same as the quantity"], "a": 0, "f": ["12 = 3L gives L = 4. The wage is 3 + 4 = £7 from the supply curve.", "£11 is MRP and MC at 4 workers. The wage is read down to supply, not MC or MRP.", "That is the competitive outcome (15 − L = 3 + L). The monopsony sets MRP = MC.", "£4 is the quantity of workers, not the wage. Substitute L = 4 into the supply curve."]}, "trap": "Reading the wage off MC or MRP at the profit-maximising quantity. Go down to the supply curve: that is the wage actually paid.", "recap": "A monopsony hires where MRP = MC of labour, then pays the lower supply-curve wage, so wage and employment are below competitive levels."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.7", "kind": "idea", "why": "Contestability appears in market structure questions on monopoly and oligopoly, often as a 'to what extent' evaluation point about whether firms with market power really abuse it.", "hook": {"q": "A town has only one bus company, but a rival could start running buses at no cost and leave again at no loss. What would you expect the company to do?", "o": ["Charge high prices, since it is the only firm", "Keep prices low to avoid inviting entry", "Raise prices as demand is inelastic", "Stop running buses"], "a": 1, "f": ["Ignores the rival who could enter freely and cheaply.", "Right: the threat of entry disciplines the firm, even with one seller.", "High prices would invite the rival to enter and undercut.", "Nothing in the scenario suggests the firm would leave."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What contestability means", "b": "A **contestable market** has low **barriers to entry and exit**. Firms can enter, compete and leave easily. What matters is the **threat of entry**, not how many firms are in the market now."}, {"h": "Sunk costs", "b": "**Sunk costs** cannot be recovered when a firm leaves, such as specialist advertising or training. High sunk costs make entry risky and weaken the threat. In a perfectly contestable market there are **no sunk costs**."}, {"h": "Hit-and-run entry", "b": "If supernormal profit exists, entrants can use **hit-and-run entry**: enter, take the profit and leave without loss. Incumbents may therefore use **limit pricing**, setting price near average cost to deter entry."}, {"h": "Barriers and evaluation", "b": "Entry barriers include **economies of scale**, legal barriers and brand loyalty. Exit barriers include long contracts and termination fees. Real markets are rarely perfectly contestable, so the discipline on firms is usually partial."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a market with only two firms may still produce outcomes close to competition if it is contestable.", "steps": [{"do": "State that contestable means low barriers to entry and exit and low sunk costs.", "why": "Defines the key term, which earns knowledge marks."}, {"do": "Say that potential entrants can enter and leave at little cost if the firms earn supernormal profit.", "why": "Applies the concept to the two-firm market."}, {"do": "Explain that this hit-and-run entry would erode the profit of the two firms.", "why": "Links entry to the effect on profit: analysis."}, {"do": "Conclude that the firms may cut price towards average cost to deter entry.", "why": "Shows the outcome resembles competition, answering the question."}], "answer": "Low barriers and sunk costs let entrants take supernormal profit and leave, so the two firms limit price towards average cost to deter them. Outcomes can then resemble competition despite few firms."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer focus on potential entrants rather than the two existing firms?", "o": ["Contestability depends on the threat of entry, which disciplines current firms", "Potential entrants are always already in the market", "Two firms can never have market power", "Existing firms always make normal profit"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the threat of entry, not current numbers, constrains pricing.", "Potential entrants are by definition not yet in the market.", "Two firms can have market power; contestability limits its use.", "Existing firms may earn supernormal profit; entry threat reduces it."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how long contracts with high termination fees affect contestability in a market.", "given": ["Long contracts and termination fees are exit barriers.", "Exit barriers make costs of leaving a market higher."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["They make entry riskier, so potential entrants are deterred and contestability falls", "They raise contestability because firms stay longer", "They remove all sunk costs for entrants", "They increase the number of firms immediately"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: if leaving is costly, entry is riskier, so fewer firms threaten incumbents.", "Firms staying longer is not the same as easy entry and exit; the threat weakens.", "Termination fees are a cost that cannot be recovered, so they add to sunk costs.", "Higher exit costs deter entry, so firm numbers are unlikely to rise."]}, "trap": "Do not say a market is uncompetitive just because few firms operate. Always ask how easy entry and exit are, and whether sunk costs are high.", "recap": "A market is contestable when low barriers and sunk costs make the threat of entry discipline firms, whatever the number of firms."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.5.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Labour market questions often ask you to explain why firms hire workers and what shifts demand; this chain of reasoning appears in short and extended answers.", "hook": {"q": "A seaside ice cream kiosk expects a very hot summer. What will it probably do about hiring?", "o": ["Hire more staff, because it expects to sell more ice cream", "Hire fewer staff, because summer is busy", "Keep staff the same, because wages decide hiring", "Pay staff less, because demand for labour has fallen"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: more expected sales means extra workers are worth more to the firm, so labour demand rises.", "Busier trade raises the value of workers' output; hiring fewer reverses the logic.", "Wages matter, but expected sales change the value of workers' output too.", "Labour demand rises with product demand, not falls."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Derived demand", "b": "Firms do not want workers for their own sake. They hire them to produce goods and services. So **labour demand** is a **derived demand**: it depends on the demand for the output the workers produce."}, {"h": "The MRP curve", "b": "**Marginal revenue product (MRP)** is the extra revenue from employing one more worker: MRP = **MPP** × MR. Because of diminishing returns, MPP falls, so MRP slopes down. A profit-maximising firm hires up to where MRP equals the wage. MRP is the firm's labour demand curve."}, {"h": "Movement or shift", "b": "A change in the wage causes a **movement along** the curve. A **shift** needs a change in MRP: product demand or price, productivity (training, technology), or the price of substitute or complementary factors such as capital."}, {"h": "Elasticity of demand", "b": "Wage elasticity of demand for labour is %ΔQ labour ÷ %Δ wage. Demand is more elastic when labour is a large share of costs, substitutes such as machines are available, product demand is elastic, and in the long run."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a fall in consumer demand for new cars may reduce the demand for car assembly workers.", "steps": [{"do": "State that labour demand is a derived demand.", "why": "Defines the key concept the question rests on."}, {"do": "Say lower consumer demand reduces the price or quantity car firms can sell, so marginal revenue falls.", "why": "Links the product market to the firm's revenue."}, {"do": "Explain that MRP = MPP × MR, so MRP of each worker falls.", "why": "Uses the correct formula to show why workers are worth less."}, {"do": "Conclude the labour demand curve shifts left, so at any wage fewer workers are hired.", "why": "Gives the outcome as a shift, not a movement."}], "answer": "Labour demand is derived from product demand. Falling car sales lower marginal revenue, so each worker's MRP falls. The labour demand curve shifts left, so at any wage firms hire fewer assembly workers."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example describe the outcome as a leftward shift rather than a movement along the curve?", "o": ["Because the fall in MRP applies at every wage, not just one", "Because the wage rate has fallen", "Because workers have become less productive", "Because labour supply has increased"], "a": 0, "x": "", "f": ["Right: MRP falls at every wage level, so the whole curve shifts left.", "The wage did not change in the example; a wage change gives a movement along.", "MPP did not change; the fall was in marginal revenue from weaker sales.", "Supply is not involved; it would change the wage, not shift the demand curve."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why new labour-saving technology might raise demand for labour at a warehouse firm.", "given": ["Step 1: Technology raises the marginal physical product (MPP) of each worker."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["MRP = MPP × MR rises, so the labour demand curve shifts right", "The wage falls, so there is a movement along the curve", "MRP falls, so the labour demand curve shifts left", "Labour supply increases, so firms hire more"], "a": 0, "x": "", "f": ["Right: higher MPP raises MRP at each wage, so labour demand shifts right.", "Technology changes MRP, not the wage; a wage change would be a movement along.", "Higher MPP raises MRP; a fall would be the wrong direction.", "Supply is unrelated; the argument is about the value of each worker's output."]}, "trap": "Do not call a wage change a shift. Only changes in MRP (product demand, productivity, prices of other factors) shift the curve; wage changes move along it.", "recap": "Labour demand is derived from product demand and equals the MRP curve; changes in MRP shift it, wage changes move along it."}
{"why": "Supply of labour appears in short-answer and essay questions on wages, unemployment and policy; you must tell movements from shifts and explain immobility.", "s": "micro", "t": "3.5.2", "kind": "idea", "hook": {"q": "A firm in a high-cost city cannot fill vacancies, yet people are unemployed elsewhere in the UK. What is the most likely reason?", "o": ["Workers cannot easily move to where the jobs are", "Unemployed people prefer leisure to any work", "Wages in the city are set above equilibrium", "Unemployed workers receive no state benefits"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: barriers to moving, such as housing costs, are called geographical immobility.", "People who choose leisure are inactive, not unemployed, so this cannot explain the unemployment.", "A wage above equilibrium creates excess labour supply, so the firm would fill its posts easily.", "Benefit levels do not explain a regional mismatch between vacancies and unemployed workers."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Supply to one occupation", "b": "The **supply of labour** to a job is upward sloping: a higher wage attracts more workers from other jobs and from outside the labour force. A wage change causes a **movement along** the curve."}, {"h": "Shifts in supply", "b": "Non-wage factors shift supply: **qualifications and training**, **migration**, working conditions and non-wage benefits, tax and **benefit levels**, and the size of the working-age population. More workers at each wage shifts it right; fewer shifts it left."}, {"h": "Geographical immobility", "b": "**Geographical immobility** is difficulty moving between regions. Causes: high **housing costs**, family and social ties, and poor information. The IMF noted in May 2026 that high housing costs make it harder for UK workers to move to higher-productivity regions."}, {"h": "Occupational immobility", "b": "**Occupational immobility** is difficulty moving between jobs because of lacking skills or qualifications. It causes **structural unemployment** alongside vacancies. Retraining, education and apprenticeships reduce it."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a rise in the number of skilled workers allowed to migrate to the UK might affect the supply of labour in the construction industry.", "steps": [{"do": "State the factor: migration policy changes the number of workers able to work in construction.", "why": "Names a non-wage determinant of labour supply."}, {"do": "Say that more workers are available at each wage rate.", "why": "Links the factor to quantity supplied at every wage."}, {"do": "Conclude that the supply curve shifts right.", "why": "Non-wage changes shift the curve; they are not movements along it."}, {"do": "Link to the market: with demand unchanged, the equilibrium wage falls and employment rises.", "why": "Connects supply to the wage outcome."}], "answer": "Greater skilled migration raises the number of workers available at each wage, shifting supply right. With unchanged demand, the equilibrium wage falls and employment rises."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the change drawn as a shift rather than a movement along the curve?", "o": ["Because the cause is a non-wage factor", "Because the wage has risen", "Because demand has moved", "Because the curve is inelastic"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: migration is not the wage, so it changes supply at every wage.", "Wages are the axis variable; their change would be a movement, which is not the cause here.", "Demand is not mentioned; it is held constant in the answer.", "Elasticity affects steepness, not whether a curve shifts."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a fall in the number of retraining courses might reduce labour supply to the IT industry.", "given": ["Factor: fewer courses mean fewer people gain the qualifications IT firms need."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Fewer workers qualified at each wage, so supply shifts left", "Wages rise, so there is a movement up the curve", "Demand for IT workers falls, so wages fall", "Workers become geographically mobile"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: a non-wage factor reducing qualified workers shifts supply left.", "Wages are not the cause here; a wage change would be a movement, not this shift.", "The courses affect who can supply labour; demand for IT workers is unchanged.", "Missing qualifications are occupational, not geographical, immobility."]}, "trap": "Do not draw a movement along the curve when a non-wage factor changes. Ask: is the cause the wage in this job, or something else?", "recap": "Wage changes move you along labour supply; non-wage factors shift it; immobility, geographical or occupational, stops labour moving to where it is needed."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.5.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Wage determination, minimum wages and elasticity of labour demand appear in short-answer and data-response questions, and often feature in 25-mark essays on labour markets.", "hook": {"q": "A law forces a shop to pay £12 an hour when the market wage is £10. What happens to the quantity of labour supplied and demanded at £12?", "o": ["Both rise, because higher pay helps the shop grow", "Supplied rises and demanded falls, so there is excess supply", "Both fall, because workers dislike government rules", "Supplied falls and demanded rises, so there is a shortage"], "a": 1, "f": ["Higher pay does not make firms want more workers; a higher price of labour reduces quantity demanded.", "Right. More people want jobs at £12, while the firm hires fewer because each worker costs more.", "Workers are more willing to work at a higher wage, so quantity supplied rises, not falls.", "This reverses the movements. A higher wage raises quantity supplied and lowers quantity demanded."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Competitive wage setting", "b": "The demand for labour is a **derived demand**: it comes from the **marginal revenue product (MRP)** of workers. Wages settle where labour demand meets labour supply. Higher productivity or product price shifts demand right. More skilled workers or migration shift supply right."}, {"h": "Minimum and maximum wages", "b": "A **minimum wage** only bites if above equilibrium. It causes **excess supply of labour** (unemployment). The UK National Living Wage was £12.71 from 1 April 2026. A **maximum wage** below equilibrium creates excess demand: a labour shortage."}, {"h": "Elasticity of labour", "b": "**Wage elasticity of demand for labour** = %Δ quantity of labour ÷ %Δ wage. Demand is more inelastic if labour is a small share of costs, substitutes are few, or product demand is inelastic. Supply is more inelastic if skills are specialised or training is long."}, {"h": "Monopsony", "b": "A **monopsony** is a sole or dominant buyer of labour. It hires where marginal cost of labour = MRP, and pays the wage on the supply curve, below the competitive wage. A minimum wage between the two can raise **wages and employment**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a minimum wage set above the equilibrium wage affects a competitive labour market.", "steps": [{"do": "State the starting point: the equilibrium wage is where labour demand equals labour supply, so the market clears.", "why": "Shows the baseline before the floor is introduced."}, {"do": "Say the minimum wage is above equilibrium, so it is binding and the firm must pay at least this wage.", "why": "A floor below equilibrium would have no effect."}, {"do": "Quantity of labour supplied extends along the supply curve, as more people want to work at the higher wage.", "why": "Wage is the price of labour, so this is a movement along."}, {"do": "Quantity of labour demanded contracts along the demand curve, as workers cost more relative to their MRP.", "why": "Firms cut hours or jobs; again a movement along."}, {"do": "The gap is excess supply of labour (unemployment). It is smaller if labour demand is inelastic.", "why": "Links to elasticity and shows analysis."}], "answer": "A binding floor above equilibrium raises the wage, so quantity supplied extends and quantity demanded contracts. This creates excess supply of labour. Job losses are smaller if demand for labour is wage inelastic."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example describe the fall in labour demanded as a movement along the demand curve, not a shift?", "o": ["Because the minimum wage changes workers' productivity", "Because the firm's product demand has fallen", "Because the wage is the price of labour, and a change in price moves us along", "Because the supply curve has shifted left"], "a": 2, "f": ["Productivity changes would shift MRP and demand; a minimum wage does not alter productivity.", "Falling product demand would shift labour demand left, but nothing in the example changes product demand.", "Right. A change in the wage itself causes a movement along the labour demand curve; shifts need other factors to change.", "The supply curve does not shift; the quantity supplied extends along it because of the higher wage."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a maximum wage (pay cap) set below the equilibrium wage affects a competitive labour market.", "given": ["Equilibrium wage is £20 an hour, where labour demand equals labour supply.", "The cap is £16, below equilibrium, so it is binding."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Quantity supplied extends and demanded contracts, causing excess supply (unemployment)", "The labour demand curve shifts right, because firms can afford more workers", "The labour supply curve shifts right, because the cap attracts workers", "Quantity demanded extends and quantity supplied contracts, causing excess demand (a shortage)"], "a": 3, "f": ["This is the minimum-wage result. A cap below equilibrium lowers the wage, so demanded extends and supplied contracts.", "Wage is the price of labour, so a lower wage causes a movement along demand, not a shift.", "A lower wage reduces quantity supplied along the supply curve; it does not shift supply.", "Right. At £16 firms want more workers and fewer people offer labour, giving a shortage of labour."]}, "trap": "Assuming every minimum wage causes unemployment. Check whether the floor is above equilibrium, and whether the employer has monopsony power, which can make a floor raise employment.", "recap": "Wages are set where labour demand meets supply; a floor above equilibrium creates excess supply, smaller if demand is inelastic, but monopsony can change the result."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.6.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions on government intervention in competition ask you to explain how the CMA and regulators act, then judge whether it works, so you need the tools and their limits.", "hook": {"q": "Two mobile firms want to merge. The new firm would have fewer rivals but could afford a bigger network. What should a regulator do first?", "o": ["Block it automatically, because fewer firms is always worse", "Allow it automatically, because bigger firms are always more efficient", "Weigh the loss of competition against possible efficiency gains", "Ignore it, because markets always correct themselves"], "a": 2, "f": ["Blocking automatically ignores possible gains such as investment and lower costs. Regulators judge each case.", "Larger firms are not always more efficient. Diseconomies of scale and higher prices are possible.", "Regulators weigh costs and benefits. They can clear, block or add remedies.", "Markets with high barriers to entry may not self-correct, which is why competition policy exists."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why intervene?", "b": "A firm with **market power** can raise price and restrict output. This causes a loss of **allocative efficiency** and may reduce the incentive to cut costs (**X-inefficiency**). Governments intervene to protect consumers, workers and suppliers, but need to weigh any gains from large firms too."}, {"h": "Controlling mergers", "b": "The **CMA** reviews mergers that could reduce competition. A Phase 1 review can lead to a deeper **Phase 2** inquiry. The CMA can clear a merger, block it, or accept **remedies**, such as legally binding undertakings. Efficiency gains and investment are weighed against higher prices."}, {"h": "Regulating and opening markets", "b": "Regulators can use a **price cap (RPI − X)**, performance targets or profit regulation on monopolies. X is the efficiency gain expected. Governments also promote competition by cutting **barriers to entry**, fining cartels and requiring price transparency. Regulation can fail: **regulatory capture** and poor information are risks."}, {"h": "Workers and suppliers", "b": "A **minimum wage** protects workers. In a competitive labour market a wage above equilibrium may cut employment. In a **monopsony** it can raise both wages and employment. Suppliers can also be protected from powerful buyers. Costs include compliance and possible job losses."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the CMA might accept remedies rather than block a merger between two mobile network firms (for example Vodafone UK and Three UK).", "steps": [{"do": "State the concern: the merger removes a rival, so the CMA ran a Phase 2 inquiry. Reduced competition could mean higher prices and less choice for consumers.", "why": "Starts with the competition problem that justifies intervention."}, {"do": "State the benefit: a larger firm can fund network investment such as 5G rollout. This gives dynamic efficiency and better quality for consumers.", "why": "Shows the other side that the CMA weighs."}, {"do": "Link to remedies: the CMA cleared the merger on 5 December 2024 and on 28 March 2025 accepted binding undertakings, including three-year caps on selected retail tariffs and enforced 5G rollout.", "why": "Applies a real case and explains how remedies combine both aims."}, {"do": "Judge: remedies keep the investment gain while limiting price rises. They need monitoring, and caps last only three years, so the risk returns later.", "why": "A short evaluation shows the limits of the policy."}], "answer": "Remedies let the CMA keep the investment benefits of a merger while limiting the harm from reduced competition. In March 2025 binding undertakings capped selected tariffs for three years and enforced 5G rollout. This is better than blocking if the gains are real, but monitoring is needed."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked answer mention the three-year caps on selected retail tariffs?", "o": ["They limit harm from reduced competition while the investment benefits are delivered", "They permanently remove the merged firm's market power", "They prove the merger reduced the firm's average costs", "They show the CMA has no power to block mergers"], "a": 0, "f": ["The caps are a remedy: they cut the risk of higher prices while the merger delivers its gains.", "The caps last three years and cover selected tariffs. They do not remove market power permanently.", "Price caps say nothing about costs. They constrain prices.", "The CMA can block mergers. Choosing remedies is a judgement, not a lack of power."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how an RPI − X price cap can help consumers of a regulated monopoly.", "given": ["The cap limits how much the firm can raise its average price each year to RPI minus X."], "q": "Which is the best next point?", "o": ["The firm can only raise profit by cutting costs, so it has an incentive to improve productive efficiency", "The firm sets X itself, so it can always choose the level that maximises profit", "The cap makes the firm produce exactly where P = MC, so allocative efficiency is certain", "The cap removes barriers to entry, so the monopoly becomes a perfectly competitive firm"], "a": 0, "f": ["Correct: with prices constrained, extra profit comes from lower costs, which encourages efficiency and can lower future prices.", "The regulator sets X, not the firm. If the firm set it, the cap would not protect consumers.", "The cap does not guarantee P = MC. It limits price rises, so 'certain' overstates the effect.", "A price cap does not change barriers to entry or turn a monopoly into perfect competition."]}, "trap": "Assuming intervention always works or that mergers are simply blocked. Always give a remedy or alternative, then evaluate: costs, poor information, regulatory capture and monitoring problems.", "recap": "The CMA weighs lost competition against efficiency gains, using clearance, blocking or remedies, and you should always evaluate whether regulation works."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.6.2", "kind": "writing", "why": "Evaluate questions on 3.6.2 appear in Paper 1 and Paper 3 essays and ask you to judge whether intervention works, using effects and limits.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Five effects to cover", "b": "Intervention changes prices and profits. It can affect efficiency (allocative and productive), quality and choice. A good answer picks the effects that matter for the context, explains the chain from policy to outcome, and then weighs the **unintended consequences**, such as **government failure**."}, {"h": "Price controls", "b": "A **maximum price** below equilibrium causes excess demand. Supply contracts, quality may fall and a **black market** may appear. A **minimum price** above equilibrium creates a surplus. Producers may gain revenue but consumers pay more and resources may be wasted, since the surplus must be stored or bought."}, {"h": "Taxes and subsidies", "b": "An indirect tax shifts supply left; a **subsidy** shifts it right. Who benefits or pays (incidence) depends on **elasticity**. A subsidy lowers prices and raises output, but costs taxpayers money, with an **opportunity cost**."}, {"h": "Limits and evaluation", "b": "Limits include **government failure**: imperfect information, **unintended consequences**, enforcement costs and time lags. Regulators may also be weak against firms with market power. Evaluate by magnitude, elasticity, time period and cost, then reach a supported judgement."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate the effectiveness of regulatory fines in improving the quality of a service supplied by a firm with market power.", "steps": [{"do": "Fines raise the cost of poor quality, so a firm with market power may improve its service if the fine exceeds the cost of doing so.", "why": "Knowledge and analysis: states the mechanism linking the policy to quality."}, {"do": "This could raise consumer welfare, because a firm with few competitors has little other incentive to improve service.", "why": "Analysis: connects market power to why intervention is needed."}, {"do": "Yet in 2025/26 Royal Mail delivered only 90.2% of Second Class mail within three working days, below its 98.5% target, despite over £37 million in past Ofcom fines.", "why": "Application: real evidence is used to question the policy."}, {"do": "So fines alone seem weak; they depend on size, monitoring and competition. Overall, they help but are probably insufficient on their own.", "why": "Evaluation: gives conditions and a justified judgement."}], "answer": "Fines raise the cost of poor quality, so firms may improve. Yet in 2025/26 Royal Mail delivered only 90.2% of Second Class mail within three working days, below its 98.5% target, despite over £37 million in past Ofcom fines. So fines alone are weak without tougher penalties or competition."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 quote the Royal Mail figures?", "o": ["Every essay needs a real figure to gain any marks", "It applies evidence showing fines did not secure the target, supporting the evaluation", "It proves that regulatory fines never work", "It defines what a regulatory fine is"], "a": 1, "f": ["Marks reward relevant application, not figures for their own sake. Evidence must be used to support a point.", "Right. The data applies the idea to a real firm and shows a limit of the policy, which earns application and evaluation marks.", "One example does not prove 'never'. The answer says fines seem weak alone, a measured judgement.", "Defining the fine belongs to step 1. This step uses data as evidence."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate the use of a maximum price (rent control) in the market for rented flats in a city.", "given": ["Knowledge: A maximum price is a legal ceiling set below the free-market equilibrium rent.", "Analysis: At the lower rent, quantity demanded extends and quantity supplied contracts, so excess demand arises."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to earn the most marks?", "o": ["Rent control is a bad policy and should be abolished.", "Rents in many cities are high, and many people rent flats.", "A maximum price is a price set by the government.", "Landlords may cut maintenance or withdraw flats, so quality and choice fall."], "a": 3, "f": ["This is an unsupported judgement. The mark scheme rewards a developed chain of reasoning before any conclusion.", "This is a descriptive statement with no link to the policy's effects, so it earns no analysis marks.", "This repeats the definition already given and adds no new analysis or application.", "Right. It extends the chain from lower rents to quality and choice, which is the effect the question asks you to analyse."]}, "trap": "Describing what a policy is meant to do and stopping. Always follow the chain to side effects and add a limit, such as elasticity, cost, time lag or government failure.", "recap": "For each intervention, state the intended effect, trace the unintended effects and costs, then give a judgement that depends on elasticity, size and time."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Globalisation appears in short-answer and essay questions, often as 'explain' or 'evaluate' on causes and effects for different groups. Clear chains of reasoning earn the marks.", "hook": {"q": "A UK clothing factory closes because the same clothes can be made more cheaply abroad. Who is most likely to lose?", "o": ["Shoppers buying clothes", "Factory workers with few other skills", "Overseas workers hired", "Importers of the clothes"], "a": 1, "f": ["Shoppers usually gain from lower prices.", "Right: they face job loss and may struggle to find new work, a structural problem.", "Overseas workers gain jobs.", "Importers gain from supplying cheaper goods."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What it means", "b": "**Globalisation** is growing integration and interdependence of economies. It happens through flows of **goods and services**, **capital**, labour, technology and information across borders."}, {"h": "Main drivers", "b": "**Trade liberalisation** cuts tariffs and quotas. **Transport** and **communication** costs fall. **Transnational corporations** (TNCs) spread production worldwide. Capital becomes more mobile, and emerging economies join world markets."}, {"h": "Gains and losses", "b": "Consumers gain lower prices and choice. Firms gain **economies of scale** and wider markets. Countries gain growth and **FDI**. Losers include workers in declining industries facing **structural unemployment**, and some face wage pressure and widening inequality."}, {"h": "Environment and risk", "b": "More trade and transport raise **carbon emissions**, and some firms may locate where regulation is weak. Close links also create **interdependence**: shocks, such as supply disruption abroad, spread quickly between economies."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how falling transport costs may affect a UK furniture retailer and its UK workers.", "steps": [{"do": "Falling transport costs make it cheaper to ship goods across the world.", "why": "Names the driver of globalisation clearly."}, {"do": "The retailer can import furniture from lower-cost countries, cutting its unit costs.", "why": "Links the driver to the firm's costs."}, {"do": "Lower costs let it cut prices or raise profit margins, improving competitiveness.", "why": "Develops the effect on the firm."}, {"do": "UK furniture makers face stronger competition and may close, so their workers lose jobs.", "why": "Shows the effect on a different group."}, {"do": "Workers may be structurally unemployed if skills and location do not match other vacancies.", "why": "Uses the correct concept to explain persistence."}], "answer": "Cheaper transport lets the retailer import low-cost furniture, cutting costs and prices, but UK manufacturers lose sales and may close, causing structural unemployment."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example call the job losses structural rather than cyclical?", "o": ["They come from a lasting change in where goods are produced, not from a fall in demand", "They happen only during a recession", "They are caused by wages rising", "They disappear when output grows"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the industry itself shrinks, so skills no longer match vacancies.", "Cyclical unemployment is linked to recessions; here the cause is changing production location.", "Rising wages are not the cause given in the example.", "Structural unemployment can persist even when output grows."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a TNC setting up a factory in a developing country could affect that country.", "given": ["The TNC brings FDI, which adds to capital investment in the host country.", "The new factory creates jobs for local workers."], "q": "What is the best next point?", "o": ["Wages and incomes rise, increasing consumption and potentially tax revenue and growth", "The host country's exports must fall", "The TNC's home country gains all the benefits", "Pollution is impossible"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: it follows the chain from jobs to incomes, spending, tax revenue and growth.", "FDI-based production often raises exports; this contradicts the argument.", "The point asked concerns the host country, so this loses focus.", "Environmental costs may occur; an absolute claim is unsupported."]}, "trap": "Do not claim globalisation helps everyone. Name who gains and who loses, and link each effect to a cause, such as lower costs or job losses.", "recap": "Globalisation is rising economic integration driven by liberalisation, technology and TNCs, and its gains and costs fall unevenly across consumers, firms, workers and the environment."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.2", "kind": "method", "why": "Opportunity cost calculations from tables and PPF data appear in 4.1.2 multiple-choice and short-answer questions; evaluation of the assumptions then supports longer answers.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Find opportunity costs", "b": "The **opportunity cost** of good X is the output of Y given up divided by the output of X gained. From a table of **maximum outputs**: OC of X = max Y ÷ max X. Work it out for both goods and both countries."}, {"h": "Absolute or comparative?", "b": "**Absolute advantage**: more output from the same resources. **Comparative advantage**: a lower opportunity cost. A country can have an absolute advantage in both goods, but it cannot have a comparative advantage in both."}, {"h": "Specialise and trade", "b": "Each country specialises in the good with the lower opportunity cost. They trade at **terms of trade** strictly between the two opportunity costs. Both can then consume beyond their own PPF, so **total output** rises."}, {"h": "Assumptions and limits", "b": "The model assumes **constant opportunity costs**, no transport costs, no trade barriers, mobile factors and similar goods. In practice, transport costs, tariffs, structural unemployment and **over-specialisation** limit the gains. Comparative advantage also changes over time. UK services exports rose from £487.2 billion to £522.8 billion in 2025 (ONS)."}], "example": {"task": "Farmland makes 90t potatoes or 30t wheat. Hillside makes 40t potatoes or 20t wheat. Who has the comparative advantage in each good? Give a mutually beneficial price of wheat.", "steps": [{"do": "Farmland: 1 wheat costs 90 ÷ 30 = 3 potatoes. Hillside: 1 wheat costs 40 ÷ 20 = 2 potatoes.", "why": "Opportunity cost of wheat = potatoes given up ÷ wheat gained."}, {"do": "Hillside's cost of wheat (2) is lower than Farmland's (3), so Hillside has the comparative advantage in wheat.", "why": "Lower opportunity cost means comparative advantage."}, {"do": "Farmland: 1 potato costs 30 ÷ 90 = 1/3 wheat. Hillside: 1 potato costs 20 ÷ 40 = 1/2 wheat.", "why": "Check the other good by inverting the ratios."}, {"do": "Farmland's cost of potatoes (1/3) is lower, so it has the comparative advantage in potatoes, despite also having an absolute advantage in both goods.", "why": "Absolute advantage does not decide specialisation."}, {"do": "Terms of trade: 1 wheat should exchange for more than 2 and less than 3 potatoes, for example 2.5.", "why": "Both gain only if the price lies between the two opportunity costs."}], "answer": "Hillside has the comparative advantage in wheat (2 potatoes against 3). Farmland has it in potatoes (1/3 wheat against 1/2). A price of 2.5 potatoes per wheat benefits both."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example compare opportunity costs rather than the maximum outputs of the two countries?", "o": ["Farmland leads in both goods, so only opportunity cost shows where each loses least", "Because the country with the lower output must always import everything", "Because maximum outputs are different units and cannot be compared", "Because opportunity costs are the same in both countries"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: gains depend on what each country gives up. Farmland is better at both goods yet still gains from specialising where its cost is lower.", "Wrong: a country with lower output can still export the good where its opportunity cost is lower.", "Wrong: the units are comparable here (tonnes), and the issue is not units but sacrifice.", "Wrong: the opportunity costs differ (3 against 2 potatoes per wheat), and that difference creates the gains."]}, "faded": {"task": "Country M makes 50 cars or 100 bikes. Country N makes 30 cars or 30 bikes. Find who has the comparative advantage in cars.", "given": ["OC of 1 car in M = 100 ÷ 50 = 2 bikes.", "OC of 1 car in N = 30 ÷ 30 = 1 bike."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["M has it, because it makes more cars (50 against 30)", "M has it, because 2 bikes is the larger opportunity cost", "Neither, because M has an absolute advantage in both goods", "N has it, because 1 bike is a lower opportunity cost than 2 bikes"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong: this uses absolute output. Comparative advantage depends on the lower opportunity cost, not the higher output.", "Wrong: a larger opportunity cost is a disadvantage, not an advantage.", "Wrong: absolute advantage in both goods does not rule out comparative advantage; N's car cost is still lower.", "Right: N gives up 1 bike per car and M gives up 2, so N has the comparative advantage in cars."]}, "trap": "Deciding specialisation by who produces more. Always calculate opportunity costs for both goods; the country with the lower cost specialises in that good, even if it is less productive overall.", "recap": "Comparative advantage means the lower opportunity cost, so specialise there and trade at a price between the two countries' opportunity costs."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Pattern of trade appears in 4.1 explain and evaluate questions, often with data on who the UK trades with and why this changes over time.", "hook": {"q": "Country P is better than country Q at making both wheat and cloth. Can trade still benefit both?", "o": ["No, P gains nothing from buying from a less efficient country", "Yes, if their opportunity costs of the two goods differ", "Yes, but only if Q has an absolute advantage in one good", "No, trade only helps the country with the absolute advantage"], "a": 1, "f": ["P can gain by specialising where its opportunity cost is lower and buying the other good.", "Right: with different opportunity costs each specialises where its cost is lower, and both can gain.", "Absolute advantage is not needed; comparative advantage depends only on opportunity cost.", "This ignores that Q also gains by specialising in its lower opportunity cost good."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Comparative advantage", "b": "A country has a **comparative advantage** when it produces a good at a lower **opportunity cost** than another country. It need not be cheaper or higher in output. Countries gain by specialising where their opportunity cost is lowest, and the pattern of trade follows."}, {"h": "Emerging economies", "b": "**Emerging economies** grow quickly through industrialisation, **foreign direct investment** and rising productivity. Their comparative advantage shifts towards manufactures, so their share of world trade rises. As incomes grow they also become larger export markets, changing who trades with whom."}, {"h": "Trading blocs", "b": "A **free trade area** removes tariffs between members. A **customs union** adds a **common external tariff**. A common market adds free movement of factors. Trade creation shifts buying to efficient members; **trade diversion** shifts it from lower-cost outsiders to dearer members."}, {"h": "Exchange rates", "b": "A fall in the exchange rate makes exports cheaper abroad and imports dearer at home. This shifts the pattern of trade towards exports and domestic goods. The size depends on the **price elasticity of demand** for exports and imports, and takes time."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how the growth of an emerging economy can change the UK's pattern of trade.", "steps": [{"do": "State the cause: the emerging economy industrialises, attracts foreign investment and raises productivity.", "why": "Identifies why its comparative advantage in manufactures grows."}, {"do": "Link to lower costs: its manufactured goods become cheaper relative to UK-made equivalents, so UK imports from it rise.", "why": "Shows the chain from advantage to trade flows."}, {"do": "Support with evidence: the ONS reported July 2026 recorded the highest monthly level of UK car imports from China.", "why": "A dated real example earns application marks."}, {"do": "Add the other side: rising incomes there raise demand for UK services and goods, so it also becomes an export market.", "why": "Shows trade patterns change in both directions."}], "answer": "Growth builds the emerging economy's comparative advantage in manufactures, so UK imports from it rise, as with car imports from China in July 2026. Rising incomes also make it a bigger UK export market."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer add that rising incomes make the emerging economy an export market?", "o": ["Because imports from it must always be matched by equal exports to it", "Because the question asks about changes in both directions of trade, not just imports", "Because higher incomes lower its comparative advantage in manufactures", "Because exports to it automatically cancel out the UK's import growth"], "a": 1, "x": "", "f": ["Trade need not balance bilaterally; this assumes it must.", "Right: a changed pattern includes new export markets as well as higher imports, so the answer covers both.", "Higher incomes do not remove its comparative advantage; the point is about demand for UK goods.", "Nothing cancels automatically; the two flows are separate and balances can differ."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a fall in the value of the pound might change the UK's pattern of trade.", "given": ["A lower pound cuts the foreign-currency price of UK exports.", "Imports priced in foreign currency cost more in pounds."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["UK exports now cost more abroad, so fewer are sold", "Quantity demanded of exports rises and imports fall, by an amount depending on elasticities", "Trade patterns are unchanged because depreciation only affects tourism", "The UK will now trade only with the EU, as other markets rely on pounds"], "a": 1, "x": "", "f": ["This reverses the effect: a lower pound makes UK exports cheaper abroad, not dearer.", "Right: cheaper exports and dearer imports shift trade towards exports and domestic goods, with the size set by price elasticity.", "Depreciation affects all traded goods and services, not only tourism.", "A depreciation does not restrict trade to one bloc; it changes relative prices with all partners."]}, "trap": "Do not say the country with higher output or lower costs in everything has the comparative advantage in everything. Compare opportunity costs good by good.", "recap": "Trade patterns follow comparative advantage, which emerging economies, trading blocs and exchange rates keep changing; always compare opportunity costs, not absolute output."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.4", "kind": "method", "why": "Terms of trade calculations appear in data-response and short-answer questions, and the effects of a change feed into evaluation of the current account and living standards.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "The formula", "b": "The **terms of trade** compare the prices a country gets for its exports with the prices it pays for imports. Terms of trade = (**export price index** ÷ **import price index**) × 100. It is an index, not a money amount."}, {"h": "Reading a change", "b": "A rise is an **improvement**: each unit of exports buys more imports. A fall is a **deterioration**. A change happens when export and import prices move at different rates. Equal percentage rises leave the index unchanged."}, {"h": "What changes it", "b": "The **exchange rate** matters: an appreciation lowers import prices in pounds. Relative **inflation** and productivity change export prices. **Commodity prices**, such as oil, move import prices. Demand conditions and market power also affect export prices."}, {"h": "Effects of a change", "b": "An improvement raises **real income** and lowers import costs, but may reduce export volumes. A deterioration squeezes real income. ONS reported that in Q2 2026 UK real gross domestic income grew 0.2% against real GDP growth of 0.5%, as import prices outpaced export prices."}], "example": {"task": "Between 2024 and 2026 a country's export price index rises from 100 to 112 and its import price index from 100 to 120. Calculate the 2026 terms of trade and explain the change.", "steps": [{"do": "Write the formula: terms of trade = export price index ÷ import price index × 100.", "why": "Always start from the definition so the ratio is the right way up."}, {"do": "Substitute the 2026 values: 112 ÷ 120 × 100.", "why": "Use the 2026 indices, not the percentage rises."}, {"do": "Calculate: 112 ÷ 120 = 0.9333, so the index is 93.3.", "why": "Giving one decimal place is accurate enough for an index."}, {"do": "Compare with the 2024 value of 100 (100 ÷ 100 × 100). The index fell by 6.7 points.", "why": "The 2024 base gives 100, so the comparison shows the direction of change."}, {"do": "Interpret: import prices rose faster than export prices, so a deterioration.", "why": "Marks are for linking the number to its meaning."}], "answer": "The 2026 terms of trade is 93.3, down from 100. Import prices rose by 20% and export prices by only 12%, so the terms of trade deteriorated: each unit of exports now buys fewer imports."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the export price index divided by the import price index, rather than the other way round?", "o": ["It shows how many imports one unit of exports buys, so a rise means an improvement", "Exports are always larger than imports, so they must be the numerator", "Dividing this way converts the answer into a percentage change", "Import prices are more important, so they are placed under the line"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the ratio measures the import purchasing power of exports, so a higher value is better for the country.", "Wrong: the ratio uses price indices, not the value or volume of trade, so size is irrelevant.", "Wrong: the ×100 makes it an index. It is not a percentage change.", "Wrong: the order is about what a unit of exports buys, not about which price matters more."]}, "faded": {"task": "A country's export price index is 130 and its import price index is 104. Find the terms of trade and say what it shows.", "given": ["Formula: export price index ÷ import price index × 100.", "Substitute: 130 ÷ 104 × 100."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["80, a deterioration", "80, an improvement", "125, a deterioration", "125, an improvement"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong: 80 comes from 104 ÷ 130, the inverted ratio, and the meaning would be wrong even then.", "Wrong: 80 is the inverted ratio. An improvement needs a figure above 100 here.", "Wrong: the value 125 is right, but a figure above 100 shows exports buying more imports, an improvement.", "Right: 130 ÷ 104 = 1.25, so the index is 125. It is above 100, so exports buy more imports: an improvement."]}, "trap": "Do not invert the ratio, and do not assume a higher terms of trade always helps the current account. Export volumes and elasticity of demand also matter.", "recap": "Terms of trade = export price index ÷ import price index × 100; a rise means exports buy more imports."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.5", "kind": "idea", "why": "Trading blocs and the WTO appear in short explain questions and in longer evaluation questions, where you must weigh trade creation against trade diversion.", "hook": {"q": "A country joins a free trade area with its neighbours. Which decision does it most likely still make for itself?", "o": ["The tariff it charges on imports from non-members", "The tariff it charges on imports from other members", "Whether members' goods can cross its border", "The currency all members use"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. In a free trade area each member sets its own external tariffs on non-members.", "Wrong. Removing tariffs between members is the whole point of a free trade area.", "Wrong. Free trade area members allow each other's goods across the border without tariffs.", "Wrong. A common currency is monetary union, a much deeper stage than a free trade area."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Types of trading bloc", "b": "In a **free trade area**, members remove tariffs and quotas between themselves but set their own external tariffs. A **customs union** adds a **common external tariff**. A **common market** adds free movement of labour and capital. A monetary union adds a single currency."}, {"h": "Creation and diversion", "b": "**Trade creation**: tariff-free access lets buyers switch from a high-cost domestic producer to a lower-cost member, so efficiency rises. **Trade diversion**: the external tariff makes buyers switch from a lower-cost non-member to a higher-cost member, so efficiency falls."}, {"h": "Costs and benefits", "b": "Benefits: larger markets, **economies of scale**, more competition and possibly more foreign direct investment. Costs: trade diversion, reduced trade with non-members, and some loss of **sovereignty** (for example a common external tariff). Net effect depends on the size of the external tariff."}, {"h": "The WTO and blocs", "b": "The **WTO** promotes freer trade, runs negotiations, monitors policies and settles disputes. Its **non-discrimination** principle conflicts with blocs, which favour members. In March 2026 the 166-member WTO sent reform talks back to Geneva, showing how hard multilateral agreement is."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a customs union might lead to trade diversion.", "steps": [{"do": "Define it: trade diversion is when a country switches purchases from a lower-cost non-member to a higher-cost member.", "why": "A clear definition earns the knowledge mark."}, {"do": "Before the union, suppose the country charged the same tariff on all imports, so the efficient non-member producer was cheapest.", "why": "Sets up the starting point for comparison."}, {"do": "After joining, member goods enter tariff-free, but the common external tariff still applies to non-member goods.", "why": "Shows why relative prices change: the tariff is the cause."}, {"do": "Member goods now cost buyers less than non-member goods, even though the non-member's real cost is lower, so buyers switch.", "why": "Links the price change to the switch in buying."}, {"do": "Resources are used less efficiently, as goods come from a higher-cost source, so welfare may fall.", "why": "Ends with the consequence, completing the chain."}], "answer": "Trade diversion is a switch from a lower-cost non-member to a higher-cost member. The common external tariff keeps non-member goods dear while member goods are tariff-free. Buyers switch to the dearer member, so resources are used less efficiently."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer stress that the common external tariff still applies to non-members?", "o": ["It makes member goods cheaper to buyers than non-member goods despite higher real cost, causing the switch", "It shows that non-members have raised their own tariffs in return", "It proves the country has lost all control of its trade policy", "It shows that internal tariffs have not been removed"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Without the external tariff, non-member goods would stay cheapest and no diversion would occur.", "Wrong. The tariff is the bloc's own external tariff; non-members' retaliation is a different point.", "Wrong. A common external tariff limits some control, but that is not why it matters to diversion.", "Wrong. Internal tariffs are removed; the point is that the external tariff remains."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why joining a free trade area may lead to trade creation.", "given": ["Trade creation is a switch from a higher-cost domestic producer to a lower-cost member producer.", "Suppose Country P protects its steelmakers with a tariff, so imported steel was dearer than P's own."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Tariff-free steel from member Q is now cheaper than P's, so buyers switch to Q and resources are used more efficiently", "P's buyers switch from non-member steel to dearer steel from Q, so efficiency falls", "Q raises its external tariff, so P's steelmakers sell more at home", "P's steel output rises because buyers prefer domestic goods once the tariff ends"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Removing the tariff exposes P's dearer producers to cheaper member steel, so resources move to the more efficient producer.", "Wrong. This describes trade diversion, not creation; here the switch is from a domestic producer.", "Wrong. Q's external tariff does not affect trade between P and Q, and would not create trade.", "Wrong. Removing the tariff exposes domestic firms to competition, so their output is likely to fall."]}, "trap": "Do not assume blocs always raise welfare. Trade diversion can outweigh trade creation, especially where the external tariff is high. Always state which effect you expect and why.", "recap": "A bloc gains if trade creation outweighs trade diversion; a high common external tariff makes diversion more likely."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.6", "kind": "idea", "why": "Explaining who gains and loses from tariffs, quotas and subsidies appears in short-answer and essay questions on trade, and usually needs a diagram described in words.", "hook": {"q": "The government puts a tariff on imported steel. Who is most likely to gain directly?", "o": ["UK consumers of steel products", "UK car makers who buy steel", "UK steel producers and the government", "Foreign steel producers"], "a": 2, "f": ["Consumers face higher prices, so they lose.", "Car makers use steel as an input, so their costs rise and they lose.", "Domestic producers sell more at a higher price, and the government collects tariff revenue.", "Foreign producers sell less in the UK, so they lose."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Why restrict trade", "b": "Governments use **protectionism** to shield domestic firms from foreign competition. Common reasons: protecting **infant industries** until they gain scale, supporting declining industries, saving jobs, stopping **dumping** (selling abroad below cost or fair value), strategic industries, raising revenue and improving the current account."}, {"h": "How a tariff works", "b": "A **tariff** is a tax on imports. It raises the domestic price to world price plus tariff. Domestic output extends, quantity demanded contracts, imports fall, and government gains **tariff revenue** (tariff × remaining imports). Two **deadweight loss** triangles show lost efficiency."}, {"h": "Quotas, subsidies, other barriers", "b": "A **quota** limits import quantity. Supply falls, so the price rises, but importers keep the extra revenue unless licences are sold. A **subsidy** lowers domestic costs, shifting supply right, at taxpayers' expense. **Non-tariff barriers** include regulations, embargoes and administrative delays."}, {"h": "Evaluating protection", "b": "Consumers pay higher prices, inefficient firms survive, and firms using imported inputs face higher costs. Trading partners may **retaliate**. Effects depend on the size of the tariff and **elasticity**. Announced 28 October 2026, Budget 2026 temporarily suspended UK import tariffs on 135 food products to ease household living costs during the Middle East conflict."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a tariff on imported bicycles affects the UK market for bicycles.", "steps": [{"do": "The tariff raises the domestic price from the world price to the world price plus the tariff.", "why": "A tariff is a tax on imports, so importers pass it on."}, {"do": "At the higher price, UK quantity demanded contracts along the demand curve.", "why": "A price rise reduces demand; consumers lose surplus."}, {"do": "UK producers' output extends along the supply curve, as higher prices make extra output profitable.", "why": "Protection helps domestic firms and jobs."}, {"do": "Imports fall, since domestic supply rises and demand falls. The government gains revenue on remaining imports.", "why": "Revenue equals tariff × imports after the tariff."}, {"do": "Overall there is a deadweight loss, because costlier domestic output replaces cheaper imports and some consumption is lost.", "why": "Shows resources are used less efficiently."}], "answer": "A tariff raises the domestic price to world price plus tariff. Quantity demanded contracts, UK output extends and imports fall. The government gains revenue on remaining imports. However, consumers lose and costly domestic production replaces cheaper imports, creating deadweight welfare loss."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does UK producers' output rise after the tariff in the worked example?", "o": ["The supply curve shifts right because the tariff cuts their costs", "The higher domestic price makes extra output profitable, extending supply", "Demand rises because foreign bicycles are banned", "The government pays them the tariff revenue"], "a": 1, "f": ["A tariff does not cut costs; it moves firms along the supply curve, not a shift.", "Correct: the price rise causes an extension along the existing supply curve.", "Imports are taxed, not banned, and demand falls as price rises.", "Tariff revenue goes to government, not to producers; that would be a subsidy."]}, "faded": {"task": "A UK toy shop's wholesaler imports toy trains at a world price of £8. The government imposes a £2 per unit tariff. Explain the effect on UK producers.", "given": ["Step 1: The domestic price rises from £8 to £10 (world price plus tariff)."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Output extends along the supply curve, as £10 makes extra output profitable", "The supply curve shifts right because their costs have fallen", "Output falls because retaliation is certain", "Output is unchanged because imports are still cheaper"], "a": 0, "f": ["Correct: a higher price causes an extension along the supply curve, so output rises.", "The tariff does not cut their costs; a supply shift would be caused by a subsidy.", "Retaliation affects exporters, not this step; the immediate effect is higher domestic output.", "Imports now cost £10 domestically, equal to the new price, so domestic firms are no longer undercut."]}, "trap": "Do not treat a tariff as a subsidy: domestic supply extends along its curve, it does not shift. Also include the losers, consumers and welfare, for evaluation.", "recap": "Protection raises domestic prices, helps protected producers and government, but costs consumers and efficiency, and may provoke retaliation."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.7", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 3 often asks you to explain or evaluate causes and consequences of a current account deficit and policies to correct it; you need the accounts' structure first.", "hook": {"q": "The UK buys more from abroad than it earns from abroad. How can it pay for the difference?", "o": ["By foreigners lending to the UK or buying UK assets", "By printing extra money for overseas sellers", "It cannot; the gap must be zero each year", "By raising the exchange rate until imports stop"], "a": 0, "f": ["Correct. Payment comes through net inflows of finance: borrowing from abroad, foreign purchases of UK assets or using reserves. These appear in the financial account.", "Imports are paid for in foreign currency or by assets, not by creating pounds for overseas sellers to spend.", "A current account gap can be large and persistent. It is offset by the other accounts, not forced to zero.", "A higher exchange rate makes imports cheaper and exports dearer, which tends to widen a deficit."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three accounts", "b": "The **current account** records trade in goods and services, **primary income** (profits, interest, dividends, wages from abroad) and **secondary income** (transfers such as foreign aid). The **capital account** is small: capital transfers. The **financial account** records flows of foreign direct investment, portfolio investment, other investment and reserve assets."}, {"h": "Why it balances", "b": "In accounting terms the balance of payments sums to zero: current + capital + financial + net errors and omissions = 0. A current account **deficit** is matched by a net inflow on the **financial account**. That does not make it harmless: the inflow means borrowing or selling assets."}, {"h": "Causes of imbalances", "b": "Deficits may arise from strong **consumer spending** that pulls in imports, an overvalued exchange rate, low **international competitiveness** (poor productivity, high costs), or weak growth abroad reducing export demand. Surpluses come from the opposite: high competitiveness, an undervalued currency or high saving."}, {"h": "Correcting a deficit", "b": "**Expenditure switching** moves demand to domestic goods: a lower exchange rate or tariffs. **Expenditure reducing** policies cut total demand and imports: higher taxes, lower government spending, higher interest rates. **Supply-side policies** raise long-run competitiveness. Reducing policies risk slower growth and unemployment."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the UK can run a current account deficit without the balance of payments failing to balance.", "steps": [{"do": "State the deficit. In Q2 2026 the ONS reported a UK current account deficit of £19.9 billion, 2.5% of GDP.", "why": "Opening with the figure shows you know what a deficit is and applies the data."}, {"do": "Explain its meaning: payments for imports and income paid abroad exceeded receipts from exports and income earned abroad.", "why": "Defines the deficit in terms of the three current account components."}, {"do": "Say the UK must be financed from abroad, by borrowing, sales of UK assets or reserves.", "why": "Links the deficit to how the money is obtained."}, {"do": "Link to the financial account: in Q2 2026 it recorded a net inflow of £29.7 billion.", "why": "Shows the offsetting inflow with real data, as a net inflow, not a deficit."}, {"do": "Conclude: the accounts sum to zero, allowing for the capital account and net errors and omissions, but a deficit financed by borrowing may be unsustainable.", "why": "Ends by explaining the identity and adds a brief judgement."}], "answer": "The deficit of £19.9 billion in Q2 2026 means the UK paid abroad more than it earned. That gap must be financed by foreign funds, shown as a £29.7 billion net inflow on the financial account. So the accounts sum to zero, although borrowing may be unsustainable."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked answer bring in the financial account when explaining a current account deficit?", "o": ["Because a deficit is paid for by net inflows of finance, which the financial account records", "Because the financial account is part of the current account", "Because the financial account measures exports of services", "Because the financial account is the main cause of the deficit"], "a": 0, "f": ["Correct. The balance of payments sums to zero, so the current account deficit is matched by net financial inflows.", "The financial account is a separate account from the current account. Mixing the two is a common slip.", "Trade in services is in the current account. The financial account records investment and other capital flows.", "The financial account mirrors the deficit rather than causing it; the causes lie in trade and income flows."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a fall in the exchange rate might reduce a current account deficit.", "given": ["Step 1: A lower £ makes UK exports cheaper in foreign currency and imports dearer in pounds."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Export revenue always rises because exports are cheaper, so the deficit closes at once.", "Demand for exports rises and for imports falls; if elasticities are high enough (PEDx + PEDm > 1), net exports rise.", "Aggregate demand falls, so lower incomes cut spending on imports.", "Imports become cheaper for UK consumers, so import spending falls."], "a": 1, "f": ["Revenue depends on elasticity, and effects are often slow (the J-curve). 'Always' and 'at once' are errors.", "Correct. The mechanism is expenditure switching, and the Marshall-Lerner condition decides whether the balance improves.", "That is an expenditure-reducing mechanism. A depreciation switches spending; it does not mainly work by lowering incomes.", "Depreciation makes imports dearer in pounds, not cheaper. This reverses the direction of the exchange rate effect."]}, "trap": "Do not say a deficit means the balance of payments fails to balance. In total it always sums to zero; explain the offsetting financial account flows and whether they are sustainable.", "recap": "Current account deficit is matched by net financial and capital inflows; correct it by expenditure switching, expenditure reducing or supply-side policies, each with costs."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.8", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exchange rate systems and the J-curve appear in short-answer and data-response questions, and support evaluation in essays on policy and the current account.", "hook": {"q": "A country's currency falls in value. Imports now cost more in that currency. In the first few weeks, what do you expect to happen to the quantity of imports bought?", "o": ["It barely changes, because people cannot switch quickly", "It falls sharply at once", "It rises, because imports are in the news", "It falls to zero"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Contracts and habits delay adjustment, which is the idea behind the J-curve.", "Quick falls need elastic demand, and short-run demand is usually inelastic.", "There is no reason for quantity to rise when prices go up.", "Demand rarely drops to zero; some imports are essential."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three exchange rate systems", "b": "A **floating** rate is set by market demand and supply. A **fixed** rate is held at a target by the central bank. A **managed** rate floats but the central bank intervenes, for example to keep it within a band."}, {"h": "Changes in the rate", "b": "Under floating rates a market-driven fall is **depreciation** and a rise is **appreciation**. Under fixed rates a deliberate cut is **devaluation** and a deliberate rise is **revaluation**. The government decides, not the market."}, {"h": "Defending a fixed rate", "b": "To stop the rate falling, the central bank buys its own currency using **foreign reserves** and raises **interest rates** to attract inflows. Reserves are limited, so this cannot last forever."}, {"h": "The J-curve", "b": "After a fall in the rate, the trade balance often worsens first because demand is **inelastic** in the short run. Later, volumes adjust and the balance improves if the **Marshall-Lerner condition** holds: the sum of PED for exports and PED for imports exceeds 1."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a devaluation of the pound, if it were pegged, might worsen the current account before improving it.", "steps": [{"do": "Define devaluation: a deliberate cut in the pegged rate, so each pound buys less foreign currency.", "why": "Shows the correct term and the starting point of the chain."}, {"do": "Import prices in pounds rise; export prices in foreign currency fall.", "why": "Links the rate change to relative prices."}, {"do": "In the short run demand for both is inelastic, so volumes change little.", "why": "Explains why contracts and habits delay a response."}, {"do": "Import spending rises and export earnings barely change, so the deficit widens.", "why": "Completes the downward part of the J-curve."}, {"do": "Over time volumes adjust; if the Marshall-Lerner condition is met, the balance improves.", "why": "Shows the recovery and the condition needed."}], "answer": "Devaluation raises import prices and lowers export prices, but short-run inelastic demand means volumes change little, so the deficit widens first. Later volumes adjust and, if the Marshall-Lerner condition holds, the balance improves."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example say import spending rises in the short run after devaluation?", "o": ["Import prices rise in pounds while quantities hardly fall", "Import quantities rise because they are cheaper", "Exports fall, which raises import spending", "The central bank buys more imports"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Higher price times almost unchanged quantity means higher spending.", "Imports become dearer after devaluation, not cheaper.", "Exports do not directly set import spending; the cause is price and quantity of imports.", "The central bank does not buy imports; households and firms do."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why raising interest rates helps a central bank defend a fixed exchange rate that is under downward pressure.", "given": ["Downward pressure means supply of the currency exceeds demand at the target rate.", "Higher interest rates make UK assets more attractive to foreign savers."], "q": "What is the next step in the chain?", "o": ["Foreigners buy the currency to hold these assets, raising demand and supporting the rate", "Foreigners sell the currency, raising supply and supporting the rate", "Exports become cheaper, raising demand at once", "The central bank sells reserves to cut demand"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Hot money inflows raise demand for the currency, offsetting the downward pressure.", "Selling the currency would push the rate down further, not support it.", "Interest rates work via capital flows first; export prices are not directly changed.", "Selling reserves to cut demand reverses the logic; it needs buying the currency."]}, "trap": "Do not call a deliberate fall in a fixed rate depreciation, and do not assume a weaker currency improves the current account at once. Check the system and the elasticities.", "recap": "Floating rates move with the market; fixed rates are held by intervention; after a fall, the J-curve means the trade balance worsens before it improves."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.9", "kind": "idea", "why": "Competitiveness links to the balance of payments, growth and jobs, and appears in 4.1 explain and evaluate questions on exchange rates and trade.", "hook": {"q": "A firm makes twice as many toys with the same workers on the same pay. What happens to labour cost per toy?", "o": ["It doubles", "It stays the same", "It halves", "It rises slightly"], "a": 2, "f": ["Doubling output spreads the same wage bill over more units, so cost per toy falls.", "Total labour cost is the same, but output changed, so cost per toy cannot stay the same.", "The same wage bill is divided by twice the output, so cost per toy halves.", "More output from the same wage bill cannot raise cost per toy."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Unit labour costs", "b": "**Unit labour cost** = total labour cost ÷ output. It rises when pay grows faster than **productivity**. ONS figures released on 17 November 2026 showed whole-economy unit labour costs rose 5.5% in 2025. Lower unit labour costs help firms cut prices or raise profit."}, {"h": "Relative measures", "b": "**International competitiveness** is judged against rivals. **Relative unit labour costs** compare a country's unit labour costs with those of trading partners. **Relative export prices** compare export prices in a common currency. Both are affected by the **exchange rate**."}, {"h": "What affects competitiveness", "b": "Price competitiveness depends on wages, productivity, the exchange rate and input costs. **Non-price competitiveness** depends on quality, design, reliability and delivery. Investment, R&D, skills and infrastructure raise productivity and quality over time."}, {"h": "Why it matters", "b": "Better competitiveness raises export demand and cuts import demand. This can improve the current account, raise AD, growth and employment. Poor competitiveness can do the reverse. Effects depend on **price elasticity** of demand for exports."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a rise in labour productivity can improve a country's international competitiveness.", "steps": [{"do": "Higher output per worker means the same labour cost is spread over more units.", "why": "This links productivity to unit labour cost, the key measure."}, {"do": "If pay rises by less than productivity, unit labour costs fall.", "why": "Shows the condition: only pay relative to productivity matters."}, {"do": "Lower unit costs let firms cut export prices or raise profit margins.", "why": "Moves from costs to prices, the competitiveness link."}, {"do": "Lower relative export prices raise the quantity of exports demanded abroad.", "why": "Connects price to demand, depending on elasticity."}, {"do": "Higher export sales raise AD and may improve the current account.", "why": "Finishes with the macroeconomic consequence."}], "answer": "Higher productivity lowers unit labour costs if pay rises less. Firms can cut export prices, so relative export prices fall and export demand rises. This improves the current account and raises AD."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example say unit labour costs fall only 'if pay rises by less than productivity'?", "o": ["Because unit labour cost depends on pay relative to output per worker", "Because productivity is measured in pounds, as pay is", "Because higher pay always lowers output", "Because export prices are set by productivity alone"], "a": 0, "f": ["Unit labour cost is pay divided by output per worker, so it falls only if output grows faster.", "Productivity is output per worker or per hour, not a money value.", "Higher pay does not always lower output; the point is the ratio of the two.", "Export prices depend on costs, the exchange rate and margins, not on productivity alone."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how higher investment in R&D can improve a country's international competitiveness.", "given": ["R&D leads to new or better-quality products."], "q": "What is the best next point?", "o": ["Quality improves non-price competitiveness, so export demand rises even if prices do not fall", "Quality raises unit labour costs, so exports become cheaper", "R&D lowers the exchange rate, so exports become cheaper", "Better products reduce export demand because buyers need fewer"], "a": 0, "f": ["Better quality raises demand through non-price factors, which is how R&D helps competitiveness.", "Quality gains do not make exports cheaper, and higher unit labour costs would hurt competitiveness.", "R&D does not directly lower the exchange rate; this confuses non-price and exchange rate factors.", "Better products normally raise demand; the claim contradicts the chain of reasoning."]}, "trap": "Do not say higher wages always harm competitiveness. Compare pay with productivity, and compare countries using relative measures and the exchange rate.", "recap": "Competitiveness depends on relative unit labour costs, relative export prices and non-price factors, with pay judged against productivity."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Explain and evaluate questions on poverty ask you to separate absolute from relative measures and to say why each changes. Both appear in short and extended answers.", "hook": {"q": "Someone says: 'Poverty means not being able to afford what is normal in the society you live in.' Which measure does this describe?", "o": ["Absolute poverty", "Extreme poverty only", "Relative poverty", "Income inequality shown by a Gini coefficient"], "a": 2, "f": ["Absolute poverty uses a fixed standard for basic needs, not what is normal in society.", "Extreme poverty is an absolute threshold, set at a very low income, not a comparison with society.", "Right. Relative poverty compares a household's income with others in the same society.", "The Gini coefficient measures inequality across the whole distribution, not a poverty line."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Absolute poverty", "b": "**Absolute poverty** means income below a fixed threshold needed for basic necessities such as food, clothing and shelter. The line is fixed in real terms. The World Bank estimated 811 million people, 1 in 10, lived on under USD 3.00 a day (2021 purchasing power parity) in 2026."}, {"h": "Relative poverty", "b": "**Relative poverty** means income below 60% of **median** household income. It compares households with the rest of society. It can be measured **before housing costs** or **after housing costs**. In 2024/25, 13.4 million UK residents (20%) were in relative low income after housing costs; 10.9 million (16%) before."}, {"h": "Why absolute poverty changes", "b": "Absolute poverty changes when **real incomes** rise or fall against the fixed line. Economic growth that reaches the poorest, higher employment, and rising real benefits reduce it. Falling real incomes or rising prices of necessities increase it."}, {"h": "Why relative poverty changes", "b": "Relative poverty depends on the **distribution of income**, because the line moves with the median. If the poorest gain less than the median, relative poverty rises even if they are better off. In a recession a falling median can lower the line and cut measured relative poverty."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why relative poverty in an economy may rise even though the real incomes of the poorest households are rising.", "steps": [{"do": "Relative poverty is the share of households with income below 60% of median household income.", "why": "Defining the measure shows the line is tied to the median, not fixed."}, {"do": "Suppose the real incomes of the poorest households rise by 2%.", "why": "This sets up the condition in the question: the poor are better off in real terms."}, {"do": "Suppose median income rises by 5%, so the relative poverty line also rises by 5%.", "why": "The line moves with the median, so a faster rise in the median lifts the line more."}, {"do": "Households near the line gain less than the line rises, so some fall below it and the measure shows more relative poverty.", "why": "This links the gap between gains and the line to the rise in the measure."}], "answer": "Relative poverty is income below 60% of median income. If the poorest gain 2% but the median rises 5%, the line rises faster than their incomes. Households near the line fall below it, so relative poverty rises although they are better off in real terms."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the line rises by 5% when the median rises by 5%?", "o": ["Because the line is 60% of median income, so it moves in proportion to the median", "Because the line is a fixed real amount that is uprated for inflation", "Because the line is set at 60% of the mean, which always rises with the median", "Because absolute poverty lines always move with median income"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. The line is 60% of median income, so a 5% rise in the median raises the line by 5%.", "A fixed real line describes the absolute measure, not the relative one.", "The relative line uses the median, not the mean, and the two need not rise together.", "Absolute lines are fixed in real terms and do not follow the median."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why measured relative poverty might fall in a recession even though no household is better off.", "given": ["Relative poverty is income below 60% of median household income.", "In a recession, falling earnings and rising unemployment reduce median income."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["The 60% line falls too, so households whose incomes are unchanged may now sit above it", "The absolute line falls, so fewer households are below it", "Poor households' real incomes rise, so they leave poverty", "Mean income falls, so inequality must fall and relative poverty with it"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. The line moves with the median, so unchanged incomes can end up above a lower line.", "The absolute line is fixed in real terms and does not fall with the median.", "The question says no household is better off, so poor households' incomes have not risen.", "Mean income is not the benchmark, and a fall in it does not show that inequality has fallen."]}, "trap": "Assuming relative poverty falls whenever incomes rise. Check the median: if the poor's income rises by a smaller percentage than the median, the 60% line outpaces them and relative poverty rises.", "recap": "Absolute poverty uses a fixed real line; relative poverty uses 60% of median income, so it changes with the distribution of income."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.2.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Inequality questions in Papers 1 and 3 ask you to explain, interpret or evaluate measures like the Lorenz curve and Gini, and give causes of inequality.", "hook": {"q": "In an economy, the poorest 50% of households receive exactly 50% of total income, the poorest 20% receive 20%, and so on. What would its Lorenz curve look like?", "o": ["A straight 45° line from the origin", "A curve bowed far below the 45° line", "A horizontal line along the bottom", "A vertical line up the right-hand side"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: every x% of households gets x% of income, which is perfect equality.", "Bowing below the line would mean the poor get less than their share of income.", "A flat line would mean most households get nothing, which is extreme inequality.", "A vertical line is not a possible Lorenz curve shape here; the axes are cumulative shares."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Income and wealth", "b": "**Income** is a flow of earnings over a period, such as wages, rent, interest and benefits. **Wealth** is a stock of assets at a point in time, such as property, shares and savings. Wealth is usually distributed more unequally than income."}, {"h": "The Lorenz curve", "b": "A **Lorenz curve** plots the cumulative % of income (vertical) against the cumulative % of households (horizontal), poorest first. The **line of equality** is the 45° line. The further the curve bows below it, the greater the inequality."}, {"h": "The Gini coefficient", "b": "The **Gini coefficient** is $\\frac{A}{A+B}$, where A is the area between the line of equality and the Lorenz curve, and B is the area beneath the curve. It runs from 0 (perfect equality) to 1 (perfect inequality)."}, {"h": "Causes of inequality", "b": "Causes include differences in **skills and education**, wage differentials from labour market demand and supply, unequal **ownership of assets** that earn returns, discrimination, unemployment, inheritance and **government policy** on tax and benefits."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a Lorenz curve shows the extent of income inequality in an economy.", "steps": [{"do": "A Lorenz curve plots the cumulative % of income against the cumulative % of households, ranked from poorest to richest.", "why": "Defines the axes first, which shows accurate knowledge."}, {"do": "If income were shared equally, the curve would follow the 45° line of equality, since x% of households would receive x% of income.", "why": "Gives the benchmark that the curve is compared against."}, {"do": "In reality the curve bows below the line, because the poorest households get a smaller share of income than their share of households.", "why": "Links the shape of the curve to unequal outcomes."}, {"do": "The bigger the gap between the curve and the line, the greater the inequality, and the higher the Gini coefficient.", "why": "Connects the diagram to the Gini and the conclusion."}], "answer": "The Lorenz curve plots cumulative income against cumulative households, poorest first. Equality is the 45° line. The further the curve bows below it, the more unequal income is, and the larger the area between them, so the higher the Gini."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the 45° line represent perfect equality on a Lorenz diagram?", "o": ["Because at every point, x% of households receive x% of income", "Because the poorest households receive no income on it", "Because the Gini coefficient on that line equals 1", "Because the line shows average income for each household"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: equal shares mean cumulative income share always equals cumulative household share.", "On the line the poorest get a fair share, not zero; zero for the poor would be extreme inequality.", "The Gini on the line is 0, because there is no area between it and the curve.", "The axes show cumulative percentages, not average income levels."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why wealth tends to be distributed more unequally than income.", "given": ["Wealth is a stock of assets, such as property and shares, built up over time.", "Some households inherit assets, while others start with none."], "q": "Which is the best next linked point?", "o": ["Assets earn returns such as rent and dividends, which can be saved, so wealth grows cumulatively", "Wealth is a flow, so it changes more quickly than income each month", "A high Gini for wealth proves that income is equally shared", "Everyone must pay wealth tax, which keeps holdings equal"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: owning assets produces income that can be saved into more assets, widening the gap over time.", "Wrong: wealth is a stock, not a flow. This is the stock/flow slip.", "Wrong: a high Gini means high inequality, and it says nothing to prove equal income.", "Wrong: there is no universal wealth tax in the UK that equalises holdings; this is unsupported."]}, "trap": "Reading a higher Gini as more equal, or treating wealth as a flow. Always say: higher Gini means more unequal, and wealth is a stock of assets.", "recap": "Lorenz curve further from the 45° line means a higher Gini and more inequality; wealth is a stock, income a flow."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.3.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions on measures of development ask you to explain what HDI includes and to evaluate its strengths and limits against GDP and other indicators.", "hook": {"q": "Why might GDP per capita alone be a poor measure of how developed a country is?", "o": ["It ignores health and education, and hides how income is shared", "It is always lower than GNI per capita", "It cannot be compared between countries at all", "It is only measured in poor countries"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. GDP per capita is an average of output. It says nothing about health, education or distribution, which is why broader indicators exist.", "Neither is always lower; the two differ by net income from abroad.", "Countries can be compared, though price differences matter. That is why PPP is used.", "GDP per capita is measured for nearly every country, rich and poor."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What HDI measures", "b": "The **Human Development Index** is a **composite indicator** from 0 to 1. It combines health (life expectancy at birth), education (mean and expected years of schooling) and living standards (GNI per capita, adjusted for PPP). A higher value means higher development."}, {"h": "HDI in real data", "b": "In the UNDP's Human Development Report 2025 (2023 data), Iceland ranked 1st with an HDI of 0.972. Chad ranked 190th with 0.416. This shows a **wide gap** in **human development** that income alone would not fully show."}, {"h": "Strengths of HDI", "b": "HDI is **broader than GDP** because it adds health and education. It is simple, published every year, and allows **comparison** between countries and over time. Using **PPP** adjusts income for differences in the cost of living between countries."}, {"h": "Limits and alternatives", "b": "HDI uses **averages**, so it hides **inequality**. It omits sustainability, political freedom and gender gaps, and schooling years do not show quality. Other indicators help: the **Multidimensional Poverty Index**, the Gini coefficient and the Gender Inequality Index."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a country with a high HDI may still have many people with a low standard of living.", "steps": [{"do": "State that HDI is built from national averages: average life expectancy, average schooling and GNI per capita.", "why": "This identifies the feature of HDI that creates the limitation."}, {"do": "Suppose most national income goes to a small wealthy group. GNI per capita is still high, because it divides total income by population.", "why": "A specific case shows how an average can mislead."}, {"do": "So many households have incomes far below the average, with poorer access to healthcare and schooling than the HDI suggests.", "why": "This links the average to actual living standards."}, {"do": "HDI does not measure income distribution, so it cannot reveal this gap.", "why": "It states precisely what is missing from the index."}, {"do": "Conclude that HDI is best used alongside a measure of inequality such as the Gini coefficient.", "why": "A short judgement shows understanding of how to use indicators."}], "answer": "HDI uses national averages. If income is concentrated, GNI per capita can be high while many households earn far less and have poorer healthcare and education. HDI does not capture distribution, so it hides this. It should be used with a measure such as the Gini coefficient."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer stress that GNI per capita is an average?", "o": ["Averages divide total income by population, so they say nothing about how it is shared", "Averages exclude income earned from abroad", "Averages are adjusted for PPP, which removes inequality", "Averages are calculated using only the richest households"], "a": 0, "f": ["Correct. Total income divided by population gives a mean, so a few very high incomes can lift it while most households earn less.", "Income from abroad is what separates GNI from GDP; it has nothing to do with the averaging problem.", "PPP adjusts for prices, not for distribution. It does not remove inequality.", "The average uses everyone in the population, not only the richest."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain one reason why HDI is a better measure of development than GDP per capita.", "given": ["GDP per capita measures only average output, or income, per person.", "HDI adds life expectancy and years of schooling to income."], "q": "What is the best next step in the explanation?", "o": ["So HDI measures GDP more accurately, because it uses PPP exchange rates.", "So HDI includes environmental sustainability, which GDP per capita ignores.", "So HDI shows whether people live long and educated lives, not only how much they earn.", "So HDI measures inequality, which GDP per capita ignores."], "a": 2, "f": ["HDI is not a more accurate GDP figure; its advantage is the extra dimensions, not the price adjustment.", "HDI has no environmental component. Claiming it does is a common error.", "Correct. This links the added health and education dimensions to the wider meaning of development.", "HDI uses averages and does not capture inequality, so this claim is wrong."]}, "trap": "Do not say HDI measures inequality, the environment or growth. It does not. Name what it omits, then use that point to evaluate it.", "recap": "HDI combines health, education and income into one 0 to 1 average, so it is broader than GDP but ignores inequality and sustainability."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.3.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "This topic appears in short explain questions and in longer essays on why some countries grow slowly. You need each barrier and its effect on growth.", "hook": {"q": "A country earns most of its income from selling one crop. Its price halves. What is the most likely effect?", "o": ["Export earnings and government revenue fall sharply", "Export earnings rise as more is sold", "Nothing changes, since the crop is still grown", "Imports become cheaper for the country"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. When the price falls and demand is inelastic, revenue falls and the economy suffers.", "Selling more would not offset a halving of price when demand is inelastic and supply is fixed in the short run.", "Output is unchanged but income is not; revenue depends on price as well as quantity.", "Falling export revenue does not make imports cheaper; it makes them harder to afford."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Primary product dependence", "b": "Many developing countries rely on a narrow range of **primary products**. Prices are **volatile** because supply (weather) and demand (world income) shift often. Primaries have low **value added**, and the **terms of trade** may worsen over time (Prebisch-Singer). UNCTAD's 2025 report said two-thirds of developing countries are commodity-dependent."}, {"h": "Savings and currency gaps", "b": "The Harrod-Domar model links growth to saving and investment. A **savings gap** is when domestic saving is below the investment needed for a target growth rate. A **foreign currency gap** is when export earnings fall short of the foreign exchange needed for imports such as machinery. Both limit investment."}, {"h": "Debt", "b": "High **debt** means large **debt service** (interest plus repayments). This has an opportunity cost: less government spending on health, education and infrastructure. Weak currencies and high interest rates make it worse. World Bank data showed developing countries' external debt rose 5.5% to a record USD 9.4 trillion in 2025."}, {"h": "Linking the barriers", "b": "The barriers reinforce each other. Volatile commodity revenue reduces saving and foreign exchange, which pushes borrowing up. Debt service then cuts public investment, so **human capital** and **productivity** stay low, and the economy stays dependent on primaries."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how dependence on primary product exports can hold back a developing economy's growth.", "steps": [{"do": "State that the country relies on a narrow range of primary products for most export earnings.", "why": "Defines the barrier and applies it to the context."}, {"do": "Explain that prices are volatile because of weather and world demand shifts, so export revenue and tax receipts swing.", "why": "Gives the first cause with a link to revenue."}, {"do": "Explain that unstable revenue makes planning and long-term investment in infrastructure and education difficult.", "why": "Extends the chain to growth."}, {"do": "Add that primaries have low value added and may suffer worsening terms of trade, so more exports are needed per import.", "why": "Brings in a second, distinct reason."}, {"do": "Conclude that growth is held back as low incomes limit saving and investment.", "why": "Closes the chain back to the question."}], "answer": "Dependence on primaries makes export revenue volatile, so government income and investment are unstable. Low value added and worsening terms of trade mean more exports are needed per import. Low and uncertain incomes limit saving and investment, holding back growth."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example link volatile prices to investment in infrastructure and education?", "o": ["Unstable revenue makes long-term spending hard to plan and fund", "Volatile prices make infrastructure cheaper to build", "Education spending is paid for by import tariffs only", "Volatility raises domestic saving automatically"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Analysis needs a chain: volatile prices, unstable revenue, weaker investment, lower growth.", "No link is given between volatile commodity prices and construction costs; the chain runs through revenue.", "Tariffs are not the key funding source here; the chain depends on revenue instability.", "Uncertainty tends to reduce, not raise, saving and investment."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a high level of external debt can hold back development.", "given": ["Step 1: High external debt means large debt service payments each year, in interest and repayments."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["Explain that debt service has an opportunity cost, reducing government spending on health, education and infrastructure", "State that debt always raises growth because it funds investment", "Say that debt service increases tax revenue", "Say that high debt lowers the price of imports"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. The opportunity cost of debt service is the next link in the chain to lower development.", "Debt can fund investment, but this does not explain how high debt holds development back.", "Debt service is a payment out; it does not raise tax revenue.", "Debt does not lower import prices; it takes foreign currency and government funds."]}, "trap": "Do not say primary products always cause low growth. Explain the dependence on a narrow range, the volatile prices and worsening terms of trade, and always link to growth.", "recap": "Growth is held back by primary dependence, savings and foreign currency gaps, and debt, each reducing investment and development."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.3.3", "kind": "writing", "why": "Evaluate questions on development strategies carry the largest marks in Paper 3, and need you to compare strategies, institutions and contexts, not just list them.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Market-based strategies", "b": "These rely on **free markets** and private incentives. Examples: **trade liberalisation**, encouraging **foreign direct investment (FDI)**, **privatisation**, floating exchange rates, removing subsidies and **microfinance**. They aim for efficiency and competition, but may widen inequality and need strong institutions."}, {"h": "Interventionist strategies", "b": "These use the state to correct **market failure**. Examples: spending on **human capital** (education, health), **infrastructure**, tariffs to protect **infant industries**, managed exchange rates, **buffer stock** schemes, tourism promotion, aid and debt relief. Risks: government failure, corruption and inefficiency."}, {"h": "IMF, World Bank, NGOs", "b": "The **IMF** gives short-term loans to countries with balance of payments problems, often with **conditionality**. The **World Bank** gives long-term loans and grants for projects; in December 2024 it announced a record USD 100 billion IDA21 replenishment for 78 low-income countries. **NGOs** are non-profit and work locally."}, {"h": "Evaluating strategies", "b": "Judge by **context**: strength of institutions, size and stage of the economy, and time frame. Market reforms may hurt the poor short term; intervention may be slow or corrupt. Most countries use a **mix**. Reach a supported judgement, not a list."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate the view that market-based strategies are more effective than interventionist strategies in promoting development in low-income countries. (25 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Market-based strategies, such as privatisation and trade liberalisation, use price signals and competition, while interventionist strategies, such as state spending on education and infrastructure, use government action to correct market failure.", "why": "Defines both strategies accurately with examples: knowledge marks."}, {"do": "For example, a landlocked low-income country that removes tariffs and welcomes FDI may gain technology and jobs in manufacturing, while the government builds roads and schools funded by World Bank loans.", "why": "Applies the terms to a context rather than staying general: application marks."}, {"do": "Liberalisation raises competition, lowering costs and prices, which raises exports and GDP. However, private firms under-provide education, so intervention raises human capital, productivity and long-run aggregate supply.", "why": "Builds chains of reasoning on both sides: analysis marks."}, {"do": "Overall, the answer depends on institutions and time. Where corruption is high, markets may serve elites and state projects may be wasted, so a mix, with market reforms in trade and intervention in human capital, is likely most effective.", "why": "Makes a justified judgement using context and time: evaluation marks."}], "answer": "Market-based strategies use competition and private incentives; interventionist ones correct market failure, for example through education. Liberalisation can raise exports and growth, but human capital needs state funding. Effectiveness depends on institutions and time horizon, so a mix of both is likely best."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example end with 'it depends on institutions and time' instead of just choosing one strategy?", "o": ["Evaluation marks reward judgements supported by criteria such as context and time frame", "Examiners prefer answers that avoid reaching any conclusion", "Institutions only matter for market-based strategies", "The IMF requires countries to use a mix of strategies"], "a": 0, "x": "", "f": ["Right: a judgement is credited when justified by relevant factors like institutions and time, which also shows why strategies succeed or fail.", "A conclusion is needed; evaluation requires a reasoned judgement, not avoidance of one.", "Institutions affect both: corruption can waste state projects as much as it can distort markets.", "The IMF does not require a mix; this invents a rule and does not explain the evaluative reasoning."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate whether aid is the most effective way to promote development in low-income countries. (25 marks)", "given": ["Knowledge: Aid is the transfer of resources, as grants or concessional loans, from donors or institutions such as the World Bank to developing countries.", "Application: For example, aid might fund a rural school-building programme in a low-income country."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to earn analysis marks?", "o": ["Aid is good because it helps countries to develop and many countries receive it.", "Aid is provided by governments and by institutions such as the World Bank.", "Better schools raise human capital and labour productivity, increasing long-run aggregate supply and potential growth.", "In conclusion, aid is the best strategy because it always works."], "a": 2, "f": ["This is assertion: it says aid is good without a chain of reasoning showing how it raises growth.", "This repeats description of who gives aid; it adds no analysis of how aid affects the economy.", "Right: it links aid to human capital, productivity and long-run aggregate supply in a developed chain of reasoning.", "An unsupported, absolute conclusion offered too early earns no analysis or evaluation credit."]}, "trap": "Listing strategies without judging them. Always weigh strategies against context (institutions, time frame, size of economy) and end with a supported judgement.", "recap": "No single strategy always works; evaluate market-based and interventionist approaches by context, time frame and institutions, then justify a judgement."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.4.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Financial markets appear in Paper 2 and 3 short and extended questions, often as context for explaining how saving, borrowing, hedging or share issues affect firms and the economy.", "hook": {"q": "You pay £100 into a savings account. The bank lends part of it to a firm. What is the bank mainly doing?", "o": ["Hedging the firm's currency risk", "Channelling savings to a borrower", "Selling shares in the firm", "Exchanging your pounds for dollars"], "a": 1, "f": ["Hedging means fixing a future price. Lending savings out does not do that.", "Right: it takes savings from one group and lends them to another. This is the saving and borrowing role of financial markets.", "Selling shares is equity finance. A loan is debt, repaid with interest.", "No currency is being exchanged. Money stays in pounds."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Saving and borrowing", "b": "Financial markets bring together **savers** and **borrowers**. Households put money into accounts and ISAs. Banks and other institutions lend it to households (such as mortgages) and firms. Savers earn a return; borrowers get funds to spend or invest now."}, {"h": "Exchange of currencies", "b": "Financial markets also allow **exchange**: firms and households swap one currency for another so they can trade internationally. A **spot** transaction is made at today's exchange rate and settled immediately. This supports trade, tourism and investment across borders."}, {"h": "Forward markets", "b": "In a **forward market**, a buyer and seller agree today a price for a currency or commodity to be delivered at a set future date. This lets firms **hedge** against price or exchange rate changes. Costs become certain, but the firm cannot gain if prices move in its favour."}, {"h": "Equity markets", "b": "The **equity market** trades shares. In the primary market, a firm issues new shares to raise funds. In the secondary market, existing shares are resold between investors. In 2025 the London Stock Exchange hosted over 1,500 issuers and enabled firms to raise over £18.5 billion in capital."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how financial markets could help a UK toy shop that imports toys priced in US dollars and wants to expand.", "steps": [{"do": "The shop could borrow from a bank to fund a new branch, repaying with interest from future sales.", "why": "Links the saving and borrowing role: funds from savers reach a firm that needs finance."}, {"do": "To pay its supplier, the shop buys dollars with pounds in the foreign exchange market.", "why": "Shows the exchange role: currencies are swapped so international trade can happen."}, {"do": "It could agree a forward contract to buy dollars in three months at a rate fixed today.", "why": "Applies the forward market: the pound cost of imports is fixed, which is hedging."}, {"do": "As the firm grows, it could issue shares to raise funds without a fixed repayment, though owners share control.", "why": "Brings in the equity market and a brief consequence of selling shares."}], "answer": "Financial markets let the shop borrow from savers to expand, exchange pounds for dollars to pay suppliers, and use a forward contract to fix its import costs. Later, issuing shares could raise funds without repayment, but ownership would be diluted."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say a forward contract fixes the pound cost of imports?", "o": ["Because the exchange rate is agreed today for a future payment", "Because the bank guarantees the shop's dollar price will fall", "Because the shop buys dollars at the spot rate on the day of payment", "Because forward contracts stop the exchange rate from changing"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the rate is set now for delivery later, so the shop knows its sterling cost in advance.", "A forward contract does not make prices fall. It only gives certainty, and it can turn out worse than the later spot rate.", "That describes a spot transaction. The forward rate is fixed in advance, not set on payment day.", "The market rate still moves. The contract only protects the firm from that movement for the agreed amount."]}, "faded": {"task": "A UK bakery chain fears the price of wheat will rise in six months. Explain how a financial market could help it.", "given": ["Step 1: Identify the risk. A rise in the wheat price would raise the chain's costs and make profit uncertain."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["It agrees a forward contract to buy wheat at a price fixed now for delivery later", "It issues new shares to raise funds from investors", "It deposits its profits in a savings account", "It buys dollars at the spot exchange rate today"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: a forward contract on a commodity fixes the purchase price now, making costs certain. This is hedging.", "Issuing shares raises finance. It does not protect against a future rise in wheat prices.", "Saving earns a return but does not fix the future price of wheat.", "Wheat is not priced in dollars here, and a spot purchase of currency does not hedge a commodity price rise."]}, "trap": "Do not say a firm gains money when its shares are traded between investors. Only a new share issue raises funds for the firm. Also, hedging gives certainty, not guaranteed savings.", "recap": "Financial markets link savers and borrowers, allow exchange, let firms hedge with forward contracts, and let firms raise funds by issuing shares."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.4.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "This topic appears in short-answer and extended questions on why financial markets fail and how regulation responds, often needing precise definitions and application to real cases.", "hook": {"q": "A friend lends you £50 and a stranger lends you £50 with no questions asked. Who knows more about whether you will repay?", "o": ["The lender, because they hold the money", "You, because you know your own plans and habits", "Both equally, because the loan is small", "Neither, because repayment is random"], "a": 1, "f": ["Holding the money gives no knowledge about your repayment; you hold the private information.", "Right. Borrowers know more about their own risk than lenders do. That gap is asymmetric information.", "Both do not know equally; the borrower has more information about repayment.", "Repayment depends on your circumstances, not pure chance, and you know more about them."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Asymmetric information", "b": "**Asymmetric information** exists when one party in a transaction knows more than the other. Borrowers know more about their risk than lenders. Insiders know more than other traders. Markets then misprice risk and resources are **misallocated**."}, {"h": "Moral hazard", "b": "**Moral hazard** is taking greater risks because someone else bears the cost if things go wrong. A bank expecting a **bailout** may lend recklessly. Taxpayers pay when risks fail, so private costs fall below **social costs**."}, {"h": "Speculative bubbles", "b": "A **speculative bubble** occurs when an asset's price rises well above its fundamental value because buyers expect further rises. Herd behaviour feeds it. When it **bursts**, prices fall sharply, wealth and confidence drop, and banks can suffer losses."}, {"h": "Market rigging and response", "b": "**Market rigging** is manipulating prices or benchmarks, such as interest rates, for gain. It undermines trust. Regulators such as the FCA respond with **fines**, prosecutions and removing firms' authorisation. In 2025/26 it imposed over £129 million in fines."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how insider dealing is an example of market failure in the financial sector.", "steps": [{"do": "Define the failure: insider dealing is trading using confidential information not available to the public.", "why": "Defining the concept first earns knowledge marks."}, {"do": "Link to asymmetric information: the insider knows more about the firm's prospects than other traders.", "why": "This names the correct market failure."}, {"do": "Show the effect: other traders buy or sell at prices that do not reflect all information, so they lose out.", "why": "Applies the idea to market outcomes."}, {"do": "Link to misallocation: share prices send wrong signals, so funds flow to the wrong firms and confidence in markets falls.", "why": "Connects to resource misallocation, the core of market failure."}, {"do": "Add real context: on 10 February 2026 the FCA fined two individuals a combined £108,731 for insider dealing in Bidstack Group plc shares.", "why": "Real application shows regulation responds to the failure."}], "answer": "Insider dealing is trading on confidential information, so the insider knows more than other traders: asymmetric information. Prices then fail to reflect all information, other investors lose, and funds are misallocated. This is why the FCA fines offenders."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer link insider dealing to misallocation of resources, not only to unfairness?", "o": ["Because market failure means resources are allocated inefficiently, not just unfairly", "Because unfairness is never relevant in economics", "Because insider dealing raises prices for everyone", "Because misallocation means the government spends too much"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Market failure is about inefficient allocation; distorted prices send wrong signals, which is the point to make.", "Fairness can matter as a goal, but marks for market failure come from inefficiency and misallocation.", "Insider dealing does not raise all prices; it distorts signals for some traders.", "Misallocation is about resources in markets, not about the size of government spending."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the expectation of a government bailout can cause market failure in banking.", "given": ["Define moral hazard: taking more risk because others bear the cost of failure."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["State that the bank expects losses to fall on taxpayers, so it lends more riskily", "State that the bank knows less than its customers about loans", "State that the bank's share price is rising because of expected rises", "State that the bank is manipulating the interest rate benchmark"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. This applies moral hazard: private risk-taking rises because the costs are shifted to taxpayers.", "This describes a different idea, and it reverses the usual position of banks against customers.", "This describes a speculative bubble, not moral hazard.", "This describes market rigging, which is not the issue here."]}, "trap": "Do not mix up asymmetric information (unequal knowledge) with moral hazard (riskier behaviour once costs are shifted). Name the failure, then apply it to the example given.", "recap": "Financial market failure comes from unequal information, risk-taking protected from costs, expectation-driven bubbles and rigging, all of which misallocate resources and justify regulation."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.4.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions ask you to explain the central bank's roles and apply them to events such as rate changes or a bank crisis, often leading to evaluation.", "hook": {"q": "A bank is solvent, but many depositors withdraw cash at once and it cannot pay them. Who is most likely to lend it emergency funds?", "o": ["The Competition and Markets Authority", "The Bank of England", "The Monetary Policy Committee raising Bank Rate", "HM Revenue and Customs"], "a": 1, "f": ["The CMA deals with competition policy, not bank liquidity.", "Right: the central bank is the lender of last resort, lending liquidity to banks to stop a run spreading.", "Raising Bank Rate makes borrowing dearer; it is not emergency lending.", "HMRC collects taxes and does not lend to banks."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Monetary policy", "b": "The Bank of England's **Monetary Policy Committee** sets **Bank Rate** to meet the government's 2% CPI target. It can also use asset purchases. Higher rates reduce consumption and investment; lower rates do the opposite. This is the central bank's macroeconomic role."}, {"h": "Banker to government and banks", "b": "The Bank is **banker to the government**, holding its main account for receipts and payments. It is also **banker to commercial banks**, which hold reserve accounts there to settle payments between them. By end-February 2026, Short-Term Repo drawings had grown from £50.3 billion to £97.0 billion (70 firms)."}, {"h": "Lender of last resort", "b": "If a bank faces a liquidity shortage, the Bank of England acts as **lender of last resort**, lending emergency funds. This maintains confidence and helps prevent a **bank run**. The drawback is **moral hazard**: banks may take more risk if they expect rescue."}, {"h": "Regulation", "b": "**Macroprudential regulation** is the Financial Policy Committee's job: protecting the whole system, for example through the **Countercyclical Capital Buffer**, held at 2% in July 2026. The Prudential Regulation Authority supervises individual firms. Both aim to promote **financial stability**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how the Bank of England acts as lender of last resort and why this matters for the economy.", "steps": [{"do": "Define the role: the Bank lends emergency funds to banks that cannot meet withdrawals.", "why": "A clear definition earns the knowledge mark."}, {"do": "Set the situation: a solvent bank has a liquidity shortage because many depositors withdraw at once.", "why": "Applying the role to a context shows when it is used."}, {"do": "Link to the mechanism: the loan lets the bank pay depositors without selling assets at a loss.", "why": "This is the chain of analysis."}, {"do": "Link to the consequence: confidence is restored, a bank run is avoided and lending to firms continues.", "why": "This explains the macroeconomic importance."}], "answer": "The Bank of England lends emergency funds to banks short of liquidity. A solvent bank hit by mass withdrawals can borrow to pay depositors, avoiding forced asset sales. This restores confidence, prevents a bank run spreading and protects lending to firms."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer describe the bank as solvent rather than insolvent?", "o": ["Last-resort lending targets liquidity shortages, not banks whose assets are worth less than liabilities", "Insolvent banks are always rescued by the MPC raising Bank Rate", "Solvent banks never need to hold reserve accounts", "Only solvent banks have depositors who can withdraw cash"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the role is to solve temporary liquidity problems, not to prop up banks that are fundamentally bankrupt.", "Bank Rate is a monetary policy tool; it is not used to rescue insolvent banks.", "All banks hold reserve accounts; this is unrelated to solvency.", "All banks have depositors; solvency does not decide who can withdraw."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the Bank of England acts as banker to the government and why this matters.", "given": ["Define: the government keeps its main bank account at the Bank of England.", "Set out: tax receipts are paid into the account and government spending is paid out of it."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["The Bank decides how much the government spends each year.", "The Bank sets income tax rates to fill the account.", "The Bank prints money without limit to cover any deficit.", "This lets the government receive and make payments reliably, so public spending is financed smoothly."], "a": 3, "f": ["Spending decisions are made by the government (fiscal policy), not the Bank.", "Tax rates are set by the government; confusing fiscal policy with the banker role.", "Unlimited money creation is not the banker role and would be monetary financing, which risks inflation.", "Right: the next step links the account to its consequence, smooth payments and receipts."]}, "trap": "Do not blur the roles. Bank Rate is monetary policy; capital buffers are regulation; last-resort lending gives emergency liquidity to banks, not firms or households.", "recap": "The Bank of England runs monetary policy, banks the government and banks, lends as last resort, and regulates for financial stability."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.5.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Public expenditure appears in short explain questions and in evaluation of fiscal policy, where crowding out is a standard limitation to discuss.", "hook": {"q": "The government borrows a large sum by selling bonds. What is the most likely effect on the interest rates firms pay for loans?", "o": ["They fall, because the government has more money to lend", "They stay the same, because bonds are separate from loans", "They rise, because more borrowers compete for savers' funds", "They fall, because higher spending lowers inflation"], "a": 2, "f": ["The government is a borrower, not a lender, in this case.", "Bonds and loans draw on the same pool of savings, so they are linked.", "Extra demand for loanable funds tends to push interest rates up. This is the root of crowding out.", "Higher spending tends to raise, not lower, demand and inflation pressure."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three types of spending", "b": "**Capital spending** buys or builds assets, such as roads and hospitals. **Current spending** pays for day-to-day running, such as wages and debt interest. **Transfer payments** move income, such as pensions, with no output in return. The OBR's October 2026 forecast for 2026–27 included £171 billion of capital investment and £353 billion of welfare transfers."}, {"h": "Why spending differs", "b": "Public spending as a share of GDP varies by country. Reasons include the **stage of development**, **demographics** (more elderly means more pensions and healthcare), political **ideology** about the size of the state, and defence needs. Tax-raising ability also limits what a government can fund."}, {"h": "Crowding out", "b": "**Crowding out** is when higher public spending reduces private sector spending. If the government borrows more, demand for loanable funds rises and the **interest rate** rises. Firms then cut private **investment**. This offsets part of the rise in AD."}, {"h": "How strong is it?", "b": "Crowding out is weaker with **spare capacity**, low interest rates, or investment that is insensitive to interest rates. It is stronger near full capacity. Public capital spending, such as transport, may also raise the productivity of private firms, which can offset the loss."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how an increase in government capital spending financed by borrowing might crowd out private sector investment.", "steps": [{"do": "State the action: the government borrows more by selling gilts to fund extra capital spending.", "why": "Starts with the cause: the spending is financed by borrowing, not taxes."}, {"do": "Link to the market: this raises demand for loanable funds, so the interest rate rises.", "why": "Shows the mechanism by which borrowing affects private borrowers."}, {"do": "Link to firms: higher interest rates raise the cost of borrowing, and fewer investment projects are profitable.", "why": "Connects the interest rate to the private investment decision."}, {"do": "Conclude: private investment falls, offsetting part of the rise in AD from the government spending.", "why": "Gives the macro consequence, and says 'part' rather than 'all'."}], "answer": "Borrowing to fund capital spending raises demand for loanable funds, so interest rates rise. Higher borrowing costs make fewer private investment projects profitable, so firms invest less. Private investment falls, which offsets part of the rise in AD from the government spending."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say private investment offsets only 'part' of the rise in AD?", "o": ["Because crowding out always cancels out the government spending exactly", "Because investment is not affected by interest rates at all", "Because the government's spending is never part of AD", "Because the fall in private investment is usually smaller than the government's added spending"], "a": 3, "f": ["Full offset would occur only in extreme cases; it is not the general rule.", "Investment is affected by interest rates; that is the whole mechanism.", "Government spending is a component of AD, so it does raise AD.", "Crowding out is usually partial, so the net effect on AD is typically still positive."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a country with an ageing population might have higher public spending as a share of GDP.", "given": ["Older populations have more people receiving state pensions, which are transfer payments."], "q": "What is the best next linked point?", "o": ["Tax revenue always rises, so total spending falls", "Older people use more healthcare, so current spending on health services rises", "Capital spending falls to zero because no roads are needed", "Transfer payments are not counted as government spending"], "a": 1, "f": ["Revenue does not automatically offset spending; it also ignores the rising spending pressures.", "Healthcare demand rises with age, so current spending rises. This adds a second reason for a higher share of GDP.", "Capital spending does not fall to zero, and this does not link to ageing.", "Transfer payments are government expenditure; they just buy no output directly."]}, "trap": "Do not call every big item 'capital'. Wages and debt interest are current, and benefits are transfers. Crowding out is also partial, not automatic.", "recap": "Spending is capital, current or transfer; it differs across countries, and borrowing to fund it can crowd out private investment via higher interest rates."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.5.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Taxation questions on Paper 2 and 3 ask you to classify taxes, explain incentive effects and evaluate the Laffer curve, usually in 6 to 12 mark answers.", "hook": {"q": "A town charges every adult a flat £100 annual fee for using its recycling centre. Who bears the larger burden relative to income?", "o": ["Higher earners, because they can afford more", "Everyone equally, because the fee is the same", "Lower earners, because £100 is a bigger share of their income", "Nobody, because it is not a tax"], "a": 2, "f": ["Affordability is not the measure; the burden is judged as a share of income.", "The £ amount is equal, but the share of income is not, so the burden is not equal.", "£100 is a larger percentage of a low income than a high one, so the fee takes a falling share as income rises.", "Compulsory payments to government are taxes in effect; the fee still has a distributional impact."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three types of tax", "b": "The **average tax rate** is tax ÷ income × 100. A **progressive** tax takes a rising share as income rises. A **proportional** tax takes a constant share. A **regressive** tax takes a falling share. Always compare percentages, not £ amounts."}, {"h": "Direct and indirect", "b": "**Direct taxes**, such as Income Tax, are paid on income or wealth. **Indirect taxes**, such as VAT, are paid on spending. The OBR's October 2026 forecast projected taxes at 90% of 2026–27 receipts, with VAT at £186 billion. Indirect taxes tend to be **regressive**."}, {"h": "Incentives to work", "b": "The **marginal tax rate** is the rate on the next £ earned. A higher one lowers the reward for work, so the **substitution effect** may reduce hours. The **income effect** may do the opposite. High rates may also encourage avoidance. The net effect is uncertain."}, {"h": "The Laffer curve", "b": "The **Laffer curve** plots revenue against the tax rate. It is an inverted U: revenue is zero at 0% and at 100%. At the **optimum rate** revenue is highest. Above it, cutting the rate can raise revenue. The optimum is hard to identify."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why cutting a very high rate of income tax could increase tax revenue.", "steps": [{"do": "State the condition: this happens only if the rate is above the revenue-maximising rate on the Laffer curve.", "why": "The Laffer curve is the theory, and it sets the condition for the result."}, {"do": "Explain the incentive effect: a lower marginal rate raises the reward for extra work, effort and enterprise.", "why": "This links the tax cut to behaviour."}, {"do": "Add avoidance and evasion: with a lower rate, there is less reason to hide income or move abroad, so more income is declared.", "why": "A second route by which the tax base widens."}, {"do": "Conclude: a larger taxable income at a lower rate can raise revenue.", "why": "Links back to the question: base up outweighs rate down."}], "answer": "If the rate is above the optimum, a cut raises the reward for work and enterprise and reduces avoidance. Taxable income rises. The larger tax base can outweigh the lower rate, so revenue increases, as on the downward-sloping section of the Laffer curve."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer begin by stating the rate must be above the optimum?", "o": ["Because every tax cut raises revenue", "Because the same cut below the optimum would reduce revenue", "Because the Laffer curve applies only to top rates", "Because the optimum is always 50%"], "a": 1, "f": ["Cuts raise revenue only on the downward-sloping section, so this overstates the claim.", "Below the optimum, a cut moves you down the upward-sloping section and revenue falls, so the condition is essential.", "The Laffer curve applies to any tax rate, not just top rates.", "The optimum is uncertain and varies by tax; no fixed figure such as 50% is given."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why VAT is often described as regressive.", "given": ["VAT is a percentage of spending, so the £ paid rises as spending rises."], "q": "Which is the best next step?", "o": ["Higher earners spend a smaller share of income, so VAT takes a smaller share of their income.", "Higher earners spend more, so VAT takes a larger share of their income.", "VAT is an indirect tax, so it is always regressive.", "VAT raises less revenue than Income Tax, so it is regressive."], "a": 0, "f": ["Higher earners save more, so spending is a smaller share of income and VAT takes a falling share. This is the regressive link.", "Higher earners pay more £, but the question is about share of income; this confuses £ with percentage.", "Labelling it indirect does not explain it; you must give the reason about spending patterns.", "Revenue size does not decide whether a tax is regressive; the share of income does."]}, "trap": "Do not judge a tax by £ paid. Calculate the percentage of income taken. A tax can take more £ from the rich yet be regressive.", "recap": "Progressive, proportional and regressive describe how the average tax rate changes with income; the Laffer curve says rates above the optimum can lower revenue."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.5.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 and 3 questions often ask you to explain automatic stabilisers or to distinguish deficit from debt and structural from cyclical deficits, then evaluate fiscal policy.", "hook": {"q": "A small toy shop takes £40,000 in a year and spends £45,000. It already owes a bank £20,000. Which figure matches a government's budget deficit?", "o": ["£20,000, the loan already owed", "£5,000, the shortfall this year, which is borrowed", "£25,000, the old loan plus the shortfall", "£45,000, all spending this year"], "a": 1, "f": ["That is the stock of past borrowing, which matches debt, not the deficit.", "Right: the deficit is the shortfall in one year, borrowed. The total owed is the debt.", "This is what is owed after borrowing. It is a stock, so it matches debt.", "Spending alone is not a deficit. The deficit is spending minus revenue."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Deficit and debt", "b": "A **budget deficit** is government spending minus revenue in one year, so it is a flow. **National debt** is the total owed, a stock built up from past deficits. In October 2026 the ONS reported net borrowing of £17.0 billion and net debt of £3,058.3 billion."}, {"h": "Automatic stabilisers", "b": "**Automatic stabilisers** are tax and welfare rules that change government finances with the cycle, with no new decision. In a downturn, tax receipts fall and **welfare spending** rises, so the deficit widens and demand is cushioned. In a boom the reverse happens."}, {"h": "Cyclical and structural", "b": "The **cyclical deficit** arises because output is below potential. The **structural deficit** would remain at potential output. Actual deficit = structural + cyclical. In October 2026 the OBR's cyclically adjusted measures were 0.7% of GDP below unadjusted ones in 2026–27 and 2027–28."}, {"h": "Why it matters", "b": "Cyclical deficits tend to shrink as growth returns. Structural deficits need **discretionary** action (higher taxes or lower spending) or growth above trend. A deficit that is mostly structural means debt keeps rising even at full capacity."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the budget deficit rises in a recession even if the government changes no tax rates or spending plans.", "steps": [{"do": "State that output falls, unemployment rises and household incomes and company profits fall.", "why": "This starts the chain. The cycle changes the tax base without any policy change."}, {"do": "Say tax receipts fall, as income tax, VAT and corporation tax depend on incomes, spending and profits.", "why": "Taxes are linked to activity, so revenue falls automatically."}, {"do": "Say welfare spending, such as Universal Credit, rises because more people qualify under unchanged rules.", "why": "Spending is demand-led, so no new decision is needed."}, {"do": "Conclude that falling revenue plus rising spending widens the deficit. This is a cyclical deficit, and it cushions the fall in aggregate demand.", "why": "Links to the stabilising role and names the cyclical deficit."}], "answer": "In a recession output and incomes fall, so tax receipts fall. More people claim benefits under unchanged rules, so welfare spending rises. Revenue falls and spending rises with no new decision, so the deficit widens. This cyclical deficit acts as an automatic stabiliser, cushioning the fall in aggregate demand."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer stress that welfare spending rises under unchanged rules?", "o": ["Because it shows the rise is automatic, not a discretionary policy choice", "Because benefit rates must be cut in a recession", "Because unchanged rules mean the deficit is structural", "Because it proves debt will fall as a result"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: stabilisers work through existing rules, so the rise needs no new decision. That is what makes them automatic.", "Nothing in the answer says rates are cut. Spending rises because more people qualify.", "The rise in spending is cyclical, tied to the downturn. Structural means it persists at potential output.", "A wider deficit adds to debt. The step is about the mechanism, not debt falling."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why UK government borrowing tends to fall in a boom, with no change in policy.", "given": ["Point 1: In a boom, output and employment rise, so incomes, spending and profits rise.", "Point 2: Tax receipts rise, as income tax, VAT and corporation tax are linked to activity."], "q": "What is the next point in the chain?", "o": ["The government cuts tax rates to cool demand, so the deficit falls", "Welfare spending rises as more people claim benefits, so the deficit falls", "Debt falls to zero automatically, so there is no deficit left", "Fewer people qualify for benefits, so welfare spending falls, and the deficit narrows"], "a": 3, "f": ["Cutting tax rates is discretionary and would lower revenue. This is not an automatic effect.", "Reversed: in a boom unemployment falls, so fewer people claim benefits and welfare spending falls.", "Debt is a stock and does not vanish. A smaller deficit just slows its growth, or a surplus lowers it.", "Right: lower unemployment cuts welfare spending under unchanged rules. With higher receipts, the deficit narrows."]}, "trap": "Do not say a smaller deficit means falling debt. Debt only falls if the budget is in surplus. Also do not treat the whole recession deficit as a policy failure, as part is cyclical.", "recap": "The deficit is the yearly flow and debt is the stock; automatic stabilisers move the cyclical part of the deficit, while the structural part needs policy."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.5.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Part 4.5.4 appears in 12- and 25-mark essays on policy effectiveness, where you must analyse global limits such as tax avoidance and capital mobility, and evaluate.", "hook": {"q": "One country sharply cuts its corporation tax rate to attract firms. What are other countries most likely to do?", "o": ["Nothing, because their tax rates have no effect on firms' location", "Consider cutting their own rates to avoid losing firms and tax revenue", "Raise their rates, because firms will always stay", "Fix their exchange rates to the first country's currency"], "a": 1, "f": ["Firms can compare tax rates when choosing where to locate or book profit, so rates do matter.", "Right. Governments compete to keep mobile firms, which is called tax competition and can push rates down.", "If firms are mobile, raising rates risks losing them and revenue.", "Exchange rates are unrelated to the tax-cut problem; this mixes up policy tools."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Transfer pricing", "b": "**Transfer pricing** is the pricing of goods, services and assets traded between subsidiaries of the same **multinational**. If prices are set artificially high or low, profit is booked in a **low-tax country**. This is tax avoidance. Governments lose corporation tax revenue."}, {"h": "Tax competition and coordination", "b": "Mobile firms can choose where to locate or book profit, so governments may cut rates: **tax competition**. International **coordination** can reduce this. In 2026 the UK enforces the OECD **Pillar Two** 15% global minimum tax for groups with revenues over €750 million, using Top-up Taxes."}, {"h": "Enforcement evidence", "b": "HMRC reported £3,387 million in UK **transfer pricing yield** in 2024–25, up from £1,786 million in 2023–24. This shows governments can act against avoidance. But it takes **resources and inquiries**, and firms can restructure, so enforcement remains an ongoing limit."}, {"h": "Limits on policymakers", "b": "Free **capital mobility** and **interdependence** reduce national control. A lower interest rate can cause capital outflow, a weaker currency and imported inflation. Foreign rates, trade partners' demand and **political limits** on agreements also constrain fiscal, monetary and supply-side policy."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how transfer pricing by multinationals can reduce a government's tax revenue.", "steps": [{"do": "State the definition: transfer pricing is the pricing of trade between subsidiaries of the same multinational.", "why": "Defining the key term first earns knowledge marks."}, {"do": "A subsidiary in a high-tax country pays an inflated price to a sister firm in a low-tax country.", "why": "This applies the idea: the price need not match a market price."}, {"do": "Costs rise and recorded profit falls in the high-tax country, while profit rises in the low-tax country.", "why": "This is the link: price changes alter where profit is recorded."}, {"do": "Corporation tax is charged on recorded profit, so the high-tax government collects less revenue.", "why": "This is the final consequence: lower tax revenue, so less spending or higher borrowing."}], "answer": "Transfer pricing sets prices inside a multinational. A high-tax subsidiary overpays a low-tax sister firm, so its recorded profit falls and the other's rises. Corporation tax is paid on recorded profit, so the high-tax government loses revenue while the group's total tax bill falls."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example say the subsidiary pays an inflated price to a sister firm?", "o": ["Because the group wants profit recorded where tax rates are lower", "Because a higher price always means higher real output", "Because competitive markets force subsidiaries to pay more", "Because governments require group firms to pay above-market prices"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. The price is chosen, not set by a market, so it can move recorded profit to the lower-tax country.", "A higher price does not mean more real output; the goods are the same.", "Trade inside a group is not set by competitive markets, which is why prices can be manipulated.", "No rule requires above-market prices; the inflated price is the firm's own choice."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a government may find expansionary monetary policy harder in a globalised economy with free capital movement.", "given": ["Step 1: The central bank cuts the interest rate to raise aggregate demand.", "Step 2: UK financial assets now offer lower returns than assets abroad."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["Sterling appreciates, making exports cheaper and boosting net exports", "Funds may flow out, so sterling depreciates and import prices rise, which can cause inflation", "Tax revenue rises automatically because foreign firms must pay more tax", "Other countries are legally required to cut their interest rates as well"], "a": 1, "f": ["Lower returns reduce demand for sterling, so it depreciates, not appreciates; this is a direction slip.", "Right. Capital outflow weakens sterling, raising import prices and cost-push inflation, which limits the policy's effect.", "Tax revenue is not linked to this mechanism; it is not the next step in the chain.", "No law forces other central banks to follow; the constraint comes from market movements."]}, "trap": "Treating global limits as absolute. Policymakers still have choices; evaluate how large the limit is, such as how mobile capital is and whether coordination like Pillar Two works.", "recap": "Transfer pricing shifts recorded profit to low-tax countries, and global capital mobility limits national policy, so coordination helps but is never complete."}
